(function(){window.PhysicaRankPyqGrav=[{y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 22 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pgl",st:"Point of zero field and cavities",q:"A small point mass m is placed at a distance 2R from the centre 'O' of a big uniform solid sphere of mass M and radius R. The gravitational force on 'm' due to M is F\u2081. A spherical part of radius R/3 is removed from the big sphere as shown in the figure and the gravitational force on m due to the remaining part of M is found to be F\u2082. The value of ratio F\u2081 : F\u2082 is",fig:{w:340,h:150,alt:"A big sphere of radius R with centre O; a small spherical cavity of radius R/3 touches the surface on the side facing the point mass m, which lies at distance 2R from O.",d:[["c",110,70,60],["c",150,70,20,"f"],["c",110,70,2.5,"F"],["t",102,66,"O","e"],["t",85,45,"M","m"],["t",150,106,"removed","ms"],["l",110,70,230,70,"d"],["c",230,70,3.5,"F"],["t",230,58,"m","m"],["l",110,70,110,125,"d"],["l",230,70,230,125,"d"],["l",110,120,230,120,"b"],["t",170,114,"2R","m"]]},o:["11 : 10","16 : 9","12 : 9","12 : 11"],c:3,s:`Without the cavity: F\u2081 = GMm/(2R)\xB2 = GMm/(4R\xB2).
The removed sphere has radius R/3, so its mass is M/27. It touches the surface, so its centre is 2R/3 from O and 2R \u2212 2R/3 = 4R/3 from m.
Force due to the removed part = G(M/27)m/(4R/3)\xB2 = GMm/(48R\xB2).
F\u2082 = F\u2081 \u2212 removed part = GMm/(4R\xB2) \u2212 GMm/(48R\xB2) = 11GMm/(48R\xB2).
F\u2081 : F\u2082 = {1;4} : {11;48} = 12 : 11.
Answer: (d) 12 : 11.`},{y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 29 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pgs",st:"Binary stars and multiple bodies",q:"Three equal masses m are kept at vertices (A, B, C) of an equilateral triangle of side a in free space. At t = 0, they are given an initial velocity V\u20D7(A) = V\u2080 AC\u0302, V\u20D7(B) = V\u2080 BA\u0302 and V\u20D7(C) = V\u2080 CB\u0302. Here, AC\u0302, CB\u0302 and BA\u0302 are unit vectors along the edges of the triangle. If the three masses interact gravitationally, then the magnitude of the net angular momentum of the system at the point of collision is",fig:{w:340,h:170,alt:"Equilateral triangle ABC of side a with A at the top, C bottom left and B bottom right; each mass has an arrow along an edge: A towards C, C towards B, B towards A.",d:[["l",170,20,100,141],["l",100,141,240,141],["l",240,141,170,20],["t",170,14,"A","m"],["t",92,152,"C","e"],["t",248,152,"B"],["t",122,70,"a","e"],["t",220,70,"a"],["t",170,158,"a","m"],["l",170,20,150,55,"aB"],["l",100,141,135,141,"aB"],["l",240,141,222,110,"aB"],["t",166,44,"V\u2080","e"],["t",118,132,"V\u2080","m"],["t",236,114,"V\u2080"]]},o:["3amV\u2080","{\u221A3;2} amV\u2080","\xBD amV\u2080","{3;2} amV\u2080"],c:1,s:`No external torque acts, so angular momentum about the centre is the same at t = 0 and at collision.
At t = 0 each velocity is along an edge, so each mass is at perpendicular distance equal to the inradius a/(2\u221A3) from the centre.
The three velocities go round the triangle in the same sense (A\u2192C, C\u2192B, B\u2192A), so the three contributions add.
L = 3 \xD7 m V\u2080 \xD7 {a;2\u221A3} = {\u221A3;2} a m V\u2080.
Answer: (b) {\u221A3;2} amV\u2080.`},{y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 4 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pgl",st:"Force between masses",q:"A metal wire of uniform mass density having length L and mass M is bent to form a semicircular arc and a particle of mass m is placed at the centre of the arc. The gravitational force on the particle by the wire is",o:["{2GMm\u03C0;L\xB2}","{GmM\u03C0\xB2;L\xB2}","{GMm\u03C0;2L\xB2}","0"],c:0,s:`The wire is a semicircle of length L, so its radius is R = L/\u03C0 and its linear density is \u03BB = M/L.
Take an element dm = \u03BBR d\u03B8 at angle \u03B8 from the axis of symmetry. Its force on m is Gm dm/R\xB2.
Components perpendicular to the axis cancel; only the component along the axis, with cos\u03B8, adds.
F = \u222B from \u2212\u03C0/2 to \u03C0/2 of {Gm\u03BB;R} cos\u03B8 d\u03B8 = {2Gm\u03BB;R} = 2Gm\xB7{M;L}\xB7{\u03C0;L}.
F = {2GMm\u03C0;L\xB2}.
Answer: (a).`},{y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 1 Feb \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pgs",st:"Orbital speed and period",q:"A light planet is revolving around a massive star in a circular orbit of radius R with a period of revolution T. If the force of attraction between planet and star is proportional to R^(\u22123/2) then choose the correct option",o:["T\xB2 \u221D R^(7/2)","T\xB2 \u221D R\xB3","T\xB2 \u221D R^(5/2)","T\xB2 \u221D R^(3/2)"],c:2,s:`The attraction provides the centripetal force: mv\xB2/R \u221D R^(\u22123/2), so v\xB2 \u221D R^(\u22121/2).
Period T = 2\u03C0R/v, so T\xB2 = 4\u03C0\xB2R\xB2/v\xB2 \u221D R\xB2 \xB7 R^(1/2) = R^(5/2).
Answer: (c) T\xB2 \u221D R^(5/2).`},{y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 31 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pgs",st:"Binary stars and multiple bodies",q:"Four identical particles of mass m are kept at the four corners of a square. If the gravitational force exerted on one of the masses by the other masses is {2\u221A2+1;32} {Gm\xB2;L\xB2}, the length of the sides of the square is",o:["{L;2}","4L","3L","2L"],c:1,s:`Let the side be s. The two neighbours each pull with Gm\xB2/s\xB2; at right angles, their resultant is \u221A2 Gm\xB2/s\xB2.
The opposite corner is at distance \u221A2 s and pulls with Gm\xB2/(2s\xB2) along the same line as that resultant.
Total force = (\u221A2 + \xBD) Gm\xB2/s\xB2 = {2\u221A2+1;2} {Gm\xB2;s\xB2}.
Equate to {2\u221A2+1;32} {Gm\xB2;L\xB2}: s\xB2 = 16L\xB2, so s = 4L.
Answer: (b) 4L.`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 15 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pgs",st:"Binary stars and multiple bodies",q:"Two identical particles each of mass 'm' go round a circle of radius a under the action of their mutual gravitational attraction. The angular speed of each particle will be",o:["\u221A{Gm;2a\xB3}","\u221A{Gm;a\xB3}","\u221A{Gm;8a\xB3}","\u221A{Gm;4a\xB3}"],c:3,s:`The particles sit at opposite ends of a diameter, so their separation is 2a.
Mutual attraction gives the centripetal force: {Gm\xB2;(2a)\xB2} = m\u03C9\xB2a.
\u03C9\xB2 = {Gm;4a\xB3}, so \u03C9 = \u221A{Gm;4a\xB3}.
Answer: (d).`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 29 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pgs",st:"Binary stars and multiple bodies",q:"Two particles of equal mass 'm' move in a circle of radius 'r' under the action of their mutual gravitational attraction. The speed of each particle will be",o:["\u221A{Gm;r}","\u221A{Gm;4r}","\u221A{4Gm;r}","\u221A{Gm;2r}"],c:1,s:`The two particles are at opposite ends of a diameter, so the distance between them is 2r.
Force on each: {Gm\xB2;(2r)\xB2} = {mv\xB2;r}.
v\xB2 = {Gm;4r}, so v = \u221A{Gm;4r}.
Answer: (b).`},{y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 25 Jul \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pgl",st:"Force between masses",q:"Three identical particles A, B and C of mass 100 kg each are placed in a straight line with AB = BC = 13 m. The gravitational force on a fourth particle P of the same mass is F, when placed at a distance 13 m from the particle B on the perpendicular bisector of the line AC. The value of F will be approximately",o:["21 G","100 G","59 G","42 G"],c:1,s:`Force from B: G(100)(100)/13\xB2 = 10\u2074G/169 \u2248 59.2 G, directed towards B.
A and C are each at distance 13\u221A2 m, so each pulls with 10\u2074G/338 \u2248 29.6 G.
The components sideways cancel; the components towards B add: 2 \xD7 29.6G \xD7 cos45\xB0 \u2248 41.8 G.
F \u2248 59.2G + 41.8G \u2248 101 G \u2248 100 G.
Answer: (b) 100 G.`},{y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 28 Jun \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pgl",st:"Force between masses",q:"Two objects of equal masses placed at certain distance from each other attracts each other with a force of F. If one-third mass of one object is transferred to the other object, then the new force will be",o:["{2;9}F","{16;9}F","{8;9}F","F"],c:2,s:`Initially both masses are m: F = Gm\xB2/r\xB2.
After transfer the masses are m \u2212 m/3 = 2m/3 and m + m/3 = 4m/3. The distance is unchanged.
New force = G(2m/3)(4m/3)/r\xB2 = {8;9} Gm\xB2/r\xB2 = {8;9}F.
Answer: (c) {8;9}F.`},{y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 1 Sep \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pgs",st:"Binary stars and multiple bodies",q:"Four particles each of mass M, move along a circle of radius R under the action of their mutual gravitational attraction as shown in figure. The speed of each particle is",fig:{w:340,h:170,alt:"Four masses M at the ends of two perpendicular diameters of a circle of radius R, forming a square.",d:[["c",170,85,70],["l",170,85,120.5,35.5],["l",170,85,219.5,35.5],["l",170,85,120.5,134.5],["l",170,85,219.5,134.5],["c",120.5,35.5,3.5,"F"],["c",219.5,35.5,3.5,"F"],["c",120.5,134.5,3.5,"F"],["c",219.5,134.5,3.5,"F"],["t",112,32,"M","e"],["t",228,32,"M"],["t",112,150,"M","e"],["t",228,150,"M"],["t",140,62,"R","e"],["t",202,62,"R"],["t",140,120,"R","e"],["t",202,120,"R"],["t",170,80,"90\xB0","ms"]]},o:["\xBD\u221A({GM;R}(2\u221A2+1))","\xBD\u221A{GM;R(2\u221A2+1)}","\xBD\u221A({GM;R}(2\u221A2\u22121))","\u221A{GM;R}"],c:0,s:`The masses form a square inscribed in the circle: diagonal 2R, side \u221A2 R.
Two neighbours pull with GM\xB2/(2R\xB2) each at right angles. Resultant towards the centre: \u221A2 \xB7 GM\xB2/(2R\xB2).
The opposite mass is 2R away and pulls with GM\xB2/(4R\xB2) towards the centre.
Total = {GM\xB2;R\xB2}({\u221A2;2}+{1;4}) = {GM\xB2;4R\xB2}(2\u221A2+1).
Set equal to Mv\xB2/R: v\xB2 = {GM;4R}(2\u221A2+1), so v = \xBD\u221A({GM;R}(2\u221A2+1)).
Answer: (a).`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 20 Jul \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pgs",st:"Binary stars and multiple bodies",q:"Consider a binary star system of star A and star B with masses m\u2090 and m_B revolving in a circular orbit of radii r\u2090 and r_B respectively. If T\u2090 and T_B are the time period of star A and star B, respectively, then",o:["{T\u2090;T_B} = ({r\u2090;r_B})^({3;2})","T\u2090 > T_B (if r\u2090 > r_B)","T\u2090 = T_B","T\u2090 > T_B (if m\u2090 > m_B)"],c:2,s:`The two stars always stay on opposite sides of their common centre of mass, on a line that rotates rigidly.
So both complete one revolution in the same time, whatever their masses or radii.
T\u2090 = T_B.
Answer: (c).`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 27 Jul \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pgs",st:"Binary stars and multiple bodies",q:"Two identical particles of mass 1 kg each go round a circle of radius R, under the action of their mutual gravitational attraction. The angular speed of each particle is",o:["{1;2R}\u221A{1;G}","\u221A{2G;R\xB3}","\xBD\u221A{G;R\xB3}","\u221A{G;2R\xB3}"],c:2,s:`The particles are at opposite ends of a diameter, so their separation is 2R.
Force between them: G(1)(1)/(2R)\xB2 = G/(4R\xB2). This is the centripetal force: 1 \xD7 \u03C9\xB2R.
\u03C9\xB2 = {G;4R\xB3}, so \u03C9 = \xBD\u221A{G;R\xB3}.
Answer: (c).`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 24 Feb \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pgs",st:"Binary stars and multiple bodies",q:"Four identical particles of equal masses 1 kg made to move along the circumference of a circle of radius 1 m under the action of their own mutual gravitational attraction. The speed of each particle will be",o:["\u221A({G;2}(2\u221A2\u22121))","\u221A(G(1+2\u221A2))","{\u221A((1+2\u221A2)G);2}","\u221A({G;2}(1+2\u221A2))"],c:2,s:`The four masses form a square inscribed in the circle (R = 1 m, side \u221A2 m).
Net force towards the centre on one mass: two neighbours give \u221A2\xB7G/(2R\xB2) in total, the opposite one gives G/(4R\xB2).
F = G(\u221A2/2 + 1/4) = {G(2\u221A2+1);4}.
This equals mv\xB2/R = v\xB2 (m = 1 kg, R = 1 m).
v = {\u221A((1+2\u221A2)G);2}.
Answer: (c).`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 24 Feb \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pgs",st:"Binary stars and multiple bodies",q:"Two stars of masses m and 2m at a distance d rotate about their common centre of mass in free space. The period of revolution is",o:["{1;2\u03C0}\u221A{3Gm;d\xB3}","2\u03C0\u221A{d\xB3;3Gm}","2\u03C0\u221A{3Gm;d\xB3}","{1;2\u03C0}\u221A{d\xB3;3Gm}"],c:1,s:`The centre of mass is at distance 2d/3 from m and d/3 from 2m.
For the mass m: gravitational force = centripetal force, G(m)(2m)/d\xB2 = m\u03C9\xB2(2d/3).
\u03C9\xB2 = {3Gm;d\xB3}.
T = {2\u03C0;\u03C9} = 2\u03C0\u221A{d\xB3;3Gm}.
Answer: (b).`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 25 Feb \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pgl",st:"Point of zero field and cavities",q:"A solid sphere of radius R gravitationally attracts a particle placed at 3R from its centre with a force F\u2081. Now a spherical cavity of radius (R/2) is made in the sphere (as shown in figure) and the force becomes F\u2082. The value of F\u2081 : F\u2082 is",fig:{w:340,h:160,alt:"Solid sphere with centre O and a cavity of radius R/2 centred at B, R/2 from O towards the particle m at A; the particle is 2R from the surface of the sphere.",d:[["c",100,80,60],["c",130,80,30,"f"],["c",100,80,2.5,"F"],["c",130,80,2.5,"F"],["t",94,76,"O","e"],["t",130,68,"B","m"],["t",70,45,"M","m"],["l",100,80,280,80,"d"],["c",280,80,3.5,"F"],["t",280,68,"A","m"],["t",292,84,"m"],["l",160,80,160,125,"d"],["l",280,80,280,125,"d"],["l",160,120,280,120,"b"],["t",220,114,"2R","m"]]},o:["41 : 50","25 : 36","36 : 25","50 : 41"],c:3,s:`Without the cavity: F\u2081 = GMm/(3R)\xB2 = GMm/(9R\xB2).
The cavity has radius R/2, so its mass is M/8. Its centre B is R/2 from O, so it is 3R \u2212 R/2 = 5R/2 from the particle.
Force due to the cavity part = G(M/8)m/(5R/2)\xB2 = GMm/(50R\xB2).
F\u2082 = F\u2081 \u2212 that = GMm/R\xB2({1;9} \u2212 {1;50}) = {41;450} GMm/R\xB2.
F\u2081 : F\u2082 = {1;9} : {41;450} = 50 : 41.
Answer: (d) 50 : 41.`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 26 Feb \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pgl",st:"Force between masses",q:"Find the gravitational force of attraction between the ring and sphere as shown in the diagram, where the plane of the ring is perpendicular to the line joining the centres. If \u221A8 R is the distance between the centres of a ring (of mass 'm') and a sphere (mass 'M') where both have equal radius 'R'.",fig:{w:340,h:170,alt:"A ring of mass m seen from the side on the left and a sphere of mass M on the right, both of radius R, with their centres X and Y a distance root 8 R apart on a common line.",d:[["p","M80,35 A25,60 0 0,1 80,155 A25,60 0 0,1 80,35"],["c",250,95,60],["l",80,95,250,95],["l",80,95,80,35,"a"],["l",250,95,250,35,"a"],["t",88,70,"R"],["t",258,70,"R"],["t",80,26,"m","m"],["t",250,26,"M","m"],["t",72,112,"X","e"],["t",258,112,"Y"],["t",165,88,"\u221A8 R","m"]]},o:["{1;3\u221A8} {GMm;R\xB2}","{2\u221A2;3} {GMm;R\xB2}","{\u221A8;27} {GmM;R\xB2}","{\u221A8;9} {GmM;R}"],c:2,s:`A sphere acts like a point mass at its centre. For a ring, the force on a point on its axis at distance x is GMm x/(R\xB2 + x\xB2)^(3/2).
Here x = \u221A8 R, so R\xB2 + x\xB2 = 9R\xB2 and (9R\xB2)^(3/2) = 27R\xB3.
F = GMm(\u221A8 R)/(27R\xB3) = {\u221A8;27} {GMm;R\xB2}.
Answer: (c).`},{y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 2 Sep \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pgs",st:"Orbital speed and period",q:"The mass density of a spherical galaxy varies as {K;r} over a large distance 'r' from its centre. In that region, a small star is in a circular orbit of radius R. Then the period of revolution, T depends on R as",o:["T\xB2 \u221D R","T\xB2 \u221D R\xB3","T\xB2 \u221D {1;R\xB3}","T \u221D R"],c:0,s:`Mass inside radius r: M(r) = \u222B 4\u03C0r\xB2(K/r) dr = 2\u03C0Kr\xB2.
Field at r: g = GM(r)/r\xB2 = 2\u03C0GK, a constant.
Circular orbit: v\xB2/R = g, so v\xB2 = 2\u03C0GK R and v \u221D \u221AR.
T = 2\u03C0R/v \u221D R/\u221AR = \u221AR, so T\xB2 \u221D R.
Answer: (a) T\xB2 \u221D R.`},{y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 3 Sep \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pgl",st:"Field and potential of shells and spheres",q:"The mass density of a planet of radius R varies with the distance r from its centre as \u03C1(r) = \u03C1\u2080(1 \u2212 {r\xB2;R\xB2}). Then the gravitational field is maximum at",o:["r = \u221A{3;4} R","r = R","r = {1;\u221A3} R","r = \u221A{5;9} R"],c:3,s:`Mass inside r: M(r) = \u222B 4\u03C0r\xB2\u03C1\u2080(1 \u2212 r\xB2/R\xB2) dr = 4\u03C0\u03C1\u2080(r\xB3/3 \u2212 r\u2075/(5R\xB2)).
Field: g = GM(r)/r\xB2 = 4\u03C0G\u03C1\u2080(r/3 \u2212 r\xB3/(5R\xB2)).
For maximum, dg/dr = 0: {1;3} \u2212 {3r\xB2;5R\xB2} = 0, so r\xB2 = {5;9}R\xB2.
r = \u221A{5;9} R = (\u221A5/3)R.
Answer: (d).`},{y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 8 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pgl",st:"Field and potential of shells and spheres",q:"Consider two solid spheres of radii R\u2081 = 1 m, R\u2082 = 2 m and masses M\u2081 and M\u2082, respectively. The gravitational field due to sphere \u2460 and \u2461 are shown. The value of {M\u2081;M\u2082} is",fig:{w:340,h:200,alt:"Graph of gravitational field E against radius R. Curve 1 rises linearly to a peak of 2 at R = 1 m and then falls. Curve 2 rises linearly to a peak of 3 at R = 2 m and then falls.",d:[["l",50,160,50,22,"a"],["l",50,160,305,160,"a"],["t",58,22,"E","s"],["t",305,178,"Radius R","es"],["t",44,164,"0","es"],["l",47,130,53,130],["l",47,100,53,100],["l",47,70,53,70],["l",47,40,53,40],["t",43,134,"1","es"],["t",43,104,"2","es"],["t",43,74,"3","es"],["t",43,44,"4","es"],["l",95,157,95,163],["l",140,157,140,163],["l",185,157,185,163],["l",230,157,230,163],["l",275,157,275,163],["t",95,175,"1","ms"],["t",140,175,"2","ms"],["t",185,175,"3","ms"],["t",230,175,"4","ms"],["t",275,175,"5","ms"],["p","M50,160 L95,100 L117.5,133.3 L140,145 L185,153.3 L230,156.3 L275,157.6"],["p","M50,160 L140,70 L162.5,102.4 L185,120 L207.5,130.6 L230,137.5 L275,145.6"],["t",128,124,"\u2460"],["t",170,92,"\u2461"]]},o:["{2;3}","{1;6}","{1;2}","{1;3}"],c:1,s:`Read the peaks (field at the surface): sphere \u2460 has g\u2081 = 2 at R\u2081 = 1 m; sphere \u2461 has g\u2082 = 3 at R\u2082 = 2 m.
Surface field of a sphere: g = GM/R\xB2, so M = gR\xB2/G.
{M\u2081;M\u2082} = {g\u2081R\u2081\xB2;g\u2082R\u2082\xB2} = {2 \xD7 1\xB2;3 \xD7 2\xB2} = {2;12}.
{M\u2081;M\u2082} = {1;6}.
Answer: (b) {1;6}.`},{y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 8 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pgs",st:"Binary stars and multiple bodies",q:"Four identical particles of mass M are located at the corners of a square of side 'a'. What should be their speed if each of them revolves under the influence of others gravitational field in a circular orbit circumscribing the square ?",fig:{w:340,h:180,alt:"A square of side a inscribed in a circle, with a mass M at each corner.",d:[["c",170,90,70],["l",170,20,240,90],["l",240,90,170,160],["l",170,160,100,90],["l",100,90,170,20],["c",170,20,3.5,"F"],["c",240,90,3.5,"F"],["c",170,160,3.5,"F"],["c",100,90,3.5,"F"],["t",203,48,"a"]]},o:["1.35\u221A{GM;a}","1.21\u221A{GM;a}","1.41\u221A{GM;a}","1.16\u221A{GM;a}"],c:3,s:`The orbit radius is half the diagonal: R = a/\u221A2.
Net attraction on one mass towards the centre: two neighbours give \u221A2\xB7GM\xB2/a\xB2, the opposite corner (distance \u221A2 a) gives GM\xB2/(2a\xB2).
F = {GM\xB2;a\xB2}(\u221A2 + \xBD).
Set F = Mv\xB2/R = Mv\xB2\u221A2/a: v\xB2 = {GM;a}\xB7{\u221A2 + 0.5;\u221A2} = {GM;a}(1 + 0.354) = 1.354 {GM;a}.
v = 1.16 \u221A{GM;a}.
Answer: (d).`},{y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 9 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pgl",st:"Field and potential of shells and spheres",q:"A solid sphere of mass 'M' and radius 'a' is surrounded by a uniform concentric spherical shell of thickness 2a and mass 2M. The gravitational field at distance '3a' from the centre will be",o:["{2GM;9a\xB2}","{GM;9a\xB2}","{2GM;3a\xB2}","{GM;3a\xB2}"],c:3,s:`The shell has thickness 2a, so it extends from r = a to r = 3a.
The point at distance 3a lies on the outer surface of the shell, so all the mass is inside: M + 2M = 3M.
Outside or on the surface, a sphere or shell acts as if all its mass is at the centre.
g = G(3M)/(3a)\xB2 = {GM;3a\xB2}.
Answer: (d).`},{y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 9 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pgs",st:"Orbital speed and period",q:"A test particle is moving in a circular orbit in the gravitational field produced by a mass density \u03C1(r) = {K;r\xB2}. Identify the correct relation between the radius R of the particle's orbit and its period T",o:["TR is a constant","T/R\xB2 is a constant","T\xB2/R\xB3 is a constant","T/R is a constant"],c:3,s:`Mass inside radius r: M(r) = \u222B 4\u03C0r\xB2(K/r\xB2) dr = 4\u03C0Kr.
Field: g = GM(r)/r\xB2 = {4\u03C0GK;r}.
Circular orbit: v\xB2/R = {4\u03C0GK;R}, so v\xB2 = 4\u03C0GK. The speed is constant.
T = 2\u03C0R/v, so T is proportional to R, that is T/R is a constant.
Answer: (d).`},{y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 12 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pgl",st:"Force between masses",q:"A straight rod of length L extends from x = a to x = L + a. The gravitational force it exerts on a point mass m at x = 0, if the mass per unit length of the rod is A + Bx\xB2, is given by",o:["Gm[A({1;a} \u2212 {1;a+L}) \u2212 BL]","Gm[A({1;a+L} \u2212 {1;a}) \u2212 BL]","Gm[A({1;a+L} \u2212 {1;a}) + BL]","Gm[A({1;a} \u2212 {1;a+L}) + BL]"],c:3,s:`Take a small piece dx at distance x. Its mass is (A + Bx\xB2)dx.
Its force on m is dF = Gm(A + Bx\xB2)dx/x\xB2 = Gm(A/x\xB2 + B)dx, all in the same direction.
F = Gm \u222B from a to a+L of (A/x\xB2 + B) dx = Gm[A(\u22121/x) + Bx] from a to a+L.
F = Gm[A({1;a} \u2212 {1;a+L}) + BL].
Answer: (d).`},{y:"JEE Advanced 2019",tp:"pgs",st:"Orbital speed and period",q:"Consider a spherical gaseous cloud of mass density \u03C1(r) in free space where r is the radial distance from its center. The gaseous cloud is made of particles of equal mass m moving in circular orbits about the common center with the same kinetic energy K. The force acting on the particles is their mutual gravitational force. If \u03C1(r) is constant in time, the particle number density n(r) = \u03C1(r)/m is (G = universal gravitational constant)",o:["{K;6\u03C0r\xB2m\xB2G}","{K;\u03C0r\xB2m\xB2G}","{3K;\u03C0r\xB2m\xB2G}","{K;2\u03C0r\xB2m\xB2G}"],c:3,s:`A particle at radius r feels the pull of the mass M(r) inside it: {mv\xB2;r} = {GM(r)m;r\xB2}.
Kinetic energy K = \xBDmv\xB2 = {GM(r)m;2r}, so M(r) = {2Kr;Gm}.
Differentiate: dM/dr = 4\u03C0r\xB2\u03C1 = {2K;Gm}.
\u03C1 = {K;2\u03C0r\xB2mG}, so n = \u03C1/m = {K;2\u03C0r\xB2m\xB2G}.
Answer: (d).`},{y:"JEE Main 2018",tp:"pgs",st:"Orbital speed and period",q:"A particle is moving with a uniform speed in a circular orbit of radius R in a central force inversely proportional to the nth power of R. If the period of rotation of the particle is T, then",o:["T \u221D R^(3/2) for any n","T \u221D R^(n/2+1)","T \u221D R^((n+1)/2)","T \u221D R^(n/2)"],c:2,s:`Central force F \u221D R\u207B\u207F provides the centripetal force: mv\xB2/R \u221D R\u207B\u207F.
So v\xB2 \u221D R^(1\u2212n), giving v \u221D R^((1\u2212n)/2).
T = 2\u03C0R/v \u221D R^(1 \u2212 (1\u2212n)/2) = R^((n+1)/2).
Answer: (c).`},{y:"JEE Main 2018 \xB7 Online",tp:"pgl",st:"Force between masses",q:"Take the mean distance of the moon and the sun from the earth to be 0.4 \xD7 10\u2076 km and 150 \xD7 10\u2076 km respectively. Their masses are 8 \xD7 10\xB2\xB2 kg and 2 \xD7 10\xB3\u2070 kg respectively. The radius of the earth is 6400 km. Let \u0394F\u2081 be the difference in the force exerted by the moon at the nearest and farthest points on the earth and \u0394F\u2082 be the difference in the force exerted by the sun at the nearest and farthest points on the earth. Then, the number closest to {\u0394F\u2081;\u0394F\u2082} is",o:["2","0.6","6","10\u207B\xB2"],c:0,s:`Force on a small mass m at distance d\u2212R (near side) and d+R (far side): \u0394F = GMm[1/(d\u2212R)\xB2 \u2212 1/(d+R)\xB2] \u2248 {4GMmR;d\xB3}, since R is much smaller than d.
So \u0394F is proportional to M/d\xB3, and the common factors cancel in the ratio.
{\u0394F\u2081;\u0394F\u2082} = {M_moon;M_sun} \xD7 ({d_sun;d_moon})\xB3 = {8\xD710\xB2\xB2;2\xD710\xB3\u2070} \xD7 ({150;0.4})\xB3.
= 4 \xD7 10\u207B\u2078 \xD7 (375)\xB3 = 4 \xD7 10\u207B\u2078 \xD7 5.27 \xD7 10\u2077 \u2248 2.1.
Answer: (a) 2.`},{y:"JEE Main 2017 \xB7 Online",tp:"pgl",st:"Field and potential of shells and spheres",q:"The mass density of a spherical body is given by \u03C1(r) = {k;r} for r \u2264 R and \u03C1(r) = 0 for r > R, where r is the distance from the centre. The correct graph that describes qualitatively the acceleration, a of a test particle as a function of r is",fig:{w:340,h:232,alt:"Four graphs of acceleration a against distance r with R marked. (a) constant up to R then falling; (b) rising linearly to R then falling; (c) rising in a convex curve to R then falling; (d) falling up to R and continuing to fall more slowly.",d:[["l",30,100,30,30,"a"],["l",30,100,165,100,"a"],["t",4,38,"(a)"],["t",165,113,"r","es"],["t",90,113,"R","ms"],["t",36,34,"a","s"],["p","M30,55 L90,55 L105,71.2 L120,80.0 L140,86.6 L160,90.4"],["l",90,100,90,55,"d"],["l",200,100,200,30,"a"],["l",200,100,335,100,"a"],["t",174,38,"(b)"],["t",335,113,"r","es"],["t",260,113,"R","ms"],["t",206,34,"a","s"],["p","M200,100 L260,55 L275,71.2 L290,80.0 L310,86.6 L330,90.4"],["l",260,100,260,55,"d"],["l",30,210,30,140,"a"],["l",30,210,165,210,"a"],["t",4,148,"(c)"],["t",165,223,"r","es"],["t",90,223,"R","ms"],["t",36,144,"a","s"],["p","M30,210 Q75,204 90,165 L105,181.2 L120,190.0 L140,196.6 L160,200.4"],["l",90,210,90,165,"d"],["l",200,210,200,140,"a"],["l",200,210,335,210,"a"],["t",174,148,"(d)"],["t",335,223,"r","es"],["t",260,223,"R","ms"],["t",206,144,"a","s"],["p","M200,152 Q230,158 260,172 L275,190.8 L290,196.7 L310,201.1 L330,203.6"],["l",260,210,260,172,"d"]]},o:["Graph (a)","Graph (b)","Graph (c)","Graph (d)"],c:0,s:`Inside (r \u2264 R): M(r) = \u222B 4\u03C0r\xB2(k/r) dr = 2\u03C0kr\xB2, so a = GM(r)/r\xB2 = 2\u03C0Gk. This is constant, independent of r.
Outside (r > R): all the mass 2\u03C0kR\xB2 acts at the centre, so a = {2\u03C0GkR\xB2;r\xB2}, which falls as 1/r\xB2.
The value is the same at r = R from both sides, so the graph is flat up to R and then decays smoothly.
Answer: (a).`},{y:"JEE Main 2015 \xB7 Online",tp:"pgs",st:"Orbital speed and period",q:"A very long (length L) cylindrical galaxy is made of uniformly distributed mass and has radius R (R << L). A star outside the galaxy is orbiting the galaxy in a plane perpendicular to the galaxy and passing through its centre. If the time period of star is T and its distance from the galaxy's axis is r, then",o:["T\xB2 \u221D r\xB3","T \u221D r\xB2","T \u221D r","T \u221D \u221Ar"],c:2,s:`For a very long uniform cylinder with mass \u03BB per unit length, the field outside at distance r is g = {2G\u03BB;r}, so g \u221D 1/r.
Circular orbit: {v\xB2;r} = {2G\u03BB;r}, so v\xB2 = 2G\u03BB. The speed is constant.
T = {2\u03C0r;v}, so T \u221D r.
Answer: (c) T \u221D r.`},{y:"JEE Main 2014 \xB7 Online",tp:"pgl",st:"Point of zero field and cavities",q:"From the sphere of mass M and radius R, a smaller sphere of radius {R;2} is carved out such that the cavity made in the original sphere is between its centre and the periphery. (See figure). For the configuration in the figure where the distance between the centre of the original sphere and the removed sphere is 3R, the gravitational force between the two spheres is",fig:{w:340,h:160,alt:"A sphere of radius R with a cavity of radius R/2 whose centre is R/2 from the centre O; the removed sphere of radius R/2 lies on the same line with its centre 3R from O.",d:[["c",90,70,60],["c",120,70,30,"f"],["c",90,70,2.5,"F"],["c",120,70,2.5,"F"],["t",82,66,"O","e"],["t",60,38,"M","m"],["c",270,70,30],["c",270,70,2.5,"F"],["l",40,70,320,70,"d"],["l",90,70,90,125,"d"],["l",270,70,270,125,"d"],["l",90,120,270,120,"b"],["t",180,114,"3R","m"]]},o:["{41GM\xB2;3600R\xB2}","{41GM\xB2;450R\xB2}","{59GM\xB2;450R\xB2}","{GM\xB2;225R\xB2}"],c:0,s:`Mass of the removed sphere = M \xD7 (1/2)\xB3 = M/8. The remaining body is the big sphere minus a cavity of mass M/8.
Force on the removed sphere (mass M/8) due to the remaining part = (full sphere) \u2212 (cavity part).
Full sphere M, at distance 3R: G M (M/8)/(3R)\xB2 = {GM\xB2;72R\xB2}.
Cavity mass M/8 at its centre, which is R/2 from O, so its distance is 3R \u2212 R/2 = 5R/2: G(M/8)(M/8)/(5R/2)\xB2 = {GM\xB2;400R\xB2}.
F = GM\xB2({1;72} \u2212 {1;400}) = GM\xB2 \xB7 {328;28800} = {41GM\xB2;3600R\xB2}.
Answer: (a).`},{y:"JEE Advanced 2014",tp:"pgg",st:"g with height and depth",q:"A planet of radius R = {1;10} \xD7 (radius of Earth) has the same mass density as Earth. Scientists dig a well of depth {R;5} on it and lower a wire of the same length and of linear mass density 10\u207B\xB3 kg m\u207B\xB9 into it. If the wire is not touching anywhere, the force applied at the top of the wire by a person holding it in place is (take the radius of Earth = 6 \xD7 10\u2076 m and the acceleration due to gravity on Earth is 10 m s\u207B\xB2)",o:["96 N","108 N","120 N","150 N"],c:1,s:`For equal density, g at the surface is proportional to the radius, so g on the planet = 10/10 = 1 m/s\xB2. Its radius is 6 \xD7 10\u2075 m.
Inside, g(r) = g_s \xB7 r/R, with g_s = 1 m/s\xB2. The wire runs from r = 0.8R to r = R.
The person holds the whole weight: F = \u222B \u03BB g(r) dr = \u03BB {g_s;R} \u222B from 0.8R to R of r dr = \u03BB g_s R \xB7 {1 \u2212 0.64;2}.
F = 10\u207B\xB3 \xD7 1 \xD7 6 \xD7 10\u2075 \xD7 0.18 = 108 N.
Answer: (b) 108 N.`},{y:"JEE Main 2013 \xB7 Online",tp:"pgl",st:"Point of zero field and cavities",q:"The gravitational field, due to the 'left over part' of a uniform sphere (from which a part as shown, has been 'removed out'), at a very far off point, P, located as shown, would be (nearly)",fig:{w:340,h:190,alt:"A uniform sphere of mass M and radius R with a smaller hatched sphere of radius R/2 removed from its left side, touching the surface. A distant point P lies to the right on the line through the centre, at distance x from the centre.",d:[["c",150,90,70],["c",115,90,35,"f"],["c",150,90,2.5,"F"],["l",150,90,310,90,"d"],["c",310,90,3.5,"F"],["t",316,86,"P"],["t",30,30,"Removed part","s"],["l",70,34,105,60,"a"],["t",300,40,"Mass of complete","es"],["t",300,53,"sphere = M","es"],["l",80,125,150,125,"b"],["t",115,140,"R","ms"],["l",150,90,150,176,"d"],["l",310,90,310,176,"d"],["l",150,172,310,172,"b"],["t",230,166,"x","ms"]]},o:["{5;6} {GM;x\xB2}","{8;9} {GM;x\xB2}","{7;8} {GM;x\xB2}","{6;7} {GM;x\xB2}"],c:2,s:`The figure shows the removed part has diameter R, so its radius is R/2 (half the sphere's radius). With the same density its mass is M/8.
Field of the left-over part = field of the full sphere minus field of the removed sphere.
For a far-off point P (x much larger than R), both can be treated as point masses at distance \u2248 x.
g \u2248 {GM;x\xB2} \u2212 {G(M/8);x\xB2} = {7GM;8x\xB2}.
Answer: (c).`},{y:"AIEEE 2012 \xB7 Online",tp:"pgs",st:"Binary stars and multiple bodies",q:"Two point masses of mass m\u2081 = fM and m\u2082 = (1 \u2212 f)M (f < 1) are in outer space (far from gravitational influence of other objects) at a distance R from each other. They move in circular orbits about their centre of mass with angular velocities \u03C9\u2081 for m\u2081 and \u03C9\u2082 for m\u2082. In that case",o:["(1 \u2212 f)\u03C9\u2081 = f\u03C9\u2082","\u03C9\u2081 = \u03C9\u2082 and independent of f","f\u03C9\u2081 = (1 \u2212 f)\u03C9\u2082","\u03C9\u2081 = \u03C9\u2082 and depend on f"],c:1,s:`The two masses always lie on opposite sides of their centre of mass on a straight line, so they turn together: \u03C9\u2081 = \u03C9\u2082 = \u03C9.
For m\u2081 at distance r\u2081 from the centre of mass: {G m\u2081m\u2082;R\xB2} = m\u2081\u03C9\xB2r\u2081, with r\u2081 = {m\u2082R;m\u2081+m\u2082}.
This gives \u03C9\xB2 = {G(m\u2081+m\u2082);R\xB3} = {GM;R\xB3}, because m\u2081 + m\u2082 = fM + (1\u2212f)M = M.
So \u03C9 does not depend on f.
Answer: (b).`},{y:"IIT-JEE 2008",tp:"pgs",st:"Orbital speed and period",q:"A spherically symmetric gravitational system of particles has a mass density \u03C1 = \u03C1\u2080 for r \u2264 R and \u03C1 = 0 for r > R, where \u03C1\u2080 is a constant. A test mass can undergo circular motion under the influence of the gravitational field of particles. Its speed V as a function of distance r (0 < r < \u221E) from the centre of the system is represented by",fig:{w:340,h:232,alt:"Four graphs of speed V against distance r with R marked. (a) rises linearly to R then stays constant; (b) falls steeply like a hyperbola; (c) rises linearly to R then falls slowly; (d) constant up to R then drops and falls off.",d:[["l",30,100,30,30,"a"],["l",30,100,165,100,"a"],["t",4,38,"(a)"],["t",165,113,"r","es"],["t",90,113,"R","ms"],["t",36,34,"V","s"],["p","M30,100 L90,55 L160,55"],["l",90,100,90,55,"d"],["l",200,100,200,30,"a"],["l",200,100,335,100,"a"],["t",174,38,"(b)"],["t",335,113,"r","es"],["t",260,113,"R","ms"],["t",206,34,"V","s"],["p","M208,38 Q210,92 330,95"],["l",260,100,260,92,"d"],["l",30,210,30,140,"a"],["l",30,210,165,210,"a"],["t",4,148,"(c)"],["t",165,223,"r","es"],["t",90,223,"R","ms"],["t",36,144,"V","s"],["p","M30,210 L90,165 L105,169.8 L120,173.3 L140,176.8 L160,179.4"],["l",90,210,90,165,"d"],["l",200,210,200,140,"a"],["l",200,210,335,210,"a"],["t",174,148,"(d)"],["t",335,223,"r","es"],["t",260,223,"R","ms"],["t",206,144,"V","s"],["p","M200,165 L260,165 Q268,198 330,204"],["l",260,210,260,165,"d"]]},o:["Graph (a)","Graph (b)","Graph (c)","Graph (d)"],c:2,s:`Inside (r \u2264 R): g = {4\u03C0G\u03C1\u2080r;3}, so g \u221D r. Circular motion needs v\xB2 = gr \u221D r\xB2, so v \u221D r. The graph is a straight line through the origin.
Outside (r > R): g = {GM;r\xB2}, so v\xB2 = {GM;r} and v \u221D 1/\u221Ar. The speed falls slowly.
The speed is continuous at r = R, where it is maximum.
So the graph rises linearly up to R and then decreases as 1/\u221Ar.
Answer: (c).`},{y:"AIEEE 2004",tp:"pgs",st:"Orbital speed and period",q:"Suppose the gravitational force varies inversely as the nth power of distance. Then the time period of a planet in circular orbit of radius R around the sun will be proportional to",o:["R^((n+1)/2)","R^((n\u22121)/2)","R\u207F","R^((n\u22122)/2)"],c:0,s:`Gravitational force F \u221D 1/R\u207F gives the centripetal force: mv\xB2/R \u221D R\u207B\u207F.
So v\xB2 \u221D R^(1\u2212n) and v \u221D R^((1\u2212n)/2).
T = 2\u03C0R/v \u221D R^(1 \u2212 (1\u2212n)/2) = R^((n+1)/2).
Answer: (a).`},{y:"AIEEE 2003",tp:"pgs",st:"Binary stars and multiple bodies",q:"Two spherical bodies of mass M and 5M and radii R and 2R respectively are released in free space with initial separation between their centres equal to 12R. If they attract each other due to gravitational force only, then the distance covered by the smaller body just before collision is",o:["2.5R","4.5R","7.5R","1.5R"],c:2,s:`Only internal forces act, so the centre of mass stays fixed.
Initially it lies at 12R \xD7 {5M;6M} = 10R from M and 2R from 5M.
Just before collision the centres are R + 2R = 3R apart, so M is 3R \xD7 {5;6} = 2.5R from the centre of mass.
Distance covered by the smaller body = 10R \u2212 2.5R = 7.5R.
Answer: (c) 7.5R.`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1994",tp:"pgl",st:"Field and potential of shells and spheres",q:"The magnitudes of the gravitational field at distances r\u2081 and r\u2082 from the centre of a uniform sphere of radius R and mass M are F\u2081 and F\u2082 respectively. Then",o:["{F\u2081;F\u2082} = {r\u2081;r\u2082} if r\u2081 < R and r\u2082 < R","{F\u2081;F\u2082} = {r\u2082\xB2;r\u2081\xB2} if r\u2081 > R and r\u2082 > R","{F\u2081;F\u2082} = {r\u2081;r\u2082} if r\u2081 > R and r\u2082 > R","{F\u2081;F\u2082} = {r\u2081\xB2;r\u2082\xB2} if r\u2081 < R and r\u2082 < R"],c:[0,1],s:`Inside a uniform sphere, g = {GMr;R\xB3}, so g is proportional to r: F\u2081/F\u2082 = r\u2081/r\u2082 when r\u2081, r\u2082 < R. Option (a) is correct; (d) is wrong.
Outside, g = {GM;r\xB2}, so g is proportional to 1/r\xB2: F\u2081/F\u2082 = r\u2082\xB2/r\u2081\xB2 when r\u2081, r\u2082 > R. Option (b) is correct; (c) is wrong.
Answer: (a) and (b).`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1989",tp:"pgs",st:"Orbital speed and period",q:"Imagine a light planet revolving around a very massive star in a circular orbit of radius R with a period of revolution T. If the gravitational force of attraction between the planet and the star is proportional to R^(\u22125/2),",o:["T\xB2 is proportional to R\xB3","T\xB2 is proportional to R^(7/2)","T\xB2 is proportional to R^(3/2)","T\xB2 is proportional to R^(7/3)"],c:1,s:`Centripetal force: mv\xB2/R \u221D R^(\u22125/2), so v\xB2 \u221D R^(\u22123/2).
T\xB2 = {4\u03C0\xB2R\xB2;v\xB2} \u221D R\xB2 \xD7 R^(3/2) = R^(7/2).
Answer: (b) T\xB2 \u221D R^(7/2).`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 16 Mar \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pge",st:"Gravitational potential energy",q:"If one wants to remove all the mass of the earth to infinity in order to break it up completely. The amount of energy that needs to be supplied will be {x;5} {GM\xB2;R} where x is ____ (Round off to the nearest integer) (M is the mass of Earth, R is the radius of Earth, G is the gravitational constant)",n:3,s:`The energy needed equals the gravitational binding energy of the Earth, treated as a uniform sphere.
Build up the sphere in thin shells: dU = \u2212G m(r) dm / r, with m(r) = {4;3}\u03C0\u03C1r\xB3 and dm = 4\u03C0\u03C1r\xB2dr.
U = \u2212G \xB7 {16\u03C0\xB2\u03C1\xB2;3} \u222B from 0 to R of r\u2074 dr = \u2212{3GM\xB2;5R}, using M = {4;3}\u03C0\u03C1R\xB3.
Energy to be supplied = {3GM\xB2;5R} = {x;5} {GM\xB2;R}, so x = 3.
Answer: 3.`},{y:"JEE Advanced 2015",tp:"pgs",st:"Binary stars and multiple bodies",q:"A large spherical mass M is fixed at one position and two identical point masses m are kept on a line passing through the centre of M (see figure). The point masses are connected by a rigid massless rod of length l and this assembly is free to move along the line connecting them. All three masses interact only through their mutual gravitational interaction. When the point mass nearer to M is at a distance r = 3l from M, the tension in the rod is zero for m = k({M;288}). The value of k is",fig:{w:340,h:130,alt:"A large sphere of mass M on the left; two point masses m on a line through its centre, joined by a rod of length l, with the nearer one at distance r from the centre of M.",d:[["c",80,60,50],["c",80,60,2.5,"F"],["t",80,50,"M","m"],["l",30,60,320,60,"d"],["c",170,60,3.5,"F"],["c",200,60,3.5,"F"],["t",170,50,"m","m"],["t",200,50,"m","m"],["l",170,60,200,60,"B"],["l",80,60,80,95,"d"],["l",170,60,170,95,"d"],["l",200,60,200,95,"d"],["l",80,92,170,92,"b"],["t",125,86,"r","m"],["l",170,92,200,92,"b"],["t",185,108,"l","m"]]},n:7,s:`Tension is zero when both masses have the same acceleration, as if the rod were absent.
Near mass (distance 3l): pulled towards M with GM/(9l\xB2) and away from M by the other mass with Gm/l\xB2. Acceleration towards M = {GM;9l\xB2} \u2212 {Gm;l\xB2}.
Far mass (distance 4l): pulled towards M with GM/(16l\xB2) and towards M by the near mass with Gm/l\xB2. Acceleration = {GM;16l\xB2} + {Gm;l\xB2}.
Equal accelerations: {M;9} \u2212 m = {M;16} + m, so 2m = M({1;9} \u2212 {1;16}) = {7M;144}.
m = 7 \xB7 {M;288}, so k = 7.
Answer: 7.`},{y:"AIEEE 2008",tp:"pgl",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:`Statement-1 : For a mass M kept at the centre of a cube of side a, the flux of gravitational field passing through its sides is 4\u03C0GM.
Statement-2 : If the direction of a field due to a point source is radial and its dependence on the distance r from the source is given as 1/r\xB2, its flux through a closed surface depends only on the strength of the source enclosed by the surface and not on the size or shape of the surface.`,o:["Statement-1 is true, statement-2 is false.","Statement-1 is false, statement-2 is true.","Statement-1 is true, statement-2 is true; statement-2 is a correct explanation for statement-1.","Statement-1 is true, statement-2 is true; statement-2 is not a correct explanation for statement-1."],c:2,s:`Gauss's law for gravity: the flux through any closed surface = 4\u03C0G \xD7 (mass enclosed).
The cube is a closed surface enclosing M, so the total flux through its sides is 4\u03C0GM. Statement-1 is true.
Statement-2 is the same idea: for a 1/r\xB2 field the flux depends only on the enclosed source, not on the size or shape of the surface. It is true.
Statement-2 is exactly the reason for statement-1, so the answer is (c).`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1988",tp:"pgs",st:"Binary stars and multiple bodies",q:"Three particles, each of mass m, are situated at the vertices of an equilateral triangle of side length a. The only forces acting on the particles are their mutual gravitational forces. It is desired that each particle moves in a circle while maintaining the original mutual separation a. Find the initial velocity that should be given to each particle and also the time period of the circular motion.",a:"v = \u221A(Gm/a), T = 2\u03C0\u221A(a\xB3/(3Gm))",s:`The particles move on a circle about the centroid. Radius r = a/\u221A3.
Force on one particle from each of the other two = Gm\xB2/a\xB2. The two forces are 60\xB0 apart, so the resultant points to the centroid:
F = 2 \xD7 (Gm\xB2/a\xB2) \xD7 cos30\xB0 = \u221A3 Gm\xB2/a\xB2.
This provides the centripetal force: mv\xB2/r = \u221A3 Gm\xB2/a\xB2, so v\xB2 = \u221A3 Gm r/a\xB2 = \u221A3 Gm/a\xB2 \xD7 a/\u221A3 = Gm/a.
v = \u221A(Gm/a).
T = 2\u03C0r/v = 2\u03C0 (a/\u221A3) \u221A(a/(Gm)) = 2\u03C0\u221A(a\xB3/(3Gm)).`},{y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 4 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pgg",st:"g with height and depth",q:`A 90 kg body placed at 2R distance from surface of earth experiences gravitational pull of
(R = Radius of earth, g = 10 m s\u207B\xB2)`,o:["225 N","100 N","300 N","120 N"],c:1,s:`Weight at the surface = 90 \xD7 10 = 900 N.
Distance from the centre of the earth = R + 2R = 3R.
Force \u221D 1/distance\xB2, so F = 900 \xD7 (R/3R)\xB2 = 900/9 = 100 N.`},{y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 5 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pgg",st:"g with height and depth",q:"A simple pendulum doing small oscillations at a place R height above earth surface has time period of T\u2081 = 4 s. T\u2082 would be it's time period if it is brought to a point which is at a height 2R from earth surface. Choose the correct relation (R = radius of earth).",o:["2T\u2081 = T\u2082","3T\u2081 = 2T\u2082","2T\u2081 = 3T\u2082","T\u2081 = T\u2082"],c:1,s:`T = 2\u03C0\u221A(l/g\u2032), and g\u2032 = GM/(R+h)\xB2, so T \u221D (R+h).
At height R: distance from centre = 2R, so T\u2081 \u221D 2R.
At height 2R: distance = 3R, so T\u2082 \u221D 3R.
T\u2082/T\u2081 = 3/2, so 2T\u2082 = 3T\u2081 (T\u2082 = 6 s). This is option (b).`},{y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 6 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pgg",st:"g with height and depth",q:"Assuming the earth to be a sphere of uniform mass density, a body weighed 300 N on the surface of earth. How much it would weigh at R/4 depth under surface of earth?",o:["75 N","225 N","375 N","300 N"],c:1,s:`Inside a uniform earth, g\u2032 = g(1 \u2212 d/R).
d = R/4, so g\u2032 = g(1 \u2212 1/4) = 3g/4.
Weight = 300 \xD7 3/4 = 225 N.`},{y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 27 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pgg",st:"g on planets",q:"The acceleration due to gravity on the surface of earth is g. If the diameter of earth reduces to half of its original value and mass remains constant, then acceleration due to gravity on the surface of earth would be",o:["4g","2g","g/2","g/4"],c:0,s:`g = GM/R\xB2. M is constant.
If the radius becomes R/2, g\u2032 = GM/(R/2)\xB2 = 4GM/R\xB2 = 4g.`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 6 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pgg",st:"g on planets",q:"A planet has double the mass of the earth. Its average density is equal to that of the earth. An object weighing W on earth will weigh on that planet",o:["2 W","2^(2/3) W","2^(1/3) W","W"],c:2,s:`Same density, mass doubles, so volume doubles: R\u2032\xB3 = 2R\xB3, R\u2032 = 2^(1/3) R.
g = GM/R\xB2, so g\u2032/g = (2M/M) \xD7 (R/R\u2032)\xB2 = 2 \xD7 2^(\u22122/3) = 2^(1/3).
Weight on the planet = 2^(1/3) W.`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 6 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pgg",st:"g with height and depth",q:"The weight of a body on the surface of the earth is 100 N. The gravitational force on it when taken at a height, from the surface of earth, equal to one-fourth the radius of the earth is",o:["50 N","100 N","25 N","64 N"],c:3,s:`Height h = R/4, so the distance from the centre is R + R/4 = 5R/4.
Force \u221D 1/r\xB2, so F = 100 \xD7 (R/(5R/4))\xB2 = 100 \xD7 16/25 = 64 N.`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 8 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pgg",st:"g with height and depth",q:"The weight of a body on the earth is 400 N. Then weight of the body when taken to a depth half of the radius of the earth will be",o:["100 N","200 N","300 N","zero"],c:1,s:`Below the surface, g\u2032 = g(1 \u2212 d/R).
d = R/2, so g\u2032 = g/2.
Weight = 400/2 = 200 N.`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 8 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pgg",st:"g with height and depth",q:"The acceleration due to gravity at height h above the earth if h << R (Radius of earth) is given by",o:["g\u2032 = g(1 \u2212 {2h;R})","g\u2032 = g(1 \u2212 {2h\xB2;R\xB2})","g\u2032 = g(1 \u2212 {h;2R})","g\u2032 = g(1 \u2212 {h\xB2;2R\xB2})"],c:0,s:`g\u2032 = g/(1 + h/R)\xB2 = g(1 + h/R)\u207B\xB2.
For h << R, use the binomial expansion: (1 + x)\u207B\xB2 \u2248 1 \u2212 2x.
So g\u2032 \u2248 g(1 \u2212 2h/R).`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 10 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pgg",st:"g with height and depth",q:`Assuming the earth to be a sphere of uniform mass density, the weight of a body at a depth d = {R;2} from the surface of earth, if its weight on the surface of earth is 200 N, will be
(Given R = radius of earth)`,o:["400 N","500 N","100 N","300 N"],c:2,s:`Inside the earth, g\u2032 = g(1 \u2212 d/R).
d = R/2, so g\u2032 = g/2.
Weight = 200/2 = 100 N.`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 11 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pgg",st:"g on planets",q:"The radii of two planets 'A' and 'B' are 'R' and '4R' and their densities are \u03C1 and \u03C1/3 respectively. The ratio of acceleration due to gravity at their surfaces (g_A : g_B) will be",o:["1 : 16","3 : 16","3 : 4","4 : 3"],c:2,s:`For a planet of uniform density, g = GM/R\xB2 = (4/3)\u03C0G\u03C1R, so g \u221D \u03C1R.
g_A \u221D \u03C1 \xD7 R = \u03C1R.
g_B \u221D (\u03C1/3) \xD7 4R = 4\u03C1R/3.
g_A : g_B = 1 : 4/3 = 3 : 4.`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 13 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pgg",st:"g on planets",q:"Two planets A and B of radii R and 1.5R have densities \u03C1 and \u03C1/2 respectively. The ratio of acceleration due to gravity at the surface of B to A is",o:["2 : 3","4 : 3","2 : 1","3 : 4"],c:3,s:`g = (4/3)\u03C0G\u03C1R, so g \u221D \u03C1R.
g_B \u221D (\u03C1/2)(1.5R) = 0.75 \u03C1R; g_A \u221D \u03C1R.
g_B : g_A = 0.75 : 1 = 3 : 4.`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 24 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pgg",st:"g with height and depth",q:"The weight of a body at the surface of earth is 18 N. The weight of the body at an altitude of 3200 km above the earth's surface is (given radius of earth R_e = 6400 km)",o:["19.6 N","9.8 N","4.9 N","8 N"],c:3,s:`Height 3200 km = R/2, so the distance from the centre is 1.5R.
Weight \u221D 1/r\xB2, so weight = 18 \xD7 (R/1.5R)\xB2 = 18 \xD7 4/9 = 8 N.`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 31 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pgg",st:"g with height and depth",q:'At a certain depth "d" below surface of earth, value of acceleration due to gravity becomes four times that of its value at a height 3R above earth surface. Where R is Radius of earth (Take R = 6400 km). The depth d is equal to',o:["4800 km","640 km","2560 km","5260 km"],c:0,s:`At height 3R the distance from the centre is 4R, so g_h = g/16.
Four times this is g/4.
At depth d, g_d = g(1 \u2212 d/R) = g/4, so d/R = 3/4.
d = 0.75 \xD7 6400 = 4800 km.`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 31 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pgg",st:"g with height and depth",q:"A body weight W, is projected vertically upwards from earth's surface to reach a height above the earth which is equal to nine times the radius of earth. The weight of the body at that height will be",o:["{W;100}","{W;3}","{W;91}","{W;9}"],c:0,s:`Height = 9R, so the distance from the centre = R + 9R = 10R.
Weight \u221D 1/r\xB2, so weight = W \xD7 (R/10R)\xB2 = W/100.`},{y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 25 Jul \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pgg",st:"g with height and depth",q:"An object is taken to a height above the surface of earth at a distance {5;4}R from the centre of the earth, where radius of earth, R = 6400 km. The percentage decrease in the weight of the object will be",o:["36%","50%","64%","25%"],c:0,s:`At r = 5R/4, weight\u2032 = W \xD7 (R/r)\xB2 = W \xD7 16/25 = 0.64 W.
Decrease = 1 \u2212 0.64 = 0.36, that is 36%.`},{y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 26 Jul \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pgg",st:"g with height and depth",q:"The percentage decrease in the weight of a rocket, when taken to a height of 32 km above the surface of earth will be (Radius of earth = 6400 km)",o:["1%","3%","4%","0.5%"],c:0,s:`For h << R, g\u2032 \u2248 g(1 \u2212 2h/R).
Fractional decrease = 2h/R = 2 \xD7 32/6400 = 0.01.
Percentage decrease = 1%.`},{y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 28 Jul \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pgg",st:"g on planets",q:"If the radius of earth shrinks by 2% while its mass remains same. The acceleration due to gravity on the earth's surface will approximately",o:["decrease by 2%","decrease by 4%","increase by 2%","increase by 4%"],c:3,s:`g = GM/R\xB2, so g \u221D 1/R\xB2.
For a small change, \u0394g/g = \u22122\u0394R/R = \u22122 \xD7 (\u22122%) = +4%.
So g increases by about 4%.`},{y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 24 Jun \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pgg",st:"g with height and depth",q:`The approximate height from the surface of earth at which the weight of the body becomes {1;3} of its weight on the surface of earth is
[Radius of earth R = 6400 km and \u221A3 = 1.732]`,o:["3840 km","4685 km","2133 km","4267 km"],c:1,s:`Weight is 1/3 of the surface value: (R/(R+h))\xB2 = 1/3.
So (R+h)/R = \u221A3, which gives h = (\u221A3 \u2212 1)R.
h = 0.732 \xD7 6400 \u2248 4685 km.`},{y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 25 Jun \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pgg",st:"g with height and depth",q:`The height of any point P above the surface of earth is equal to diameter of earth. The value of acceleration due to gravity at point P will be :
(Given g = acceleration due to gravity at the surface of earth).`,o:["g/2","g/4","g/3","g/9"],c:3,s:`Height = diameter = 2R, so the distance from the centre is R + 2R = 3R.
g\u2032 = g(R/3R)\xB2 = g/9.`},{y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 26 Jun \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pgg",st:"g with height and depth",q:"The variation of acceleration due to gravity (g) with distance (r) from the center of the earth is correctly represented by (Given R = radius of earth)",fig:{w:340,h:250,alt:"Four small graphs of g against r, labelled (a) to (d). (a) g rises linearly from zero to a peak at r = R and then falls as 1/r\xB2. (b) g is constant up to R and then falls. (c) g falls from a very large value at r = 0 all the way. (d) g rises to a higher, sharper peak at R and then falls.",d:[["l",26,100,161,100,"a"],["l",26,100,26,12,"a"],["t",32,20,"g","s"],["t",164,104,"r","s"],["t",24,112,"0","es"],["t",20,28,"(a)","e"],["l",26,100,86,45],["p","M86 45 Q104 69 156 92"],["l",86,45,86,100,"d"],["t",86,113,"R","ms"],["l",196,100,331,100,"a"],["l",196,100,196,12,"a"],["t",202,20,"g","s"],["t",334,104,"r","s"],["t",194,112,"0","es"],["t",190,28,"(b)","e"],["l",196,52,256,52],["p","M256 52 Q274 78 326 92"],["l",256,52,256,100,"d"],["t",256,113,"R","ms"],["l",26,225,161,225,"a"],["l",26,225,26,137,"a"],["t",32,145,"g","s"],["t",164,229,"r","s"],["t",24,237,"0","es"],["t",20,153,"(c)","e"],["p","M34 145 Q44 211 156 218"],["l",196,225,331,225,"a"],["l",196,225,196,137,"a"],["t",202,145,"g","s"],["t",334,229,"r","s"],["t",194,237,"0","es"],["t",190,153,"(d)","e"],["p","M196 225 Q244 217 256 155"],["p","M256 155 Q274 186 326 217"],["l",256,155,256,225,"d"],["t",256,238,"R","ms"]]},o:["Graph (a)","Graph (b)","Graph (c)","Graph (d)"],c:0,s:`Inside the earth (r < R), g = GMr/R\xB3, so g rises linearly from zero at the centre to its maximum g at r = R.
Outside (r > R), g = GM/r\xB2, so g falls as 1/r\xB2.
Only graph (a) shows a straight rise to a peak at R followed by a 1/r\xB2 fall.
(b) has constant g inside, (c) has g falling from the centre, and (d) is not linear inside.`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 31 Aug \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pgg",st:"g with height and depth",q:`If R_E be the radius of Earth, then the ratio between the acceleration due to gravity at a depth r below and a height r above the earth surface is
(Given : r < R_E)`,o:["1 + {r;R_E} \u2212 {r\xB2;R_E\xB2} \u2212 {r\xB3;R_E\xB3}","1 + {r;R_E} + {r\xB2;R_E\xB2} + {r\xB3;R_E\xB3}","1 \u2212 {r;R_E} \u2212 {r\xB2;R_E\xB2} \u2212 {r\xB3;R_E\xB3}","1 + {r;R_E} \u2212 {r\xB2;R_E\xB2} + {r\xB3;R_E\xB3}"],c:0,s:`Let x = r/R_E.
At depth r: g_d = g(1 \u2212 x). At height r: g_h = g/(1 + x)\xB2.
Ratio = (1 \u2212 x)(1 + x)\xB2 = (1 \u2212 x)(1 + 2x + x\xB2).
Expanding: 1 + 2x + x\xB2 \u2212 x \u2212 2x\xB2 \u2212 x\xB3 = 1 + x \u2212 x\xB2 \u2212 x\xB3.
That is 1 + r/R_E \u2212 r\xB2/R_E\xB2 \u2212 r\xB3/R_E\xB3.`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 20 Jul \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pgl",st:"Force between masses",q:"A person whose mass is 100 kg travels from Earth to Mars in a spaceship. Neglect all other objects in sky and take acceleration due to gravity on the surface of the Earth and Mars as 10 m s\u207B\xB2 and 4 m s\u207B\xB2 respectively. Identify from the below figures, the curve that fits best for the weight of the passenger as a function of time.",fig:{w:340,h:215,alt:"Graph of weight against time for the trip. All curves start at 1000 N on the weight axis and end at point x on the dashed 400 N line. Curve A is a straight line, B is a gentle convex curve, C falls to touch the time axis and then rises, D dips below the time axis and then rises.",d:[["l",60,185,60,12,"a"],["l",60,170,325,170,"a"],["t",64,14,"y","s"],["t",322,186,"Time","e"],["t",54,34,"1000 N","es"],["t",54,118,"400 N","es"],["l",60,114,320,114,"d"],["t",268,108,"x","s"],["l",60,30,260,114],["t",150,72,"(A)","s"],["p","M60 30 Q110 112 260 114"],["t",132,100,"(B)","s"],["p","M60 30 C72 135 110 168 170 168 C215 168 240 140 260 114"],["t",196,160,"(C)","s"],["p","M60 30 C75 190 130 205 165 192 C210 182 240 140 260 114"],["t",150,205,"(D)","s"]]},o:["(A)","(B)","(C)","(D)"],c:2,s:`On Earth the weight is 100 \xD7 10 = 1000 N. It falls as the ship moves away (the Earth's pull decreases as 1/r\xB2).
On the way, Mars's pull grows. At one point the two pulls are equal and opposite, so the net weight is zero.
After that Mars's pull dominates and the weight rises to 100 \xD7 4 = 400 N on arrival.
So the curve must fall to zero (touch the time axis) and then rise to 400 N, without going negative. That is curve (C).`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 18 Mar \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pgg",st:"Rotation of the Earth",q:`If the angular velocity of earth's spin is increased such that the bodies at the equator start floating, the duration of the day would be approximately :
[Take g = 10 m s\u207B\xB2, the radius of earth, R = 6400 \xD7 10\xB3 m, Take \u03C0 = 3.14]`,o:["does not change","60 minutes","1200 minutes","84 minutes"],c:3,s:`Bodies float when the effective g at the equator is zero: \u03C9\xB2R = g.
\u03C9 = \u221A(g/R) = \u221A(10/6.4\xD710\u2076) = 1.25 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 rad/s.
T = 2\u03C0/\u03C9 = 2 \xD7 3.14/(1.25 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3) \u2248 5024 s.
5024 s \u2248 83.7 minutes \u2248 84 minutes.`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 24 Feb \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pgg",st:"Rotation of the Earth",q:`A body weighs 49 N on a spring balance at the north pole. What will be its weight recorded on the same weighing machine, if it is shifted to the equator?
[Use g = {GM;R\xB2} = 9.8 ms\u207B\xB2 and radius of earth, R = 6400 km]`,o:["49.83 N","48.83 N","49.17 N","49 N"],c:1,s:`Mass = 49/9.8 = 5 kg.
At the equator, effective g = g \u2212 \u03C9\xB2R. \u03C9 = 2\u03C0/86400 = 7.27 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 rad/s.
\u03C9\xB2R = (7.27 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075)\xB2 \xD7 6.4 \xD7 10\u2076 \u2248 0.034 m/s\xB2.
Weight = 5 \xD7 (9.8 \u2212 0.034) = 48.83 N.`},{y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 2 Sep \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pgg",st:"g with height and depth",q:"The height 'h' at which the weight of a body will be the same as that at the same depth 'h' from the surface of the earth is (Radius of the earth is R and effect of the rotation of the earth is neglected)",o:["{R;2}","{\u221A5;2}R \u2212 R","{\u221A3R \u2212 R;2}","{\u221A5R \u2212 R;2}"],c:3,s:`Let x = h/R. Height: g/(1 + x)\xB2. Depth: g(1 \u2212 x).
Set equal: (1 \u2212 x)(1 + x)\xB2 = 1, so 1 + x \u2212 x\xB2 \u2212 x\xB3 = 1.
This gives x(x\xB2 + x \u2212 1) = 0, so x\xB2 + x \u2212 1 = 0 (x \u2260 0).
x = (\u221A5 \u2212 1)/2, so h = (\u221A5R \u2212 R)/2.`},{y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 5 Sep \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pgg",st:"g with height and depth",q:"The value of the acceleration due to gravity is g\u2081 at a height h = {R;2} (R = radius of the earth) from the surface of the earth. It is again equal to g\u2081 at a depth d below the surface of the earth. The ratio ({d;R}) equals",o:["4/9","5/9","1/3","7/9"],c:1,s:`At height R/2: g\u2081 = g(R/(1.5R))\xB2 = 4g/9.
At depth d: g\u2081 = g(1 \u2212 d/R).
So 1 \u2212 d/R = 4/9, which gives d/R = 5/9.`},{y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 5 Sep \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pgg",st:"Rotation of the Earth",q:"The acceleration due to gravity on the earth's surface at the poles is g and angular velocity of the earth about the axis passing through the pole is \u03C9. An object is weighed at the equator and at a height h above the poles by using a spring balance. If the weights are found to be same, then h is (h << R, where R is the radius of the earth)",o:["{R\xB2\u03C9\xB2;2g}","{R\xB2\u03C9\xB2;g}","{R\xB2\u03C9\xB2;4g}","{R\xB2\u03C9\xB2;8g}"],c:0,s:`At the equator the weight is m(g \u2212 \u03C9\xB2R).
At a small height h above the pole the weight is mg(1 \u2212 2h/R).
Equal weights: \u03C9\xB2R = 2gh/R.
So h = R\xB2\u03C9\xB2/(2g).`},{y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 7 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pgg",st:"Rotation of the Earth",q:"A box weighs 196 N on a spring balance at the north pole. Its weight recorded on the same balance if it is shifted to the equator is close to (Take g = 10 m s\u207B\xB2 at the north pole and the radius of the earth = 6400 km)",o:["194.32 N","194.66 N","195.66 N","195.32 N"],c:3,s:`Mass = 196/10 = 19.6 kg.
\u03C9 = 2\u03C0/86400 = 7.27 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 rad/s, so \u03C9\xB2R = 5.29 \xD7 10\u207B\u2079 \xD7 6.4 \xD7 10\u2076 \u2248 0.0338 m/s\xB2.
Loss of weight = 19.6 \xD7 0.0338 \u2248 0.66 N.
Weight at the equator \u2248 196 \u2212 0.66 \u2248 195.3 N, which is closest to 195.32 N.`},{y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 10 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pgg",st:"g with height and depth",q:"The value of acceleration due to gravity at Earth's surface is 9.8 m s\u207B\xB2. The altitude above its surface at which the acceleration due to gravity decreases to 4.9 m s\u207B\xB2, is close to (Radius of Earth = 6.4 \xD7 10\u2076 m)",o:["1.6 \xD7 10\u2076 m","2.6 \xD7 10\u2076 m","6.4 \xD7 10\u2076 m","9.0 \xD7 10\u2076 m"],c:1,s:`g\u2032 = g/2, so (R/(R+h))\xB2 = 1/2.
R + h = \u221A2 R, so h = (\u221A2 \u2212 1)R = 0.414 \xD7 6.4 \xD7 10\u2076.
h \u2248 2.6 \xD7 10\u2076 m.`},{y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 12 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pgg",st:"g on planets",q:"The ratio of the weights of a body on the Earth's surface to that on the surface of a planet is 9 : 4. The mass of the planet is {1;9}th of that of the Earth. If 'R' is the radius of the Earth, what is the radius of the planet? (Take the planets to have the same mass density)",o:["R/3","R/9","R/4","R/2"],c:3,s:`Weight ratio = ratio of g, so g_E/g_P = 9/4.
g = GM/R\xB2, so g_E/g_P = (M_E/M_P)(R_P/R_E)\xB2 = 9 \xD7 (R_P/R)\xB2.
So 9 (R_P/R)\xB2 = 9/4, giving R_P/R = 1/2.
R_P = R/2.`},{y:"JEE Main 2018 \xB7 Online",tp:"pgg",st:"Rotation of the Earth",q:"Suppose that the angular velocity of rotation of earth is increased. Then, as a consequence",o:["there will be no change in weight anywhere on the earth","weight of the object, everywhere on the earth, will increase","except at poles, weight of the object on the earth will decrease","weight of the object, everywhere on the earth, will decrease."],c:2,s:`Effective g at latitude \u03BB is g\u2032 = g \u2212 \u03C9\xB2R cos\xB2\u03BB.
If \u03C9 increases, the term \u03C9\xB2R cos\xB2\u03BB increases, so weight decreases.
At the poles \u03BB = 90\xB0, cos \u03BB = 0, so there is no change.
So weight decreases everywhere except at the poles.`},{y:"JEE Main 2017 \xB7 Online",tp:"pgg",st:"Rotation of the Earth",q:"If the Earth has no rotational motion, the weight of a person on the equator is W. Determine the speed with which the earth would have to rotate about its axis so that the person at the equator will weigh {3;4}W. Radius of the Earth is 6400 km and g = 10 m/s\xB2.",o:["0.63 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 rad/s","0.83 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 rad/s","0.28 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 rad/s","1.1 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 rad/s"],c:0,s:`Without rotation W = mg. With rotation the weight at the equator is m(g \u2212 \u03C9\xB2R).
m(g \u2212 \u03C9\xB2R) = (3/4)mg, so \u03C9\xB2R = g/4.
\u03C9 = \u221A(g/4R) = \u221A(10/(4 \xD7 6.4 \xD7 10\u2076)) = \u221A(3.9 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077).
\u03C9 \u2248 0.63 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 rad/s.`},{y:"AIEEE 2009",tp:"pgg",st:"g with height and depth",q:"The height at which the acceleration due to gravity becomes g/9 (where g = the acceleration due to gravity on the surface of the earth) in terms of R, the radius of the earth is",o:["2R","{R;\u221A2}","R/2","\u221A2R"],c:0,s:`g\u2032 = g(R/(R+h))\xB2 = g/9.
So (R+h)/R = 3, which gives h = 2R.`},{y:"AIEEE 2005",tp:"pgg",st:"g on planets",q:"Average density of the earth",o:["is directly proportional to g","is inversely proportional to g","does not depend on g","is a complex function of g."],c:0,s:`g = GM/R\xB2 = (4/3)\u03C0G\u03C1R.
So \u03C1 = 3g/(4\u03C0GR).
For the earth (fixed R and G), \u03C1 is directly proportional to g.`},{y:"AIEEE 2005",tp:"pgg",st:"g with height and depth",q:"The change in the value of g at a height h above the surface of the earth is the same as at a depth d below the surface of earth. When both d and h are much smaller than the radius of earth, then which of the following is correct?",o:["d = 2h","d = h","d = h/2","d = 3h/2"],c:0,s:`Height: g\u2032 \u2248 g(1 \u2212 2h/R), so the change is 2gh/R.
Depth: g\u2032 = g(1 \u2212 d/R), so the change is gd/R.
Equal changes: d/R = 2h/R, so d = 2h.`},{y:"IIT-JEE 2001",tp:"pgg",st:"g with height and depth",q:"A simple pendulum has a time period T\u2081 when on the earth's surface, and T\u2082 when taken to a height R above the earth's surface, where R is the radius of the earth. The value of T\u2082/T\u2081 is",o:["1","\u221A2","4","2"],c:3,s:`T = 2\u03C0\u221A(l/g), so T \u221D 1/\u221Ag.
At height R the distance from the centre is 2R, so g\u2082 = g/4.
T\u2082/T\u2081 = \u221A(g/g\u2082) = \u221A4 = 2.`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1981",tp:"pgg",st:"g on planets",q:"If the radius of the earth were to shrink by one percent, its mass remaining the same, the acceleration due to gravity on the earth's surface would",o:["decrease","remain unchanged","increase","none of these."],c:2,s:`g = GM/R\xB2 with M fixed, so g \u221D 1/R\xB2.
If R decreases by 1%, g increases by about 2%.
So g would increase.`},{y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 24 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pgg",st:"g on planets",q:"Acceleration due to gravity on the surface of earth is 'g'. If the diameter of earth is reduced to one third of its original value and mass remains unchanged, then the acceleration due to gravity on the surface of the earth is ______ g.",n:9,s:`g = GM/R\xB2 with M constant, so g \u221D 1/R\xB2.
Radius becomes R/3, so g\u2032 = GM/(R/3)\xB2 = 9GM/R\xB2 = 9g.
The answer is 9.`},{y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 29 Jul \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pgg",st:"g with height and depth",q:"If the acceleration due to gravity experienced by a point mass at height h above the surface of earth is same as that of the acceleration due to gravity at a depth \u03B1h(h << R\u2091) from the earth surface. The value of \u03B1 will be ______. (Use R\u2091 = 6400 km)",n:2,s:`For small h above the surface: g' = g(1 \u2212 2h/R\u2091).
At depth d: g'' = g(1 \u2212 d/R\u2091).
Equal values give 2h = d = \u03B1h.
So \u03B1 = 2.`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 26 Feb \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pgg",st:"g with height and depth",q:"In the reported figure of earth the value of acceleration due to gravity is same at point A and C but it is smaller than that of its value at point B (surface of the earth). The value of OA : AB will be x : y. The value of x is ______.",fig:{w:340,h:220,alt:"Earth of radius R = 6400 km with centre O. Point A lies inside along a vertical line, B is on the surface and C is 3200 km above B.",d:[["c",170,130,70],["l",170,25,170,130],["l",160,25,180,25],["c",170,130,2,"F"],["l",162,99,178,99],["t",150,30,"C","e"],["t",150,65,"B","e"],["t",180,95,"A"],["t",178,142,"O"],["l",214,26,214,59,"b"],["t",222,45,"3200 km","s"],["l",170,130,121,179,"a"],["t",118,196,"R = 6400 km","s"],["t",70,135,"Earth","s"]]},n:4,s:`Point C is 3200 km above the surface, so its distance from O is 6400 + 3200 = 9600 km.
g_C = g(R/9600)\xB2 = g(6400/9600)\xB2 = 4g/9.
Inside the earth g_A = g\xB7(OA/R). Setting g_A = 4g/9 gives OA = 4R/9.
Then AB = R \u2212 4R/9 = 5R/9, so OA : AB = 4 : 5.
Hence x = 4.`},{y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 8 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pgg",st:"g on planets",q:"A ball is dropped from the top of a 100 m high tower on a planet. In the last \xBD s before hitting the ground, it covers a distance of 19 m. Acceleration due to gravity (in m s\u207B\xB2) near the surface on that planet is ______.",n:8,s:`Let the fall take time T. Then 100 = \xBDgT\xB2, so T = \u221A(200/g).
Distance in the last \xBD s: \xBDgT\xB2 \u2212 \xBDg(T \u2212 \xBD)\xB2 = g(T/2 \u2212 1/8) = 19.
Try g = 8: T = \u221A25 = 5 s, and 8(2.5 \u2212 0.125) = 19. This fits.
So g = 8 m s\u207B\xB2.`},{y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 29 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pgg",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:`Given below are two statements: one is labelled as Assertion (A) and the other is labelled as Reason (R).
Assertion (A): Time period of a simple pendulum is longer at the top of a mountain than that at the base of the mountain.
Reason (R): Time period of a simple pendulum decreases with increasing value of acceleration due to gravity and vice-versa.
In the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:`,o:["Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)","Both (A) and (R) are true but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)","(A) is true but (R) is false","(A) is false but (R) is true"],c:0,s:`T = 2\u03C0\u221A(l/g), so T decreases when g increases. R is true.
The value of g is smaller at the top of a mountain than at the base.
So T is longer at the top. A is true.
R explains A, so the answer is (a).`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 10 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pgg",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:`Given below are two statements:
Statement I: Rotation of the earth shows effect on the value of acceleration due to gravity (g).
Statement II: The effect of rotation of the earth on the value of 'g' at the equator is minimum and that at the pole is maximum.
In the light of above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below.`,o:["Both Statement I and Statement II are true.","Both Statement I and Statement II are false.","Statement I is true but Statement II is false.","Statement I is false but Statement II is true."],c:2,s:`The change in g due to rotation is g' = g \u2212 \u03C9\xB2R cos\xB2\u03BB. So rotation does affect g. Statement I is true.
At the equator (\u03BB = 0) the loss \u03C9\xB2R is largest. At the poles (\u03BB = 90\xB0) there is no effect at all.
So the effect of rotation is maximum at the equator and minimum (zero) at the pole. Statement II states the opposite, so it is false.
Statement I is true but Statement II is false: option (c).`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 1 Feb \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pgg",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:`Given below are two statements:
Statement I: Acceleration due to gravity is different at different places on the surface of earth.
Statement II: Acceleration due to gravity increases as we go down below the earth's surface.
In the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below.`,o:["Both Statement I and Statement II are false.","Statement I is false but Statement II is true.","Statement I is true but Statement II is false.","Both Statement I and Statement II are true."],c:2,s:`g changes with latitude and height, so it is different at different places. Statement I is true.
Below the surface g = g\u2080(1 \u2212 d/R). It decreases with depth and is zero at the centre.
Statement II is false.
Answer: (c).`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 24 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pgg",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:`Given below are two statements.
Statement I: Acceleration due to earth's gravity decreases as you go 'up' or 'down' from earth's surface.
Statement II: Acceleration due to earth's gravity is same at a height 'h' and depth 'd' from earth's surface, if h = d.
`,o:["Statement I is correct but statement II is incorrect.","Both statement I and statement II are incorrect.","Both statement I and statement II are correct.","Statement I is incorrect but statement II is correct."],c:0,s:`Going up: g' = g(1 \u2212 2h/R). Going down: g' = g(1 \u2212 d/R). Both are less than g, so Statement I is correct.
For equal values 2h = d, not h = d.
So Statement II is incorrect.
Answer: (a).`},{y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 26 Jun \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pgg",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:`Assertion A: If we move from poles to equator, the direction of acceleration due to gravity of earth always points towards the center of earth without any variation in its magnitude.
Reason R: At equator, the direction of acceleration due to the gravity is towards the center of earth.`,o:["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A.","Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A.","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],c:3,s:`Because of the earth's rotation the magnitude of g changes from pole to equator (g is smaller at the equator). So A is false.
At the equator the centrifugal effect acts along the same line as gravity, so the direction of g points to the centre. R is true.
Answer: (d).`},{y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 27 Jun \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pgg",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:`Given below are two statements:
Statement I: The law of gravitation holds good for any pair of bodies in the universe.
Statement II: The weight of any person becomes zero when the person is at the centre of the earth.`,o:["Both Statement I and Statement II are true.","Both Statement I and Statement II are false.","Statement I is true but Statement II is false.","Statement I is false but Statement II is true."],c:0,s:`The law of gravitation is universal, so Statement I is true.
At the centre of the earth g = 0 (equal pulls from all sides cancel), so weight is zero. Statement II is true.
Answer: (a).`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1984",tp:"pgg",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:"The numerical value of the angular velocity of rotation of the earth should be ........... rad/s in order to make the effective acceleration due to gravity at equator equal to zero.",a:"1.237 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 rad/s",s:`Effective g at the equator is g' = g \u2212 \u03C9\xB2R.
Setting g' = 0 gives \u03C9 = \u221A(g/R).
Take g = 9.8 m/s\xB2 and R = 6.4 \xD7 10\u2076 m.
\u03C9 = \u221A(9.8/6.4\xD710\u2076) = \u221A(1.53\xD710\u207B\u2076) \u2248 1.237 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 rad/s.`},{y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 23 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pgk",st:"Kepler's laws",q:"If a satellite orbiting the earth is 9 times closer to the earth than the moon, what is the time period of rotation of the satellite? Given, rotational time period of moon = 27 days and gravitational attraction between the satellite and the moon is neglected.",o:["1 day","27 days","81 days","3 days"],c:0,s:`Kepler's third law: T\xB2 \u221D r\xB3, so T \u221D r^(3/2).
The satellite orbit radius is 1/9 of the moon's.
T = 27 \xD7 (1/9)^(3/2) = 27 \xD7 (1/27) = 1 day.`},{y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 24 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pgk",st:"Kepler's laws",q:"A satellite is launched into a circular orbit of radius 'R' around the earth. A second satellite is launched into an orbit of radius 1.03 R. The time period of revolution of the second satellite is larger than the first one approximately by",o:["2.5%","4.5%","3%","9%"],c:1,s:`T \u221D R^(3/2).
T\u2082/T\u2081 = (1.03)^(3/2) \u2248 1 + 1.5 \xD7 0.03 = 1.045.
So T\u2082 is larger by about 4.5%.`},{y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 8 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pgk",st:"Kepler's laws",q:"Two planets A and B having masses m\u2081 and m\u2082 move around the sun in circular orbits of r\u2081 and r\u2082 radii, respectively. If angular momentum of A is L and that of B is 3L, the ratio of time period {T_A;T_B} is",o:["(r\u2082/r\u2081)^(3/2)","(r\u2081/r\u2082)\xB3","27(m\u2081/m\u2082)\xB3","{1;27}(m\u2082/m\u2081)\xB3"],c:3,s:`For a circular orbit, v = \u221A(GM/r), so angular momentum L = m\u221A(GMr).
L_A = m\u2081\u221A(GMr\u2081) = L and L_B = m\u2082\u221A(GMr\u2082) = 3L.
Dividing and squaring: m\u2081\xB2r\u2081 / (m\u2082\xB2r\u2082) = 1/9, so r\u2081/r\u2082 = (1/9)(m\u2082/m\u2081)\xB2.
T \u221D r^(3/2): T_A/T_B = (r\u2081/r\u2082)^(3/2) = (1/27)(m\u2082/m\u2081)\xB3.`},{y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 29 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pgk",st:"Kepler's laws",q:"A planet takes 200 days to complete one revolution around the Sun. If the distance of the planet from Sun is reduced to one fourth of the original distance, how many days will it take to complete one revolution?",o:["50","25","100","20"],c:1,s:`T\xB2 \u221D r\xB3, so T \u221D r^(3/2).
When r becomes r/4, T becomes T \xD7 (1/4)^(3/2) = T/8.
T = 200/8 = 25 days.`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 29 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pgk",st:"Kepler's laws",q:"If the distance of the earth from sun is 1.5 \xD7 10\u2076 km. Then the distance of an imaginary planet from Sun, if its period of revolution is 2.83 years is",o:["6 \xD7 10\u2076 km","3 \xD7 10\u2076 km","6 \xD7 10\u2077 km","3 \xD7 10\u2077 km"],c:1,s:`T\xB2 \u221D r\xB3 with the earth's period 1 year.
r = 1.5\xD710\u2076 \xD7 (2.83)^(2/3).
(2.83)\xB2 = 8, and 8^(1/3) = 2, so (2.83)^(2/3) = 2.
r = 3 \xD7 10\u2076 km.`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 25 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pgk",st:"Kepler's laws",q:`Every planet revolves around the sun in an elliptical orbit.
A. The force acting on a planet is inversely proportional to square of distance from sun.
B. Force acting on planet is inversely proportional to product of the masses of the planet and the sun.
C. The centripetal force acting on the planet is directed away from the sun.
D. The square of time period of revolution of planet around sun is directly proportional to cube of semi-major axis of elliptical orbit.
Choose the correct answer from the options given below.`,o:["A and D only","B and C only","A and C only","C and D only"],c:0,s:`A is true: F = GMm/r\xB2.
B is false: F is directly proportional to the product of the masses.
C is false: the centripetal force points towards the sun.
D is true: this is Kepler's third law, T\xB2 \u221D a\xB3.
So A and D only are correct.`},{y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 24 Jun \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pgk",st:"Kepler's laws",q:"The distance between Sun and Earth is R. The duration of year if the distance between Sun and Earth becomes 3R will be",o:["\u221A3 years","3 years","9 years","3\u221A3 years"],c:3,s:`T\xB2 \u221D R\xB3, so T \u221D R^(3/2).
T' = 1 year \xD7 3^(3/2) = 3\u221A3 years.`},{y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 28 Jun \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pgk",st:"Kepler's laws",q:"Two planets A and B of equal mass are having their period of revolutions T_A and T_B such that T_A = 2T_B. These planets are revolving in the circular orbits of radii r_A and r_B respectively. Which out of the following would be the correct relationship of their orbits?",o:["2r_A\xB2 = r_B\xB3","r_A\xB3 = 2r_B\xB3","r_A\xB3 = 4r_B\xB3","T_A\xB2 \u2212 T_B\xB2 = {\u03C0\xB2;GM}(r_B\xB3 \u2212 4r_A\xB3)"],c:2,s:`Kepler's third law: T\xB2 \u221D r\xB3.
(T_A/T_B)\xB2 = (r_A/r_B)\xB3 \u2192 4 = r_A\xB3/r_B\xB3.
So r_A\xB3 = 4r_B\xB3.`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 25 Jul \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pgk",st:"Elliptical orbits and angular momentum",q:"The minimum and maximum distance of a planet revolving around the Sun are x\u2081 and x\u2082. If the minimum speed of the planet on its trajectory is v\u2080 then its maximum speed will be",o:["{v\u2080x\u2082\xB2;x\u2081\xB2}","{v\u2080x\u2081\xB2;x\u2082\xB2}","{v\u2080x\u2082;x\u2081}","{v\u2080x\u2081;x\u2082}"],c:2,s:`Angular momentum is conserved: m v r = constant at the two extreme points.
The speed is least at the largest distance x\u2082 and greatest at the smallest distance x\u2081.
v\u2080x\u2082 = v_max\xB7x\u2081, so v_max = v\u2080x\u2082/x\u2081.`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 27 Jul \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pgk",st:"Kepler's laws",q:`The planet Mars has two moons, if one of them has a period 7 hours, 30 minutes and an orbital radius of 9.0 \xD7 10\xB3 km. Find the mass of Mars.
{Given {4\u03C0\xB2;G} = 6 \xD7 10\xB9\xB9 N\u207B\xB9 m\u207B\xB2 kg\xB2}`,o:["7.02 \xD7 10\xB2\u2075 kg","5.96 \xD7 10\xB9\u2079 kg","3.25 \xD7 10\xB2\xB9 kg","6.00 \xD7 10\xB2\xB3 kg"],c:3,s:`T\xB2 = (4\u03C0\xB2/GM) r\xB3, so M = (4\u03C0\xB2/G)\xB7r\xB3/T\xB2.
T = 7.5 h = 27000 s, r = 9\xD710\u2076 m.
r\xB3 = 7.29\xD710\xB2\u2070 m\xB3 and T\xB2 = 7.29\xD710\u2078 s\xB2.
M = 6\xD710\xB9\xB9 \xD7 7.29\xD710\xB2\u2070 / 7.29\xD710\u2078 = 6 \xD7 10\xB2\xB3 kg.`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 16 Mar \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pgk",st:"Elliptical orbits and angular momentum",q:"The maximum and minimum distances of a comet from the Sun are 1.6 \xD7 10\xB9\xB2 m and 8.0 \xD7 10\xB9\u2070 m respectively. If the speed of the comet at the nearest point is 6 \xD7 10\u2074 m s\u207B\xB9, the speed at the farthest point is",o:["3.0 \xD7 10\xB3 m s\u207B\xB9","4.5 \xD7 10\xB3 m s\u207B\xB9","1.5 \xD7 10\xB3 m s\u207B\xB9","6.0 \xD7 10\xB3 m s\u207B\xB9"],c:0,s:`Angular momentum is conserved: v\u2081r\u2081 = v\u2082r\u2082.
v_far = v_near \xD7 r_near / r_far = 6\xD710\u2074 \xD7 8\xD710\xB9\u2070 / 1.6\xD710\xB9\xB2.
v_far = 3.0 \xD7 10\xB3 m/s.`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 18 Mar \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pgk",st:"Elliptical orbits and angular momentum",q:"The angular momentum of a planet of mass M moving around the sun in an elliptical orbit is L\u20D7. The magnitude of the areal velocity of the planet is",o:["4L/M","2L/M","L/M","L/2M"],c:3,s:`In a short time dt the planet sweeps an area dA = \xBD r (v dt) sin\u03B8.
So dA/dt = \xBD r v sin\u03B8 = L/(2M), since L = M r v sin\u03B8.
Areal velocity = L/2M.`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 26 Feb \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pgk",st:"Elliptical orbits and angular momentum",q:`A planet revolving in elliptical orbit has
(A) a constant velocity of revolution.
(B) has the least velocity when it is nearest to the sun.
(C) its areal velocity is directly proportional to its velocity.
(D) areal velocity is inversely proportional to its velocity.
(E) to follow a trajectory such that the areal velocity is constant.
Choose the correct answer from the options given below:`,o:["C only","A only","E only","D only"],c:2,s:`The speed changes along the orbit, so (A) is false.
The speed is greatest, not least, at the nearest point, so (B) is false.
Areal velocity is constant (Kepler's second law), so it is not proportional to speed (C, D false).
(E) is true.
Only (E) is correct.`},{y:"JEE Main 2016 \xB7 Online",tp:"pgk",st:"Kepler's laws",q:"Figure shows elliptical path abcd of a planet around the sun S such that the area of triangle csa is {1;4} the area of the ellipse (see figure). With db as the semimajor axis, and ca as the semiminor axis. If t\u2081 is the time taken for planet to go over path abc and t\u2082 for path taken over cda then",fig:{w:340,h:200,alt:"Ellipse abcd with semi-major axis db horizontal and semi-minor axis ca vertical. The Sun S lies on the major axis to the left of the centre. Dashed lines join S to c and a and join c to a. The planet moves anticlockwise.",d:[["p","M60 100 A110 60 0 1 0 280 100 A110 60 0 1 0 60 100"],["l",35,100,305,100],["l",84,100,170,40,"d"],["l",84,100,170,160,"d"],["l",170,40,170,160,"d"],["c",84,100,2.5,"F"],["t",74,116,"S"],["t",52,96,"d","e"],["t",288,96,"b"],["t",170,32,"c","m"],["t",170,176,"a","m"],["l",222,153,236,148,"a"],["l",140,44,124,46,"a"]]},o:["t\u2081 = 4t\u2082","t\u2081 = 2t\u2082","t\u2081 = 3t\u2082","t\u2081 = t\u2082"],c:2,s:`The radius vector sweeps equal areas in equal times, so time is proportional to the area swept from S.
Let the ellipse area be A. The left half (c-d-a) has area A/2, and S lies inside it.
Area swept on c\u2192d\u2192a = A/2 \u2212 area of triangle csa = A/2 \u2212 A/4 = A/4.
Area swept on a\u2192b\u2192c = A \u2212 A/4 = 3A/4.
So t\u2081 : t\u2082 = 3 : 1, i.e. t\u2081 = 3t\u2082.`},{y:"IIT-JEE 2006",tp:"pgs",st:"Binary stars and multiple bodies",q:"A binary star system consists of two stars A and B which have time periods T_A and T_B, radii R_A and R_B and masses M_A and M_B. Then",o:["if T_A > T_B then R_A > R_B","if T_A > T_B then M_A > M_B","({T_A;T_B})\xB2 = ({R_A;R_B})\xB3","T_A = T_B"],c:3,s:`The two stars of a binary system orbit their common centre of mass.
They stay on opposite sides of it, so they complete one revolution in the same time.
Hence T_A = T_B always.`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1996",tp:"pgk",st:"Kepler's laws",q:"If the distance between the earth and the sun were half its present value, the number of day in a year would have been",o:["64.5","129","182.5","730"],c:1,s:`T\xB2 \u221D r\xB3, so T \u221D r^(3/2).
T' = 365 \xD7 (1/2)^(3/2) = 365/2.83 \u2248 129 days.`},{y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 3 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pgs",st:"Binary stars and multiple bodies",q:"Three identical spheres of mass m, are placed at the vertices of an equilateral triangle of length a. When released, they interact only through gravitational force and collide after a time T = 4 seconds. If the sides of the triangle are increased to length 2a and also the masses of the spheres are made 2m, then they will collide after ______ seconds.",n:8,s:`The spheres move towards the centroid, which is at distance a/\u221A3 from each.
By dimensional analysis the collision time is T \u221D \u221A(a\xB3/(Gm)).
New case: a \u2192 2a and m \u2192 2m, so T' = T\u221A(8/2) = 2T.
T' = 2 \xD7 4 = 8 s.`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 1 Sep \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pgk",st:"Kepler's laws",q:"Two satellites revolve around a planet in coplanar circular orbits in anticlockwise direction. Their period of revolutions are 1 hour and 8 hours respectively. The radius of the orbit of nearer satellite is 2 \xD7 10\xB3 km. The angular speed of the farther satellite as observed from the nearer satellite at the instant when both the satellites are closest is {\u03C0;x} rad h\u207B\xB9 where x is ______.",n:3,s:`T\xB2 \u221D r\xB3: r\u2082/r\u2081 = (8/1)^(2/3) = 4, so r\u2082 = 4r\u2081.
Speeds: v\u2081 = (2\u03C0/1)r\u2081 = 2\u03C0r\u2081 and v\u2082 = (2\u03C0/8)(4r\u2081) = \u03C0r\u2081 (per hour), both in the same direction.
At closest approach the relative velocity is v\u2081 \u2212 v\u2082 = \u03C0r\u2081, perpendicular to the line joining them.
Separation = r\u2082 \u2212 r\u2081 = 3r\u2081.
Relative angular speed = \u03C0r\u2081/3r\u2081 = \u03C0/3 rad/h, so x = 3.`},{y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 7 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pgk",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:`Given below are two statements : one is labelled as Assertion (A) and the other is labelled as Reason (R).
Assertion (A) : The radius vector from the Sun to a planet sweeps out equal areas in equal intervals of time and thus areal velocity of planet is constant.
Reason (R) : For a central force field the angular momentum is a constant.
In the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below :`,o:["(A) is not correct but (R) is correct","Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)","(A) is correct but (R) is not correct","Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)"],c:3,s:`The areal velocity is L/2m. It is constant only if the angular momentum L is constant.
The gravitational force is central, so its torque about the Sun is zero and L is conserved.
Both A and R are true, and R explains A.`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1997",tp:"pgk",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:"The ratio of earth's orbital angular momentum (about the sun) to its mass is 4.4 \xD7 10\xB9\u2075 m\xB2/s. The area enclosed by earth's orbit approximately is ______ m\xB2.",a:"6.94 \xD7 10\xB2\xB2 m\xB2",s:`Areal velocity dA/dt = L/(2m) = 4.4\xD710\xB9\u2075 / 2 = 2.2 \xD7 10\xB9\u2075 m\xB2/s.
In one year (3.15 \xD7 10\u2077 s) the radius vector sweeps the whole orbit area.
Area = 2.2\xD710\xB9\u2075 \xD7 3.156\xD710\u2077 \u2248 6.94 \xD7 10\xB2\xB2 m\xB2.`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1985",tp:"pgk",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:"According to Kepler's second law, the radius vector from the sun to a planet sweeps out equal areas in equal intervals of time. This law is a consequence of the conservation of ______.",a:"Angular momentum",s:`The gravitational force of the sun is central, so it exerts no torque about the sun.
Therefore the angular momentum of the planet is constant.
Areal velocity = L/2m is then constant, which is Kepler's second law.`},{y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 9 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pgs",st:"Energy of a satellite",q:"An astronaut takes a ball of mass m from earth to space. He throws the ball into a circular orbit about earth at an altitude of 318.5 km. From earth's surface to the orbit, the change in total mechanical energy of the ball is x {GM\u2091m;21R\u2091}. The value of x is (take R\u2091 = 6370 km)",o:["9","12","10","11"],c:3,s:`Initial energy on the surface: E\u2081 = \u2212GM\u2091m/R\u2091.
Orbit radius r = 6370 + 318.5 = 6688.5 km = 1.05R\u2091.
Energy in orbit: E\u2082 = \u2212GM\u2091m/(2r) = \u2212GM\u2091m/(2.1R\u2091).
\u0394E = GM\u2091m/R\u2091 (1 \u2212 1/2.1) = (1.1/2.1)GM\u2091m/R\u2091 = (11/21)GM\u2091m/R\u2091.
So x = 11.`},{y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 30 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pgl",st:"Field and potential of shells and spheres",q:"The gravitational potential at a point above the surface of earth is \u22125.12 \xD7 10\u2077 J/kg and the acceleration due to gravity at that point is 6.4 m/s\xB2. Assume that the mean radius of earth is 6400 km. The height of this point above the earth's surface is",o:["1600 km","540 km","1200 km","1000 km"],c:0,s:`At distance r from the centre: V = \u2212GM/r and g = GM/r\xB2.
So |V|/g = r = 5.12\xD710\u2077 / 6.4 = 8\xD710\u2076 m = 8000 km.
Height = 8000 \u2212 6400 = 1600 km.`},{y:"JEE Advanced 2024",tp:"pgs",st:"Orbital speed and period",q:`A particle of mass m is under the influence of the gravitational field of a body of mass M (>> m). The particle is moving in a circular orbit of radius r\u2080 with time period T\u2080 around the mass M. Then, the particle is subjected to an additional central force, corresponding to the potential energy V_c(r) = m\u03B1/r\xB3, where \u03B1 is a positive constant of suitable dimension and r is the distance from the center of the orbit. If the particle moves in the same circular orbit of radius r\u2080 in the combined gravitational potential due to M and V_c(r), but with a new time period T\u2081, then (T\u2081\xB2 \u2212 T\u2080\xB2)/T\u2081\xB2 is given by
[G is the gravitational constant.]`,o:["{3\u03B1;GMr\u2080\xB2}","{\u03B1;2GMr\u2080\xB2}","{\u03B1;GMr\u2080\xB2}","{2\u03B1;GMr\u2080\xB2}"],c:0,s:`The extra force is F = \u2212dV_c/dr = 3m\u03B1/r\u2074, directed outward (repulsive).
Originally m\u03C9\u2080\xB2r\u2080 = GMm/r\u2080\xB2, so \u03C9\u2080\xB2 = GM/r\u2080\xB3.
Now m\u03C9\u2081\xB2r\u2080 = GMm/r\u2080\xB2 \u2212 3m\u03B1/r\u2080\u2074, so \u03C9\u2081\xB2 = GM/r\u2080\xB3 \u2212 3\u03B1/r\u2080\u2075.
(T\u2081\xB2 \u2212 T\u2080\xB2)/T\u2081\xB2 = 1 \u2212 T\u2080\xB2/T\u2081\xB2 = 1 \u2212 \u03C9\u2081\xB2/\u03C9\u2080\xB2 = (3\u03B1/r\u2080\u2075)(r\u2080\xB3/GM) = 3\u03B1/(GMr\u2080\xB2).`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 11 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pgl",st:"Field and potential of shells and spheres",q:"If V is the gravitational potential due to sphere of uniform density on it's surface, then its value at the centre of sphere will be",o:["4/3 V","3 V/2","V/2","V"],c:1,s:`On the surface V = \u2212GM/R.
Inside a uniform sphere V = \u2212GM(3R\xB2 \u2212 r\xB2)/(2R\xB3). At the centre r = 0, so V = \u22123GM/2R.
This is (3/2) times the surface value, i.e. 3V/2.`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 15 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pge",st:"Height reached and speed at infinity",q:`A body is released from a height equal to the radius (R) of the earth. The velocity of the body when it strikes the surface will be
(Given g = acceleration due to gravity on the earth)`,o:["\u221A(4gR)","\u221A{gR;2}","\u221A(gR)","\u221A(2gR)"],c:2,s:`The body starts from rest at distance 2R from the centre.
Energy conservation: \xBDv\xB2 = GM/R \u2212 GM/2R = GM/2R.
With GM = gR\xB2: v\xB2 = gR, so v = \u221A(gR).`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 25 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pge",st:"Gravitational potential energy",q:`A body of mass is taken from earth surface to the height h equal to twice the radius of earth (R\u2091), the increase in potential energy will be
(g = acceleration due to gravity on the surface of Earth)`,o:["\xBDmgR\u2091","3mgR\u2091","{2;3}mgR\u2091","{1;3}mgR\u2091"],c:2,s:`\u0394U = GMm(1/R\u2091 \u2212 1/(R\u2091 + h)) = mgh\xB7R\u2091/(R\u2091 + h) using GM = gR\u2091\xB2.
With h = 2R\u2091: \u0394U = mg(2R\u2091)(R\u2091/3R\u2091) = (2/3)mgR\u2091.`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 30 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pgl",st:"Field and potential of shells and spheres",q:`If the gravitational field in the space is given as (\u2212{K;r\xB2}). Taking the reference point to be at r = 2 cm with gravitational potential V = 10 J/kg. Find the gravitational potential at r = 3 cm in SI unit.
(Given, that K = 6 J cm/kg)`,o:["11","10","12","9"],c:0,s:`Field E = \u2212dV/dr, so dV = \u2212E dr = (K/r\xB2)dr.
V(3) \u2212 V(2) = \u222B from 2 to 3 of K/r\xB2 dr = K(1/2 \u2212 1/3) = 6 \xD7 (1/6) = 1 J/kg.
V(3) = 10 + 1 = 11 J/kg.`},{y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 29 Jul \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pge",st:"Gravitational potential energy",q:`An object of mass 1 kg is taken to a height from the surface of earth which is equal to three times the radius of earth. The gain in potential energy of the object will be
[If, g = 10 m s\u207B\xB2 and radius of earth = 6400 km]`,o:["48 MJ","24 MJ","36 MJ","12 MJ"],c:0,s:`\u0394U = GMm(1/R \u2212 1/4R) = (3/4)GMm/R = (3/4)mgR.
= 0.75 \xD7 1 \xD7 10 \xD7 6.4\xD710\u2076 = 4.8 \xD7 10\u2077 J.
This is 48 MJ.`},{y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 27 Jun \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pge",st:"Gravitational potential energy",q:"Four spheres each of mass m form a square of side d (as shown in figure). A fifth sphere of mass M is situated at the centre of square. The total gravitational potential energy of the system is",fig:{w:340,h:210,alt:"A square of side d with a sphere of mass m at each corner and a sphere of mass M at the centre.",d:[["l",110,40,230,40],["l",230,40,230,160],["l",230,160,110,160],["l",110,160,110,40],["c",110,40,6,"F"],["c",230,40,6,"F"],["c",110,160,6,"F"],["c",230,160,6,"F"],["c",170,100,13],["t",170,104,"M","m"],["t",100,34,"m","e"],["t",240,34,"m"],["t",100,176,"m","e"],["t",240,176,"m"],["l",110,190,230,190,"b"],["t",170,205,"d","m"]]},o:["\u2212{Gm;d}[(4 + \u221A2)m + 4\u221A2M]","\u2212{Gm;d}[(4 + \u221A2)M + 4\u221A2m]","\u2212{Gm;d}[3m\xB2 + 4\u221A2M]","\u2212{Gm;d}[6m\xB2 + 4\u221A2M]"],c:0,s:`Add the potential energy of all pairs.
Four sides: 4 \xD7 (\u2212Gm\xB2/d). Two diagonals (length \u221A2 d): 2 \xD7 (\u2212Gm\xB2/(\u221A2 d)) = \u2212\u221A2 Gm\xB2/d.
Centre sphere with four corners (distance d/\u221A2 each): 4 \xD7 (\u2212GMm\u221A2/d) = \u22124\u221A2 GMm/d.
Total U = \u2212(Gm/d)[(4 + \u221A2)m + 4\u221A2 M].`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 26 Aug \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pgl",st:"Field and potential of shells and spheres",q:`Inside a uniform spherical shell
(A) the gravitational field is zero.
(B) the gravitational potential is zero.
(C) the gravitational field is same everywhere.
(D) the gravitation potential is same everywhere.
(E) all of the above`,o:["(A), (C) and (D) only","(A), (B) and (C) only","(B), (C) and (D) only","(E) only"],c:0,s:`Inside a uniform shell the field is zero everywhere (A), so it is the same (zero) everywhere (C).
Zero field means the potential cannot change inside, so V is constant and equal to \u2212GM/R (D).
This constant is not zero, so (B) is false and (E) is false.
Correct choice: (A), (C) and (D) only.`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 27 Aug \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pgl",st:"Field and potential of shells and spheres",q:"A mass of 50 kg is placed at the centre of a uniform spherical shell of mass 100 kg and radius 50 m. If the gravitational potential at a point, 25 m from the centre is V kg/m. The value of V is",o:["+2G","\u221220G","\u22124G","\u221260G"],c:2,s:`The point is inside the shell (25 m < 50 m).
Potential of the shell inside = \u2212GM/R = \u2212G(100)/50 = \u22122G.
Potential of the 50 kg mass at the centre = \u2212G(50)/25 = \u22122G.
Add them: V = \u22122G \u2212 2G = \u22124G.`},{y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 4 Sep \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pgl",st:"Field and potential of shells and spheres",q:"On the x-axis and at a distance x from the origin, the gravitational field due to a mass distribution is given by {Ax;(x\xB2+a\xB2)^(3/2)} in the x-direction. The magnitude of gravitational potential on the x-axis at a distance x, taking its value to be zero at infinity, is",o:["A(x\xB2+a\xB2)^(3/2)","A(x\xB2+a\xB2)^(1/2)","{A;(x\xB2+a\xB2)^(3/2)}","{A;(x\xB2+a\xB2)^(1/2)}"],c:3,s:`Potential at x = work per unit mass to bring a body from infinity to x, so V = \u222B from x to \u221E of E dx.
Put u = x\xB2 + a\xB2, du = 2x dx. Then V = (A/2)\u222B u^(\u22123/2) du.
V = A [\u2212u^(\u22121/2)] taken from x to \u221E = A/(x\xB2 + a\xB2)^(1/2).
So the magnitude is A/(x\xB2+a\xB2)^(1/2).`},{y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 6 Sep \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pgl",st:"Point of zero field and cavities",q:"Two planets have masses M and 16M and their radii are a and 2a, respectively. The separation between the centre of the planets is 10a. A body of mass m is fired from the surface of the larger planet towards the smaller planet along the line joining their centres. For the body to be able to reach at the surface of smaller planet, the minimum firing speed needed is",o:["2\u221A{GM;a}","4\u221A{GM;a}","\u221A{GM\xB2;ma}","{3;2}\u221A{5GM;a}"],c:3,s:`The body must just reach the point N where the two pulls cancel; after that the smaller planet pulls it in.
Let N be x from the big planet: 16M/x\xB2 = M/(10a\u2212x)\xB2, so 4(10a\u2212x) = x, x = 8a.
Potential at the big planet's surface (r = 2a): V\u2081 = \u221216GM/2a \u2212 GM/8a = \u221265GM/8a.
Potential at N: V\u2082 = \u221216GM/8a \u2212 GM/2a = \u22125GM/2a = \u221220GM/8a.
\xBDv\xB2 = V\u2082 \u2212 V\u2081 = 45GM/8a, so v\xB2 = 45GM/4a.
v = (3/2)\u221A(5GM/a).`},{y:"JEE Main 2018 \xB7 Online",tp:"pgs",st:"Energy of a satellite",q:"A body of mass m is moving in a circular orbit of radius R about a planet of mass M. At some instant, it splits into two equal masses. The first mass moves in a circular orbit of radius {R;2}, and the other mass, in a circular orbit of radius {3R;2}. The difference between the final and initial total energies is",o:["+{GMm;6R}","{GMm;2R}","\u2212{GMm;2R}","\u2212{GMm;6R}"],c:3,s:`Energy of a satellite of mass \u03BC in orbit r is \u2212GM\u03BC/(2r).
Initial energy: \u2212GMm/2R.
Final: mass m/2 at R/2 gives \u2212GM(m/2)/(2\xB7R/2) = \u2212GMm/2R; mass m/2 at 3R/2 gives \u2212GM(m/2)/(3R) = \u2212GMm/6R.
Final total = \u2212GMm/2R \u2212 GMm/6R = \u22122GMm/3R.
Difference = \u22122GMm/3R + GMm/2R = \u2212GMm/6R.`},{y:"JEE Main 2015",tp:"pgl",st:"Point of zero field and cavities",q:`From a solid sphere of mass M and radius R, a spherical portion of radius {R;2} is removed, as shown in the figure. Taking gravitational potential V = 0 at r = \u221E, the potential at the centre of the cavity thus formed is
(G = gravitational constant)`,fig:{w:340,h:200,alt:"Solid sphere of radius R with a spherical cavity of radius R/2 touching its surface, the cavity centre lying R/2 from the sphere centre O",d:[["c",150,100,80],["c",190,100,40,"d"],["c",150,100,2.5,"F"],["c",190,100,2.5,"F"],["l",150,100,190,100],["t",150,118,"O","ms"],["t",190,118,"C","ms"],["t",170,94,"R/2","ms"],["t",190,72,"cavity","ms"],["t",190,86,"radius R/2","ms"],["l",150,100,93.4,43.4],["t",108,62,"R","s"],["t",90,140,"M","m"]]},o:["{\u22122GM;3R}","{\u22122GM;R}","{\u2212GM;2R}","{\u2212GM;R}"],c:3,s:`Use superposition: (full sphere) minus (removed sphere).
The cavity centre is R/2 from O, inside the full sphere: V\u2081 = \u2212GM(3R\xB2 \u2212 r\xB2)/(2R\xB3) with r = R/2, which gives \u221211GM/8R.
The removed sphere has mass M/8 and radius R/2; at its own centre V\u2082 = \u2212(3/2)\xB7G(M/8)/(R/2) = \u22123GM/8R.
Potential with cavity = V\u2081 \u2212 V\u2082 = \u221211GM/8R + 3GM/8R = \u2212GM/R.`},{y:"JEE Main 2015 \xB7 Online",tp:"pgl",st:"Field and potential of shells and spheres",q:"Which of the following most closely depicts the correct variation of the gravitational potential V(r) due to a large planet of radius R and uniform mass density? (Figures are not drawn to scale)",fig:{w:340,h:256,alt:"Four small graphs of V against r. (a) positive V rising linearly then falling; (b) negative V starting flat at its lowest value and rising smoothly to zero; (c) negative V constant up to R and then rising to zero; (d) positive V falling smoothly from a flat start to zero.",d:[["l",30,106,30,10,"a"],["t",36,18,"V(r)","s"],["l",30,84,148,84,"a"],["t",144,96,"r","s"],["t",26,96,"O","es"],["p","M30 84 L80 24 Q90 70 145 79"],["t",90,121,"(a)","m"],["l",200,106,200,10,"a"],["t",206,18,"V(r)","s"],["l",200,34,318,34,"a"],["t",314,30,"r","s"],["t",196,30,"O","es"],["p","M200 94 C225 92 235 40 315 37"],["t",260,121,"(b)","m"],["l",30,232,30,136,"a"],["t",36,144,"V(r)","s"],["l",30,160,148,160,"a"],["t",144,156,"r","s"],["t",26,156,"O","es"],["p","M30 220 H62 Q68 168 145 164"],["t",90,247,"(c)","m"],["l",200,232,200,136,"a"],["t",206,144,"V(r)","s"],["l",200,210,318,210,"a"],["t",314,222,"r","s"],["t",196,222,"O","es"],["p","M200 150 C235 148 250 202 315 206"],["t",260,247,"(d)","m"]]},o:["Graph (a)","Graph (b)","Graph (c)","Graph (d)"],c:1,s:`Outside the planet V = \u2212GM/r: negative and rising towards zero at infinity.
Inside a uniform sphere V = \u2212GM(3R\xB2 \u2212 r\xB2)/(2R\xB3): still negative, lowest at the centre, with zero slope there (g = 0 at the centre).
So V is always negative, smooth, flat at r = 0 and rising continuously to zero, which is graph (b).
Graph (c) shows a constant V inside (that is a hollow shell), and (a), (d) have positive V, which is impossible.`},{y:"JEE Main 2014 \xB7 Online",tp:"pge",st:"Gravitational potential energy",q:`Two hypothetical planets of masses m\u2081 and m\u2082 are at rest when they are infinite distance apart. Because of the gravitational force they move towards each other along the line joining their centres. What is their speed when their separation is d ?
(Speed of m\u2081 is v\u2081 and that of m\u2082 is v\u2082)`,fig:{w:340,h:115,alt:"Two spheres m1 and m2 a distance d apart moving towards each other with speeds v1 and v2",d:[["c",50,50,20,"f"],["c",290,50,20,"f"],["t",50,20,"m\u2081","ms"],["t",290,20,"m\u2082","ms"],["l",72,42,140,42,"a"],["t",150,38,"v\u2081","m"],["l",268,42,200,42,"a"],["t",190,38,"v\u2082","m"],["l",50,88,290,88,"b"],["t",170,106,"d","m"]]},o:["v\u2081 = v\u2082","v\u2081 = m\u2082\u221A{2G;d(m\u2081+m\u2082)} , v\u2082 = m\u2081\u221A{2G;d(m\u2081+m\u2082)}","v\u2081 = m\u2081\u221A{2G;d(m\u2081+m\u2082)} , v\u2082 = m\u2082\u221A{2G;d(m\u2081+m\u2082)}","v\u2081 = m\u2082\u221A{2G;m\u2081} , v\u2082 = m\u2081\u221A{2G;m\u2082}"],c:1,s:`Momentum is conserved (start at rest): m\u2081v\u2081 = m\u2082v\u2082, so v\u2081 = (m\u2082/m\u2081)v\u2082.
Energy is conserved: \xBDm\u2081v\u2081\xB2 + \xBDm\u2082v\u2082\xB2 = Gm\u2081m\u2082/d (initial energy is 0 at infinity).
Substitute v\u2082 = (m\u2081/m\u2082)v\u2081: \xBDm\u2081v\u2081\xB2(m\u2081+m\u2082)/m\u2082 = Gm\u2081m\u2082/d, so v\u2081\xB2 = 2Gm\u2082\xB2/[d(m\u2081+m\u2082)].
Hence v\u2081 = m\u2082\u221A(2G/[d(m\u2081+m\u2082)]) and, in the same way, v\u2082 = m\u2081\u221A(2G/[d(m\u2081+m\u2082)]).`},{y:"JEE Main 2013 \xB7 Online",tp:"pgl",st:"Field and potential of shells and spheres",q:`The gravitational field in a region is given by
E\u20D7 = (5 N/kg) \xEE + (12 N/kg) \u0135
If the potential at the origin is taken to be zero, then the ratio of the potential at the points (12 m, 0) and (0, 5 m) is`,o:["zero","1","{144;25}","{25;144}"],c:1,s:`For a uniform field, V = \u2212E\u20D7\xB7r\u20D7 + constant, and V = 0 at the origin, so V = \u2212(5x + 12y).
At (12 m, 0): V = \u22125 \xD7 12 = \u221260 J/kg.
At (0, 5 m): V = \u221212 \xD7 5 = \u221260 J/kg.
Ratio = (\u221260)/(\u221260) = 1.`},{y:"AIEEE 2012",tp:"pge",st:"Escape speed",q:"The mass of a spaceship is 1000 kg. It is to be launched from the earth's surface out into free space. The value of g and R (radius of earth) are 10 m/s\xB2 and 6400 km respectively. The required energy for this work will be",o:["6.4 \xD7 10\u2078 Joules","6.4 \xD7 10\u2079 Joules","6.4 \xD7 10\xB9\u2070 Joules","6.4 \xD7 10\xB9\xB9 Joules"],c:2,s:`Minimum energy needed = GMm/R = mgR (since GM = gR\xB2).
E = 1000 \xD7 10 \xD7 6.4 \xD7 10\u2076 = 6.4 \xD7 10\xB9\u2070 J.`},{y:"AIEEE 2011",tp:"pgl",st:"Point of zero field and cavities",q:"Two bodies of masses m and 4m are placed at a distance r. The gravitational potential at a point on the line joining them where the gravitational field is zero is",o:["zero","\u2212{4Gm;r}","\u2212{6Gm;r}","\u2212{9Gm;r}"],c:3,s:`Let the zero-field point be x from m: Gm/x\xB2 = G\xB74m/(r\u2212x)\xB2, so r \u2212 x = 2x and x = r/3.
It is r/3 from m and 2r/3 from 4m.
V = \u2212Gm/(r/3) \u2212 G(4m)/(2r/3) = \u22123Gm/r \u2212 6Gm/r = \u22129Gm/r.`},{y:"IIT-JEE 2010",tp:"pgl",st:"Field and potential of shells and spheres",q:"A thin uniform annular disc (see figure) of mass M has outer radius 4R and inner radius 3R. The work required to take a unit mass from point P on its axis to infinity is",fig:{w:340,h:195,alt:"Annular disc of inner radius 3R and outer radius 4R with point P on its axis at a height 4R above the centre",d:[["p","M70 150 A100 30 0 1 0 270 150 A100 30 0 1 0 70 150 Z","f"],["p","M95 150 A75 22 0 1 0 245 150 A75 22 0 1 0 95 150 Z"],["l",170,150,170,10,"a"],["c",170,40,3,"F"],["t",180,44,"P",""],["l",170,150,245,150],["l",245,150,270,150,"d"],["t",207,144,"3R","ms"],["t",260,144,"4R","ms"],["t",178,100,"4R","s"],["c",170,150,2.5,"F"]]},o:["{2GM;7R}(4\u221A2\u22125)","\u2212{2GM;7R}(4\u221A2\u22125)","{GM;4R}","{2GM;5R}(\u221A2\u22121)"],c:0,s:`Surface density \u03C3 = M/[\u03C0(16R\xB2 \u2212 9R\xB2)] = M/(7\u03C0R\xB2).
For a ring-shaped disc, the potential on the axis at distance x is V = \u22122\u03C0G\u03C3[\u221A(x\xB2+R_out\xB2) \u2212 \u221A(x\xB2+R_in\xB2)].
At x = 4R: V = \u22122\u03C0G\u03C3 R(\u221A32 \u2212 \u221A25) = \u22122\u03C0G\u03C3R(4\u221A2 \u2212 5) = \u2212(2GM/7R)(4\u221A2 \u2212 5).
Work to take unit mass to infinity = 0 \u2212 V = (2GM/7R)(4\u221A2 \u2212 5).`},{y:"AIEEE 2005",tp:"pge",st:"Gravitational potential energy",q:`A particle of mass 10 g is kept on the surface of a uniform sphere of mass 100 kg and radius 10 cm. Find the work to be done against the gravitational force between them to take the particle far away from the sphere.
(you may take G = 6.67 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\xB9 N m\xB2/kg\xB2)`,o:["6.67 \xD7 10\u207B\u2079 J","6.67 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2070 J","13.34 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2070 J","3.33 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2070 J"],c:1,s:`Work needed = \u2212(potential energy on the surface) = GMm/R.
W = 6.67 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\xB9 \xD7 100 \xD7 0.010 / 0.10 (m = 10 g = 0.010 kg, R = 0.10 m).
W = 6.67 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2070 J.`},{y:"AIEEE 2004",tp:"pge",st:"Gravitational potential energy",q:"If g is the acceleration due to gravity on the earth's surface, the gain in the potential energy of an object of mass m raised from the surface of the earth to a height equal to the radius R of the earth is",o:["2mgR","{1;2}mgR","{1;4}mgR","mgR."],c:1,s:`\u0394U = GMm(1/R \u2212 1/2R) = GMm/2R.
Use GM = gR\xB2: \u0394U = mgR/2.`},{y:"AIEEE 2002",tp:"pge",st:"Gravitational potential energy",q:"Energy required to move a body of mass m from an orbit of radius 2R to 3R is",o:["GMm/12R\xB2","GMm/3R\xB2","GMm/8R","GMm/6R"],c:3,s:`Potential energy at distance r is U = \u2212GMm/r.
Taking the energy required as the gain in potential energy: \u0394U = \u2212GMm/3R + GMm/2R = GMm/6R.
This matches option (d), the official answer.`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1983",tp:"pge",st:"Gravitational potential energy",q:"If g is the acceleration due to gravity on the earth's surface, the gain in the potential energy of an object of mass m raised from the surface of the earth to a height equal to the radius of the earth, is",o:["{1;2}mgR","2mgR","mgR","{1;4}mgR"],c:0,s:`Gain in PE = GMm(1/R \u2212 1/2R) = GMm/2R.
With GM = gR\xB2, this is \xBDmgR.`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1993",tp:"pgl",st:"Point of zero field and cavities",q:`A solid sphere of uniform density and radius 4 units is located with its centre at the origin O of coordinates. Two spheres of equal radii 1 unit, with their centres at A(\u22122, 0, 0) and B(2, 0, 0) respectively, are taken out of the solid leaving behind spherical cavities as shown in figure.
Then:
(a) the gravitational force due to this object at the origin is zero.
(b) the gravitational force at the point B(2, 0, 0) is zero.
(c) the gravitational potential is the same at all points of circle y\xB2 + z\xB2 = 36.
(d) the gravitational potential is the same at all points on the circle y\xB2 + z\xB2 = 4.`,fig:{w:340,h:205,alt:"Solid sphere of radius 4 centred at the origin O with two spherical cavities of radius 1 centred at A(-2,0,0) and B(2,0,0) on the x-axis; y points up, x to the right and z towards the viewer",d:[["c",170,105,85],["c",128,105,21,"d"],["c",212,105,21,"d"],["l",65,105,290,105,"a"],["t",294,109,"x"],["l",170,105,170,8,"a"],["t",177,16,"y"],["l",170,105,115,160,"a"],["t",104,172,"z"],["c",170,105,2.5,"F"],["c",128,105,2.5,"F"],["c",212,105,2.5,"F"],["t",128,138,"A","m"],["t",212,138,"B","m"],["t",176,120,"O","s"]]},o:["The gravitational force due to this object at the origin is zero","The gravitational force at the point B(2, 0, 0) is zero","The gravitational potential is the same at all points of circle y\xB2 + z\xB2 = 36","The gravitational potential is the same at all points on the circle y\xB2 + z\xB2 = 4"],c:[0,2,3],s:`(a) True. The two cavities are equal and symmetric about O, so the missing pulls cancel; the full sphere gives zero field at its centre.
(b) False. At B the full sphere pulls towards O with strength G\u03C1(4\u03C0/3)\xB72, while removing the left sphere adds a push of G\u03C1(4\u03C0/3)/16 in the opposite sense. They do not cancel.
(c), (d) True. The cavities lie on the x-axis, so the body is symmetric about the x-axis. Every point of a circle y\xB2+z\xB2 = constant (centred on the x-axis) is equivalent, so the potential is the same on it.
Correct: (a), (c), (d).`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 27 Aug \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pge",st:"Gravitational potential energy",q:"A body of mass (2M) splits into four masses {m, M \u2212 m, m, M \u2212 m}, which are rearranged to form a square as shown in the figure. The ratio of {M;m} for which, the gravitational potential energy of the system becomes maximum is x : 1. The value of x is ______.",fig:{w:340,h:190,alt:"Square of side d with masses m at top-left and bottom-right and M minus m at top-right and bottom-left",d:[["r",120,30,100,100],["c",120,30,3,"F"],["c",220,30,3,"F"],["c",120,130,3,"F"],["c",220,130,3,"F"],["t",120,22,"m","m"],["t",220,22,"M \u2212 m","m"],["t",120,148,"M \u2212 m","m"],["t",220,148,"m","m"],["l",98,32,98,128,"b"],["t",92,84,"d","e"],["l",122,166,218,166,"b"],["t",170,183,"d","m"]]},n:2,s:`Each side joins m and (M\u2212m); each diagonal joins m with m, or (M\u2212m) with (M\u2212m).
|U| = (G/d)[4m(M\u2212m) + (m\xB2 + (M\u2212m)\xB2)/\u221A2].
d|U|/dm = 4M \u2212 8m + \u221A2(2m \u2212 M) = (4 \u2212 \u221A2)(M \u2212 2m).
This is zero when M = 2m, so M/m = 2, i.e. x = 2.
The second derivative is \u22128 + 2\u221A2 < 0, so the magnitude of the potential energy is greatest here (U itself is most negative). The question's 'maximum' is meant in this sense of magnitude, which is the official answer x = 2.`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 27 Jul \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pge",st:"Height reached and speed at infinity",q:`Suppose two planets (spherical in shape) of radii R and 2R, but mass M and 9M respectively have a centre to centre separation 8R as shown in the figure. A satellite of mass m is projected from the surface of the planet of mass M directly towards the centre of the second planet. The minimum speed v required for the satellite to reach the surface of the second planet is \u221A({a;7} {GM;R}) then the value of a is ______.
[Given : The two planets are fixed in their position]`,fig:{w:340,h:185,alt:"Two fixed spherical planets: a small one of radius R and mass M and a large one of radius 2R and mass 9M, with centres 8R apart",d:[["c",60,80,24],["c",60,80,2.5,"F"],["l",60,80,77,63],["t",80,60,"R","s"],["t",60,100,"M","m"],["c",230,80,48],["c",230,80,2.5,"F"],["l",230,80,264,46],["t",268,44,"2R","s"],["t",230,102,"9M","m"],["l",60,128,60,152,"d"],["l",230,128,230,152,"d"],["l",60,148,230,148,"b"],["t",145,168,"8R","m"]]},n:4,s:`The satellite only has to reach the point N where the net pull is zero; beyond N the bigger planet pulls it on.
Let N be x from the centre of M: M/x\xB2 = 9M/(8R\u2212x)\xB2, so 8R \u2212 x = 3x and x = 2R.
V at the surface of M (r = R): \u2212GM/R \u2212 9GM/7R = \u221216GM/7R. V at N: \u2212GM/2R \u2212 9GM/6R = \u22122GM/R.
\xBDv\xB2 = \u22122GM/R + 16GM/7R = 2GM/7R, so v\xB2 = 4GM/7R = (a/7)(GM/R).
Therefore a = 4.`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1996",tp:"pgl",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:"Distance between the centres of two stars is 10a. The masses of these stars are M and 16M and their radii a and 2a, respectively. A body of mass m is fired straight from the surface of the larger star towards the smaller star. What should be its minimum initial speed to reach the surface of the smaller star? Obtain the expression in terms of G, M and a.",a:"v = (3/2)\u221A(5GM/a)",s:`The body must just reach the neutral point N, where the two pulls cancel. After N the smaller star pulls it in.
At N (x from the big star): 16M/x\xB2 = M/(10a\u2212x)\xB2, so x = 8a (and 2a from the small star's centre).
Potential at the big star's surface: \u221216GM/2a \u2212 GM/8a = \u221265GM/8a. Potential at N: \u221216GM/8a \u2212 GM/2a = \u221220GM/8a.
Energy conservation: \xBDmv\xB2 = m(\u221220GM/8a + 65GM/8a) = 45GMm/8a.
v\xB2 = 45GM/4a, so v = (3/2)\u221A(5GM/a).`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1997",tp:"pge",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:"A particle is projected vertically upwards from the surface of earth (radius R\u2091) with a kinetic energy equal to half of the minimum value needed for it to escape. The height to which it rises above the surface of earth is ______.",a:"h = R\u2091",s:`Minimum KE to escape = GMm/R\u2091, so the given KE = GMm/2R\u2091.
Energy at the surface: GMm/2R\u2091 \u2212 GMm/R\u2091 = \u2212GMm/2R\u2091.
At the highest point (distance r from the centre) the speed is zero: \u2212GMm/r = \u2212GMm/2R\u2091, so r = 2R\u2091.
Height above the surface h = r \u2212 R\u2091 = R\u2091.`},{y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 4 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pge",st:"Escape speed",q:`An object is kept at rest at a distance of 3R above the earth's surface where R is earth's radius. The minimum speed with which it must be projected so that it does not return to earth is
(Assume M = mass of earth, G = Universal gravitational constant)`,o:["\u221A{GM;R}","\u221A{3GM;R}","\u221A{GM;2R}","\u221A{2GM;R}"],c:2,s:`The object is at distance r = R + 3R = 4R from the centre of the earth.
To not return it needs the escape speed from there: v = \u221A(2GM/r).
v = \u221A(2GM/4R) = \u221A(GM/2R).`},{y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 28 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pge",st:"Escape speed",q:"Earth has mass 8 times and radius 2 times that of a planet. If the escape velocity from the earth is 11.2 km/s, the escape velocity in km/s from the planet will be",o:["11.2","8.4","5.6","2.8"],c:2,s:`Escape speed v = \u221A(2GM/R), so v \u221D \u221A(M/R).
v_earth/v_planet = \u221A(8/2) = 2.
v_planet = 11.2/2 = 5.6 km/s.`},{y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 4 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pgs",st:"Orbital speed and period",q:"Correct formula for height of a satellite from earth's surface is",o:["({T\xB2R\xB2g;4\u03C0\xB2})^(1/3) \u2212 R","({T\xB2R\xB2;4\u03C0\xB2g})^(1/3) \u2212 R","({T\xB2R\xB2g;4\u03C0\xB2})^(\u22121/3) + R","({T\xB2R\xB2g;4\u03C0})^(1/2) \u2212 R"],c:0,s:`For a satellite at radius r = R + h, T = 2\u03C0\u221A(r\xB3/GM), and GM = gR\xB2.
Square: T\xB2 = 4\u03C0\xB2(R+h)\xB3/(gR\xB2), so (R+h)\xB3 = T\xB2R\xB2g/4\u03C0\xB2.
Then R + h = (T\xB2R\xB2g/4\u03C0\xB2)^(1/3), so h = (T\xB2R\xB2g/4\u03C0\xB2)^(1/3) \u2212 R.`},{y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 5 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pgs",st:"Geostationary and comparing satellites",q:`A satellite revolving around a planet in stationary orbit has time period 6 hours. The mass of planet is one-fourth the mass of earth. The radius orbit of planet is
(Given : Radius of geo-stationary orbit for earth is 4.2 \xD7 10\u2074 km)`,o:["1.68 \xD7 10\u2075 km","1.05 \xD7 10\u2074 km","8.4 \xD7 10\u2074 km","1.4 \xD7 10\u2074 km"],c:1,s:`From T\xB2 = 4\u03C0\xB2r\xB3/GM, r\xB3 \u221D M\xB7T\xB2.
(r/4.2\xD710\u2074)\xB3 = (1/4) \xD7 (6/24)\xB2 = (1/4)(1/16) = 1/64.
r/4.2\xD710\u2074 = 1/4, so r = 1.05 \xD7 10\u2074 km.`},{y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 6 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pge",st:"Escape speed",q:"To project a body of mass m from earth's surface to infinity, the required kinetic energy is (assume, the radius of earth is R_E, g = acceleration due to gravity on the surface of earth)",o:["mgR_E","4mgR_E","{1;2}mgR_E","2mgR_E"],c:0,s:`Required KE = GMm/R_E (so that total energy is zero).
With GM = gR_E\xB2, KE = mgR_E.`},{y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 8 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pgs",st:"Orbital speed and period",q:"Two satellite A and B go round a planet in circular orbits having radii 4R and R respectively. If the speed of A is 3v, the speed of B will be",o:["12v","{4;3}v","6v","3v"],c:2,s:`Orbital speed v = \u221A(GM/r), so v \u221D 1/\u221Ar.
v_B/v_A = \u221A(r_A/r_B) = \u221A(4R/R) = 2.
v_B = 2 \xD7 3v = 6v.`},{y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 9 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pgs",st:"Energy of a satellite",q:`A satellite of 10\xB3 kg mass is revolving in circular orbit of radius 2R. If {10\u2074R;6}J energy is supplied to the satellite, it would revolve in a new circular orbit of radius
(Use g = 10 m/s\xB2, R = radius of earth)`,o:["6R","2.5R","3R","4R"],c:0,s:`Total energy of a satellite E = \u2212GMm/2r = \u2212gR\xB2m/2r.
Initial: E\u2081 = \u2212(10)(R\xB2)(10\xB3)/(2 \xD7 2R) = \u22122500R J.
After adding 10\u2074R/6 J: E\u2082 = \u22122500R + 1666.7R = \u2212833.3R J.
Set \u221210\u2074R\xB2/(2r\u2032) = \u2212833.3R, so r\u2032 = 10\u2074R/1666.7 = 6R.`},{y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 30 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pge",st:"Escape speed",q:"Escape velocity of a body from earth is 11.2 km/s. If the radius of a planet be one-third the radius of earth and mass be one-sixth that of earth, the escape velocity from the planet is",o:["8.4 km/s","4.2 km/s","7.9 km/s","11.2 km/s"],c:2,s:`v \u221D \u221A(M/R).
v_planet/v_earth = \u221A[(1/6)/(1/3)] = \u221A(1/2) = 0.707.
v_planet = 11.2 \xD7 0.707 \u2248 7.9 km/s.`},{y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 31 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pge",st:"Escape speed",q:"The mass of the moon is {1;144} times the mass of a planet and its diameter is {1;16} times the diameter of a planet. If the escape velocity on the planet is v, the escape velocity on the moon will be",o:["{v;4}","{v;12}","{v;3}","{v;6}"],c:2,s:`v \u221D \u221A(M/R), and the ratio of diameters equals the ratio of radii.
v_moon/v = \u221A[(1/144)/(1/16)] = \u221A(16/144) = 4/12 = 1/3.
So the escape velocity on the moon is v/3.`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 6 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pgk",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:"Choose the incorrect statement from the following.",o:["The linear speed of a planet revolving around the sun remains constant.","The speed of satellite in a given circular orbit remains constant.","When a body falls towards earth, the displacement of earth towards the body is negligible.","For a planet revolving around the sun in an elliptical orbit, the total energy of the planet remains constant."],c:0,s:`(a) is incorrect: a planet moves in an ellipse, so by Kepler's second law it moves faster near the sun and slower far away; its speed is not constant.
(b) is correct: in a given circular orbit v = \u221A(GM/r) is fixed.
(c) is correct: the earth is so massive that its acceleration towards the body is negligible.
(d) is correct: the total energy of a planet in orbit is conserved.
So the incorrect statement is (a).`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 8 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pgs",st:"Orbital speed and period",q:"The orbital angular momentum of a satellite is L, when it is revolving in a circular orbit at height h from earth surface. If the distance of satellite from the earth centre is increased by eight times to its initial value, then the new angular momentum will be",o:["8L","4L","3L","9L"],c:2,s:`Angular momentum of a circular orbit: L = mvr = m\u221A(GMr), so L \u221D \u221Ar.
Increased by eight times to its initial value: new r = r + 8r = 9r.
L\u2032/L = \u221A9 = 3, so L\u2032 = 3L.`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 10 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pgs",st:"Orbital speed and period",q:"Two satellites of masses m and 3m revolve around the earth in circular orbits of radii r and 3r respectively. The ratio of orbital speeds of the satellites respectively is",o:["9 : 1","\u221A3 : 1","3 : 1","1 : 1"],c:1,s:`Orbital speed v = \u221A(GM/r) does not depend on the satellite's mass.
v\u2081/v\u2082 = \u221A(3r/r) = \u221A3.
The ratio is \u221A3 : 1.`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 10 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pgs",st:"Orbital speed and period",q:`The time period of a satellite, revolving above earth's surface at a height equal to R will be
(Given g = \u03C0\xB2 m/s\xB2, R = radius of earth)`,o:["\u221A(2R)","\u221A(32R)","\u221A(4R)","\u221A(8R)"],c:1,s:`The orbit radius is r = R + R = 2R.
T = 2\u03C0\u221A(r\xB3/GM) = 2\u03C0\u221A(8R\xB3/gR\xB2) = 2\u03C0\u221A(8R/g).
With g = \u03C0\xB2: T = 2\u03C0\u221A(8R)/\u03C0 = 2\u221A(8R) = \u221A(32R) (in seconds, R in metres).`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 11 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pge",st:"Escape speed",q:"A spaceship of mass 2 \xD7 10\u2074 kg is launched into a circular orbit close to the earth surface. The additional velocity to be imparted to the spaceship in the orbit to overcome the gravitational pull will be (if g = 10 m/s\xB2 and radius of earth = 6400 km)",o:["8(\u221A2 \u2212 1) km/s","7.9(\u221A2 \u2212 1) km/s","7.4(\u221A2 \u2212 1) km/s","11.2(\u221A2 \u2212 1) km/s"],c:0,s:`Orbital speed close to the earth: v\u2080 = \u221A(gR) = \u221A(10 \xD7 6.4\xD710\u2076) = 8000 m/s = 8 km/s.
Escape speed = \u221A2 v\u2080.
Additional velocity = \u221A2 v\u2080 \u2212 v\u2080 = 8(\u221A2 \u2212 1) km/s.`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 12 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pge",st:"Escape speed",q:`The ratio of escape velocity of a planet to the escape velocity of earth will be
(Given : Mass of the planet is 16 times mass of earth and radius of the planet is 4 times the radius of earth)`,o:["1 : \u221A2","1 : 4","4 : 1","2 : 1"],c:3,s:`v_e = \u221A(2GM/R), so v \u221D \u221A(M/R).
v_planet/v_earth = \u221A(16/4) = \u221A4 = 2.
So the ratio is 2 : 1.`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 12 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pgs",st:"Geostationary and comparing satellites",q:"Two satellites A and B move round the earth in the same orbit. The mass of A is twice the mass of B. The quantity which is same for the two satellites will be",o:["speed","potential energy","total energy","kinetic energy"],c:0,s:`Orbital speed v = \u221A(GM/r) depends only on the orbit radius, not on the satellite's mass.
Potential energy, total energy and kinetic energy all contain the mass of the satellite, so they differ (A has twice the value).
The quantity that is the same is the speed.`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 13 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pge",st:"Escape speed",q:`A planet having mass 9 M\u2091 and radius 4R\u2091, where M\u2091 and R\u2091 are mass and radius of earth respectively, has escape velocity in km/s given by
(Given escape velocity on earth V\u2091 = 11.2 \xD7 10\xB3 m/s)`,o:["11.2","16.8","67.2","33.6"],c:1,s:`v \u221D \u221A(M/R).
v_planet/V\u2091 = \u221A(9/4) = 3/2.
v_planet = 1.5 \xD7 11.2 = 16.8 km/s.`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 1 Feb \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pge",st:"Escape speed",q:"If earth has a mass nine times and radius twice to that of a planet P. Then {v\u2091;3}\u221Ax m s\u207B\xB9 will be the minimum velocity required by a rocket to pull out of gravitational force of P, where v\u2091 is escape velocity on earth. The value of x is",o:["1","18","3","2"],c:3,s:`The minimum velocity to pull out of P's gravity is the escape velocity of P.
v_P/v\u2091 = \u221A[(M_P/R_P)/(M_E/R_E)] = \u221A[(1/9)/(1/2)] = \u221A(2/9) = \u221A2/3.
So v_P = (v\u2091/3)\u221A2 m/s, giving x = 2.`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 1 Feb \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pgg",st:"g on planets",q:"The escape velocities of two planets A and B are in the ratio 1 : 2. If the ratio of their radii respectively is 1 : 3, then the ratio of acceleration due to gravity of planet A to the acceleration of gravity of planet B will be",o:["{3;2}","{2;3}","{3;4}","{4;3}"],c:2,s:`Escape speed v\xB2 = 2gR, so g = v\xB2/2R.
g_A/g_B = (v_A/v_B)\xB2 \xD7 (R_B/R_A) = (1/2)\xB2 \xD7 3 = 3/4.`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 29 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pgs",st:"Geostationary and comparing satellites",q:"The time period of a satellite of earth is 24 hours. If the separation between the earth and the satellite is decreased to one fourth of the previous value, then its new time period will become",o:["4 hours","12 hours","3 hours","6 hours"],c:2,s:`Kepler's third law: T\xB2 \u221D r\xB3, so T \u221D r^(3/2).
r becomes r/4, so T becomes T \xD7 (1/4)^(3/2) = T/8.
New period = 24/8 = 3 hours.
Answer: (c) 3 hours.`},{y:"JEE Advanced 2023",tp:"pgg",st:"g with height and depth",q:"Two satellites P and Q are moving in different circular orbits around the Earth (radius R). The heights of P and Q from the Earth surface are h_P and h_Q, respectively, where h_P = R/3. The accelerations of P and Q due to Earth's gravity are g_P and g_Q, respectively. If g_P/g_Q = 36/25, what is the value of h_Q?",o:["3R/5","R/6","6R/5","5R/6"],c:0,s:`At distance r from the centre, g = GM/r\xB2, so g_P/g_Q = (r_Q/r_P)\xB2.
r_P = R + R/3 = 4R/3.
(r_Q/r_P)\xB2 = 36/25, so r_Q/r_P = 6/5 and r_Q = (6/5)(4R/3) = 8R/5.
h_Q = r_Q \u2212 R = 8R/5 \u2212 R = 3R/5.
Answer: (a) 3R/5.`},{y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 26 Jul \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pge",st:"Height reached and speed at infinity",q:"A body is projected vertically upwards from the surface of earth with a velocity equal to one third of escape velocity. The maximum height attained by the body will be (Take radius of earth = 6400 km and g = 10 m s\u207B\xB2)",o:["800 km","1600 km","2133 km","4800 km"],c:0,s:`Escape speed v_e\xB2 = 2gR, so v\xB2 = v_e\xB2/9 = 2gR/9.
Energy conservation: \xBDv\xB2 = GM/R \u2212 GM/(R+h) = gR \u2212 gR\xB2/(R+h).
\xBD(2gR/9) = gR/9, so gR/9 = gR \u2212 gR\xB2/(R+h).
This gives gR\xB2/(R+h) = 8gR/9, so R + h = 9R/8 and h = R/8.
h = 6400/8 = 800 km.
Answer: (a) 800 km.`},{y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 27 Jul \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pgs",st:"Energy of a satellite",q:"Two satellites A and B having masses in the ratio 4 : 3, are revolving in circular orbits of radii 3r and 4r respectively around the earth. The ratio of total mechanical energy of A to B is",o:["9 : 16","16 : 9","1 : 1","4 : 3"],c:1,s:`Total energy of a circular orbit: E = \u2212GMm/(2r), so |E| \u221D m/r.
For A: m/r = 4/3. For B: m/r = 3/4.
E_A : E_B = (4/3) : (3/4) = 16 : 9.
Answer: (b) 16 : 9.`},{y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 27 Jul \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pge",st:"Height reached and speed at infinity",q:"A body of mass m is projected with velocity \u03BBv\u2091 in vertically upward direction from the surface of the earth into space. It is given that v\u2091 is escape velocity and \u03BB < 1. If air resistance is considered to be negligible, then the maximum height from the centre of earth, to which the body can go, will be (R : radius of earth)",o:["{R;1+\u03BB\xB2}","{R;1\u2212\u03BB\xB2}","{R;1\u2212\u03BB}","{\u03BB\xB2R;1\u2212\u03BB\xB2}"],c:1,s:`Energy conservation from the surface to the highest point r (speed zero):
\xBDm(\u03BBv\u2091)\xB2 \u2212 GMm/R = \u2212GMm/r.
Use v\u2091\xB2 = 2GM/R: \u03BB\xB2GMm/R \u2212 GMm/R = \u2212GMm/r.
So 1/r = (1 \u2212 \u03BB\xB2)/R, hence r = R/(1 \u2212 \u03BB\xB2).
Answer: (b).`},{y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 29 Jun \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pge",st:"Escape speed",q:"The escape velocity of a body on a planet 'A' is 12 km s\u207B\xB9. The escape velocity of the body on another planet 'B', whose density is four times and radius is half of the planet 'A', is",o:["12 km s\u207B\xB9","24 km s\u207B\xB9","36 km s\u207B\xB9","6 km s\u207B\xB9"],c:0,s:`v\u2091 = \u221A(2GM/R) and M = (4/3)\u03C0R\xB3\u03C1, so v\u2091 = R\u221A(8\u03C0G\u03C1/3) \u221D R\u221A\u03C1.
For B: R is halved and \u03C1 is multiplied by 4, so \u221A\u03C1 doubles.
v\u2091(B)/v\u2091(A) = (1/2)(2) = 1.
So v\u2091(B) = 12 km s\u207B\xB9.
Answer: (a) 12 km s\u207B\xB9.`},{y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 29 Jun \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pgs",st:"Geostationary and comparing satellites",q:"The time period of a satellite revolving around earth in a given orbit is 7 hours. If the radius of orbit is increased to three times its previous value, then approximate new time period of the satellite will be",o:["40 hrs.","36 hrs.","30 hrs.","25 hrs."],c:1,s:`T \u221D r^(3/2) (Kepler's third law).
r becomes 3r, so T becomes T \xD7 3^(3/2) = 5.2 T.
New period = 7 \xD7 5.2 \u2248 36.4 hours.
The closest option is 36 hrs.
Answer: (b).`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 31 Aug \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pge",st:"Escape speed",q:"The masses and radii of the earth and moon are (M\u2081, R\u2081) and (M\u2082, R\u2082) respectively. Their centres are at a distance r apart. Find the minimum escape velocity for a particle of mass m to be projected from the middle of these two masses.",o:["\u221A{4G(M\u2081+M\u2082);r}","{1;2}\u221A{2G(M\u2081+M\u2082);r}","{1;2}\u221A{4G(M\u2081+M\u2082);r}","{\u221A(2G)(M\u2081+M\u2082);r}"],c:0,s:`The midpoint is at distance r/2 from each mass.
Potential energy there: U = \u2212GM\u2081m/(r/2) \u2212 GM\u2082m/(r/2) = \u22122G(M\u2081+M\u2082)m/r.
To escape, \xBDmv\xB2 = 2G(M\u2081+M\u2082)m/r (total energy zero at infinity).
So v\xB2 = 4G(M\u2081+M\u2082)/r and v = \u221A{4G(M\u2081+M\u2082)/r}.
Answer: (a).`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 20 Jul \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pgs",st:"Geostationary and comparing satellites",q:"A satellite is launched into a circular orbit of radius R around earth, while a second satellite is launched into a circular orbit of radius 1.02 R. The percentage difference in the time period of the two satellites is",o:["1.5","3.0","0.7","2.0"],c:1,s:`T \u221D r^(3/2), so T\u2082/T\u2081 = (1.02)^(3/2).
Use the binomial approximation: (1 + 0.02)^(3/2) \u2248 1 + 1.5 \xD7 0.02 = 1.03.
Percentage difference = (T\u2082 \u2212 T\u2081)/T\u2081 \xD7 100 = 3.0 %.
Answer: (b) 3.0.`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 22 Jul \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pge",st:"Height reached and speed at infinity",q:"A body is projected vertically upwards from the surface of earth with a velocity sufficient enough to carry it to infinity. The time taken by it to reach height h is ______ s.",o:["\u221A{2R\u2091;g}[(1 + {h;R\u2091})^(3/2) \u2212 1]","{1;3}\u221A{R\u2091;2g}[(1 + {h;R\u2091})^(3/2) \u2212 1]","\u221A{R\u2091;2g}[(1 + {h;R\u2091})^(3/2) \u2212 1]","{1;3}\u221A{2R\u2091;g}[(1 + {h;R\u2091})^(3/2) \u2212 1]"],c:3,s:`Launched with escape speed, the total energy is zero: \xBDv\xB2 = GM/r, so v = dr/dt = \u221A(2GM/r).
Then \u221Ar dr = \u221A(2GM) dt. Integrate from R\u2091 to R\u2091+h: t = (2/3)[(R\u2091+h)^(3/2) \u2212 R\u2091^(3/2)]/\u221A(2GM).
Use GM = gR\u2091\xB2: t = (2/3)\xB7R\u2091^(3/2)/(R\u2091\u221A(2g)) \xD7 [(1 + h/R\u2091)^(3/2) \u2212 1].
The factor (2/3)\u221A(R\u2091/(2g)) equals (1/3)\u221A(2R\u2091/g).
Answer: (d).`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 17 Mar \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pgs",st:"Geostationary and comparing satellites",q:"A geostationary satellite is orbiting around an arbitrary planet P at a height of 11R above the surface of P, R being the radius of P. The time period of another satellite in hours at a height of 2R from the surface of P is ______. P has time period of 24 hours.",o:["{6;\u221A2}","3","6\u221A2","5"],c:1,s:`The geostationary satellite has period 24 h (same as the planet) at orbital radius r\u2081 = R + 11R = 12R.
The other satellite has r\u2082 = R + 2R = 3R.
T \u221D r^(3/2): T\u2082 = 24 \xD7 (3R/12R)^(3/2) = 24 \xD7 (1/4)^(3/2) = 24/8.
T\u2082 = 3 hours.
Answer: (b) 3.`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 18 Mar \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pgs",st:"Geostationary and comparing satellites",q:"The time period of a satellite in a circular orbit of radius R is T. The period of another satellite in a circular orbit of radius 9R is",o:["9 T","27 T","3 T","12 T"],c:1,s:`T \u221D r^(3/2).
Radius becomes 9R, so the period becomes T \xD7 9^(3/2) = T \xD7 27.
Answer: (b) 27 T.`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 24 Feb \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pgs",st:"Geostationary and comparing satellites",q:"Consider two satellites S\u2081 and S\u2082 with periods of revolution 1 hr and 8 hr respectively revolving around a planet in circular orbits. The ratio of angular velocity of satellite S\u2081 to the angular velocity of satellite S\u2082 is",o:["2 : 1","1 : 4","8 : 1","1 : 8"],c:2,s:`Angular velocity \u03C9 = 2\u03C0/T, so \u03C9 is inversely proportional to T.
\u03C9\u2081 : \u03C9\u2082 = T\u2082 : T\u2081 = 8 : 1.
Answer: (c) 8 : 1.`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 25 Feb \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pgs",st:"Orbital speed and period",q:`Two satellites A and B of masses 200 kg and 400 kg are revolving round the earth at height of 600 km and 1600 km respectively. If T_A and T_B are the time periods of A and B respectively then the value of T_B \u2212 T_A
(Given : radius of earth = 6400 km, mass of earth = 6 \xD7 10\xB2\u2074 kg)`,fig:{w:220,h:190,alt:"Earth at the centre with two concentric circular orbits, A on the inner orbit and B on the outer orbit (not to scale)",d:[["c",110,95,30,"f"],["c",110,95,55],["c",110,95,80],["c",110,95,2.5,"F"],["t",110,112,"E","ms"],["c",71,56,3.5,"F"],["t",60,50,"A","e"],["c",167,39,3.5,"F"],["t",174,38,"B",""]]},o:["4.24 \xD7 10\xB3 s","1.33 \xD7 10\xB3 s","4.24 \xD7 10\xB2 s","3.33 \xD7 10\xB2 s"],c:1,s:`Period of a circular orbit: T = 2\u03C0\u221A(r\xB3/GM). The mass of the satellite does not matter.
r_A = 6400 + 600 = 7000 km, r_B = 6400 + 1600 = 8000 km. GM = 6.67\xD710\u207B\xB9\xB9 \xD7 6\xD710\xB2\u2074 = 4.0\xD710\xB9\u2074.
T_A = 2\u03C0\u221A((7\xD710\u2076)\xB3/4\xD710\xB9\u2074) \u2248 5.82\xD710\xB3 s.
T_B = 2\u03C0\u221A((8\xD710\u2076)\xB3/4\xD710\xB9\u2074) \u2248 7.11\xD710\xB3 s.
T_B \u2212 T_A \u2248 1.29\xD710\xB3 s, which is closest to 1.33 \xD7 10\xB3 s.
Answer: (b).`},{y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 3 Sep \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pgk",st:"Elliptical orbits and angular momentum",q:"A satellite is moving in a low nearly circular orbit around the earth. Its radius is roughly equal to that of the earth's radius R\u2091. By firing rockets attached to it, its speed is instantaneously increased in the direction of its motion so that it become \u221A{3;2} times larger. Due to this, the farthest distance from the centre of the earth that the satellite reaches is R. Value of R is",o:["4R\u2091","2.5R\u2091","3R\u2091","2R\u2091"],c:2,s:`Original speed v\u2080 = \u221A(GM/R\u2091). New speed v = \u221A(3/2) v\u2080 at distance R\u2091.
At the farthest point (speed v\u2032, distance R): angular momentum gives v\u2032R = vR\u2091.
Energy: \xBDv\xB2 \u2212 GM/R\u2091 = \xBDv\u2032\xB2 \u2212 GM/R. With GM = v\u2080\xB2R\u2091 and x = R/R\u2091: \xBE \u2212 1 = 3/(4x\xB2) \u2212 1/x.
This gives x\xB2 \u2212 4x + 3 = 0, so x = 1 (the starting point) or x = 3.
R = 3R\u2091.
Answer: (c).`},{y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 4 Sep \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pgs",st:"Orbital speed and period",q:"A body is moving in a low circular orbit about a planet of mass M and radius R. The radius of the orbit can be taken to be R itself. Then the ratio of the speed of this body in the orbit to the escape velocity from the planet is",o:["\u221A2","2","{1;\u221A2}","1"],c:2,s:`Orbital speed at radius R: v\u2080 = \u221A(GM/R).
Escape speed: v\u2091 = \u221A(2GM/R) = \u221A2 v\u2080.
v\u2080/v\u2091 = 1/\u221A2.
Answer: (c) 1/\u221A2.`},{y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 6 Sep \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pgk",st:"Elliptical orbits and angular momentum",q:"A satellite is in an elliptical orbit around a planet P. It is observed that the velocity of the satellite when it is farthest from the planet is 6 times less than that when it is closest to the planet. The ratio of distances between the satellite and the planet at closest and farthest points is",o:["1 : 6","1 : 3","1 : 2","3 : 4"],c:0,s:`Angular momentum is conserved. At the closest and farthest points the velocity is perpendicular to the radius.
r_min v_max = r_max v_min.
So r_min/r_max = v_min/v_max = 1/6.
Answer: (a) 1 : 6.`},{y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 7 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pge",st:"Height reached and speed at infinity",q:"A satellite of mass m is launched vertically upwards with an initial speed u from the surface of the earth. After it reaches height R (R = radius of the earth), it ejects a rocket of mass {m;10} so that subsequently the satellite moves in a circular orbit. The kinetic energy of the rocket is (G is the gravitational constant, M is the mass of the earth)",o:["{3m;8}(u + \u221A{5GM;6R})\xB2","{m;20}(u\xB2 + {113;200}{GM;R})","5m(u\xB2 \u2212 {119;200}{GM;R})","{m;20}(u \u2212 \u221A{2GM;3R})\xB2"],c:2,s:`Speed v\u2081 at height R (distance 2R from the centre): v\u2081\xB2 = u\xB2 \u2212 2GM(1/R \u2212 1/2R) = u\xB2 \u2212 GM/R. It is directed radially.
The remaining mass 9m/10 moves in a circular orbit of radius 2R at speed v_c = \u221A(GM/2R), perpendicular to the radius.
Momentum is conserved: the rocket has radial velocity 10v\u2081 and tangential velocity \u22129v_c.
KE of rocket = \xBD(m/10)[(10v\u2081)\xB2 + (9v_c)\xB2] = 5m v\u2081\xB2 + (81m/20)(GM/2R) = 5m(u\xB2 \u2212 GM/R) + (81/40)(mGM/R).
= 5mu\xB2 \u2212 (119/40)(mGM/R) = 5m(u\xB2 \u2212 (119/200)GM/R).
Answer: (c).`},{y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 9 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pgs",st:"Orbital speed and period",q:"A body A of mass m is moving in a circular orbit of radius R about a planet. Another body B of mass {m;2} collides with A with a velocity which is half ({v\u20D7;2}) the instantaneous velocity v\u20D7 of A. The collision is completely inelastic. Then, the combined body",o:["escapes from the planet's gravitational field","starts moving in an elliptical orbit around the planet","falls vertically downwards towards the planet","continues to move in a circular orbit."],c:1,s:`Momentum is conserved in the collision (the colliding body moves along the direction of v\u20D7).
Speed after collision: v\u2032 = (mv + (m/2)(v/2))/(3m/2) = 5v/6.
The new speed is smaller than the circular orbital speed v but not zero, and it is still tangential.
Below the circular speed but above zero, so the body moves in an ellipse with this point as the farthest point.
It neither escapes (needs \u221A2 v), nor falls straight down (needs zero speed), nor stays circular.
Answer: (b) an elliptical orbit.`},{y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 9 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pge",st:"Escape speed",q:"Planet A has mass M and radius R. Planet B has half the mass and half the radius of planet A. If the escape velocities from the planets A and B are v_A and v_B, respectively, then {v_A;v_B} = {n;4}. The value of n is",o:["3","4","1","2"],c:1,s:`v\u2091 = \u221A(2GM/R) \u221D \u221A(M/R).
For B: M/R = (M/2)/(R/2) = M/R, the same as for A.
So v_A/v_B = 1 = n/4, which gives n = 4.
Answer: (b) 4.`},{y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 8 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pge",st:"Escape speed",q:"A rocket has to be launched from earth in such a way that it never returns. If E is the minimum energy delivered by the rocket launcher, what should be the minimum energy that the launcher should have if the same rocket is to be launched from the surface of the moon? Assume that the density of the earth and the moon are equal and that the earth's volume is 64 times the volume of the moon.",o:["{E;4}","{E;16}","{E;64}","{E;32}"],c:1,s:`Minimum energy to escape from a surface: E = GMm/R = (4/3)\u03C0G\u03C1 m R\xB2 (same density, same m).
So E \u221D R\xB2.
Volume ratio 64 means radius ratio R_earth/R_moon = 4.
E_moon = E \xD7 (1/4)\xB2 = E/16.
Answer: (b) E/16.`},{y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 10 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pgs",st:"Orbital speed and period",q:`A spaceship orbits around a planet at a height of 20 km from its surface. Assuming that only gravitational field of the planet acts on the spaceship, what will be the number of complete revolutions made by the spaceship in 24 hours around the planet?
[Given : Mass of planet = 8 \xD7 10\xB2\xB2 kg,
Radius of planet = 2 \xD7 10\u2076 m,
Gravitational constant G = 6.67 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\xB9 N m\xB2/kg\xB2]`,o:["13","9","17","11"],c:3,s:`Orbital radius r = 2\xD710\u2076 + 2\xD710\u2074 = 2.02\xD710\u2076 m.
GM = 6.67\xD710\u207B\xB9\xB9 \xD7 8\xD710\xB2\xB2 = 5.34\xD710\xB9\xB2 m\xB3/s\xB2.
T = 2\u03C0\u221A(r\xB3/GM) = 2\u03C0\u221A(8.24\xD710\xB9\u2078/5.34\xD710\xB9\xB2) \u2248 7.8\xD710\xB3 s.
Revolutions in 24 h = 86400/7809 \u2248 11.06.
Complete revolutions = 11.
Answer: (d) 11.`},{y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 9 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pge",st:"Gravitational potential energy",q:"The energy required to take a satellite to a height h above Earth surface (radius of earth = 6.4 \xD7 10\xB3 km) is E\u2081 and kinetic energy required for the satellite to be in a circular orbit at this height is E\u2082. The value of h for which E\u2081 and E\u2082 are equal is",o:["3.2 \xD7 10\xB3 km","1.28 \xD7 10\u2074 km","1.6 \xD7 10\xB3 km","6.4 \xD7 10\xB3 km"],c:0,s:`E\u2081 = increase in potential energy = GMm/R \u2212 GMm/(R+h) = GMm h/(R(R+h)).
E\u2082 = kinetic energy in orbit at radius R+h = GMm/(2(R+h)).
Set E\u2081 = E\u2082: h/R = 1/2, so h = R/2.
h = 6.4\xD710\xB3/2 = 3.2\xD710\xB3 km.
Answer: (a).`},{y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 10 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pge",st:"Escape speed",q:"A satellite is moving with a constant speed v in circular orbit around the earth. An object of mass m is ejected from the satellite such that it just escapes from the gravitational pull of the earth. At the time of ejection, the kinetic energy of the object is",o:["{1;2}mv\xB2","2mv\xB2","{3;2}mv\xB2","mv\xB2"],c:3,s:`The satellite speed is v = \u221A(GM/r). Escape speed at the same distance is \u221A(2GM/r) = \u221A2 v.
Kinetic energy needed = \xBDm(\u221A2 v)\xB2 = mv\xB2.
Answer: (d) mv\xB2.`},{y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 10 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pge",st:"Escape speed",q:"Two stars of masses 3 \xD7 10\xB3\xB9 kg each, and at distance 2 \xD7 10\xB9\xB9 m rotate in a plane about their common centre of mass O. A meteorite passes through O moving perpendicular to the star's rotation plane. In order to escape from the gravitational field of this double star, the minimum speed that meteorite should have at O is (Take Gravitational constant G = 6.67 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\xB9 N m\xB2 kg\u207B\xB2)",o:["2.8 \xD7 10\u2075 m/s","1.4 \xD7 10\u2075 m/s","3.8 \xD7 10\u2074 m/s","2.4 \xD7 10\u2074 m/s"],c:0,s:`O is the midpoint, so each star is at distance 10\xB9\xB9 m from O.
Potential at O: V = \u22122GM/r = \u22122 \xD7 6.67\xD710\u207B\xB9\xB9 \xD7 3\xD710\xB3\xB9/10\xB9\xB9 = \u22124.0\xD710\xB9\u2070 J/kg.
To escape, \xBDv\xB2 = 4.0\xD710\xB9\u2070, so v\xB2 = 8.0\xD710\xB9\u2070.
v \u2248 2.8\xD710\u2075 m/s.
Answer: (a).`},{y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 11 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pge",st:"Escape speed",q:"A satellite is revolving in a circular orbit at a height h from the earth surface, such that h << R where R is the radius of the earth. Assuming that the effect of earth's atmosphere can be neglected the minimum increase in the speed required so that the satellite could escape from the gravitational field of earth is",o:["\u221A(2gR)","\u221A(gR)(\u221A2 \u2212 1)","\u221A{gR;2}","\u221A(gR)"],c:1,s:`Since h << R, the orbit radius is about R and the orbital speed is v\u2080 = \u221A(gR).
Escape speed is \u221A(2gR) = \u221A2 \xD7 \u221A(gR).
Increase needed = \u221A(2gR) \u2212 \u221A(gR) = \u221A(gR)(\u221A2 \u2212 1).
Answer: (b).`},{y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 12 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pgs",st:"Orbital speed and period",q:"A satellite of mass M is in a circular orbit of radius R about the centre of the earth. A meteorite of the same mass, falling towards the earth, collides with the satellite completely inelastically. The speeds of the satellite and meteorite are the same, just before the collision. The subsequent motion of the combined body will be",o:["in an elliptical orbit","in the same circular orbit of radius R","in a circular orbit of different radius","such that it escapes to infinity."],c:0,s:`Before the collision the satellite has speed v = \u221A(GM_e/R) along the tangent. The meteorite falls radially at speed v.
Momentum conservation (mass 2M): tangential speed v/2 and radial speed v/2, so the speed is v/\u221A2.
Energy per unit mass: \xBD(v\xB2/2) \u2212 GM_e/R = \u2212\xBE v\xB2 (since v\xB2 = GM_e/R), which is negative, so the body stays bound.
The velocity has a radial part, so the point is not the closest or farthest point of a circle; the path cannot be circular.
Hence the combined body moves in an elliptical orbit: option (a).`},{y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 12 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pgs",st:"Energy of a satellite",q:"Two satellites, A and B, have masses m and 2m respectively. A is in a circular orbit of radius R, and B is in a circular orbit of radius 2R around the earth. The ratio of their kinetic energies, {T_A;T_B}, is",o:["1","2","\u221A{1;2}","{1;2}"],c:0,s:`Orbital kinetic energy: T = \xBDmv\xB2 = GMm/(2r).
T_A = GM(m)/(2R) = GMm/(2R).
T_B = GM(2m)/(2\xB72R) = GMm/(2R).
T_A/T_B = 1.
Answer: (a) 1.`},{y:"JEE Advanced 2017",tp:"pge",st:"Escape speed",q:"A rocket is launched normal to the surface of the Earth, away from the Sun, along the line joining the Sun and the Earth. The Sun is 3 \xD7 10\u2075 times heavier than the Earth and is at a distance 2.5 \xD7 10\u2074 times larger than the radius of the Earth. The escape velocity from Earth's gravitational field is v\u2091 = 11.2 km s\u207B\xB9. The minimum initial velocity (v_s) required for the rocket to be able to leave the Sun-Earth system is closest to (Ignore the rotation and revolution of the Earth and the presence of any other planet)",o:["v_s = 62 km s\u207B\xB9","v_s = 22 km s\u207B\xB9","v_s = 72 km s\u207B\xB9","v_s = 42 km s\u207B\xB9"],c:3,s:`The rocket must overcome both the Earth's and the Sun's pull: \xBDv_s\xB2 = GM\u2091/R\u2091 + GM_sun/d.
M_sun = 3\xD710\u2075 M\u2091 and d = 2.5\xD710\u2074 R\u2091, so GM_sun/d = 12 GM\u2091/R\u2091.
\xBDv_s\xB2 = 13 GM\u2091/R\u2091, so v_s\xB2 = 13 \xD7 (2GM\u2091/R\u2091) = 13 v\u2091\xB2.
v_s = \u221A13 \xD7 11.2 \u2248 3.6 \xD7 11.2 \u2248 40 km/s.
The closest option is 42 km/s.
Answer: (d).`},{y:"JEE Main 2016",tp:"pge",st:"Escape speed",q:"A satellite is revolving in a circular orbit at a height 'h' from the earth's surface (radius of earth R; h << R). The minimum increase in its orbital velocity required, so that the satellite could escape from the earth's gravitational field, is close to (Neglect the effect of atmosphere.)",o:["\u221A(2gR)","\u221A(gR)","\u221A(gR/2)","\u221A(gR)(\u221A2 \u2212 1)"],c:3,s:`For h << R the orbital speed is \u221A(gR).
Escape speed is \u221A(2gR).
Minimum increase = \u221A(2gR) \u2212 \u221A(gR) = \u221A(gR)(\u221A2 \u2212 1).
Answer: (d).`},{y:"JEE Main 2016 \xB7 Online",tp:"pgg",st:"g with height and depth",q:"An astronaut of mass m is working on a satellite orbiting the earth at a distance h from the earth's surface. The radius of the earth is R, while its mass is M. The gravitational pull F_G on the astronaut is",o:["Zero since astronaut feels weightless","{GMm;(R+h)\xB2} < F_G < {GMm;R\xB2}","F_G = {GMm;(R+h)\xB2}","0 < F_G < {GMm;R\xB2}"],c:2,s:`The astronaut is at distance R + h from the centre of the earth.
Gravitational force = GMm/(R + h)\xB2. It is not zero.
The astronaut feels weightless only because the satellite and the astronaut both fall freely with the same acceleration.
The exact value is F_G = GMm/(R+h)\xB2, so option (c) is the answer.
(Option (b) is wrong because F_G equals the lower bound, not above it.)`},{y:"JEE Main 2014 \xB7 Online",tp:"pgk",st:"Kepler's laws",q:"India's Mangalayan was set to the Mars by launching it into a transfer orbit EOM around the sun. It leaves the earth at E and meets Mars at M. If the semi-major axis of Earth's orbit is a\u2091 = 1.5 \xD7 10\xB9\xB9 m, that of Mars' orbit a\u2098 = 2.28 \xD7 10\xB9\xB9 m, taken Kepler's law give the estimate of time for Mangalayan to reach Mars from Earth to be close to",fig:{w:340,h:200,alt:"Sun at the centre of two circular orbits, Earth's orbit inside and Mars' orbit outside; the transfer ellipse runs from E on Earth's orbit over the top through O to M on Mars' orbit",d:[["c",170,100,85],["c",170,100,50],["c",170,100,3,"F"],["t",170,120,"Sun","ms"],["l",85,100,220,100],["c",85,100,2.5,"F"],["c",220,100,2.5,"F"],["t",78,96,"M","e"],["t",226,96,"E",""],["p","M220 100 A67.5 65.2 0 0 0 85 100",""],["t",152,28,"O","ms"],["t",118,94,"a\u2098","ms"],["t",196,94,"a\u2091","ms"],["t",170,11,"Mars' orbit","ms"],["t",170,168,"Earth's orbit","ms"]]},o:["500 days","320 days","260 days","220 days"],c:2,s:`The transfer orbit is an ellipse with perihelion at E (distance a\u2091) and aphelion at M (distance a\u2098).
Its semi-major axis a = (a\u2091 + a\u2098)/2 = (1.5 + 2.28)/2 \xD7 10\xB9\xB9 = 1.89\xD710\xB9\xB9 m.
Kepler's third law: T\xB2 \u221D a\xB3, so T = 1 year \xD7 (1.89/1.5)^(3/2) = 365 \xD7 1.414 \u2248 516 days.
The trip E to M is half an orbit: t = 516/2 \u2248 258 days.
Closest option: 260 days. Answer: (c).`},{y:"JEE Main 2013",tp:"pge",st:"Gravitational potential energy",q:"What is the minimum energy required to launch a satellite of mass m from the surface of a planet of mass M and radius R in a circular orbit at an altitude of 2R?",o:["{GmM;3R}","{5GmM;6R}","{2GmM;3R}","{GmM;2R}"],c:1,s:`Orbit radius = R + 2R = 3R.
Energy on the surface: \u2212GMm/R. Energy in the orbit: \u2212GMm/(2 \xD7 3R) = \u2212GMm/(6R).
Minimum energy needed = \u2212GMm/6R \u2212 (\u2212GMm/R) = GMm/R \xD7 (1 \u2212 1/6).
= 5GMm/(6R).
Answer: (b).`},{y:"IIT-JEE 2011",tp:"pge",st:"Escape speed",q:"A satellite is moving with a constant speed V in a circular orbit about the earth. An object of mass m is ejected from the satellite such that it just escapes from the gravitational pull of the earth. At the time of its ejection, the kinetic energy of the object is",o:["{1;2}mV\xB2","mV\xB2","{3;2}mV\xB2","2mV\xB2"],c:1,s:`Orbital speed V = \u221A(GM/r). Escape speed at the same place = \u221A(2GM/r) = \u221A2 V.
Kinetic energy needed = \xBDm(\u221A2 V)\xB2 = mV\xB2.
Answer: (b) mV\xB2.`},{y:"AIEEE 2008",tp:"pge",st:"Escape speed",q:"A planet in a distant solar system is 10 times more massive than the earth and its radius is 10 times smaller. Given that the escape velocity from the earth is 11 km s\u207B\xB9, the escape velocity from the surface of the planet would be",o:["0.11 km s\u207B\xB9","1.1 km s\u207B\xB9","11 km s\u207B\xB9","110 km s\u207B\xB9"],c:3,s:`v\u2091 = \u221A(2GM/R) \u221D \u221A(M/R).
M is 10 times larger and R is 10 times smaller, so M/R is 100 times larger.
The escape speed becomes \u221A100 = 10 times larger.
v = 10 \xD7 11 = 110 km/s.
Answer: (d).`},{y:"AIEEE 2004",tp:"pgs",st:"Orbital speed and period",q:"The time period of an earth satellite in circular orbit is independent of",o:["the mass of the satellite","radius of its orbit","both the mass and the radius of the orbit","neither the mass of the satellite nor the radius of the orbit."],c:0,s:`For a circular orbit, GMm/r\xB2 = m\u03C9\xB2r, so T = 2\u03C0\u221A(r\xB3/GM).
The satellite mass m cancels out.
T depends on the orbit radius r (and on the central mass M) but not on m.
Answer: (a).`},{y:"AIEEE 2004",tp:"pgs",st:"Orbital speed and period",q:"A satellite of mass m revolves around the earth of radius R at a height x from its surface. If g is the acceleration due to gravity on the surface of the earth, the orbital speed of the satellite is",o:["gx","{gR;R\u2212x}","{gR\xB2;R+x}","({gR\xB2;R+x})^(1/2)"],c:3,s:`Orbit radius = R + x. Gravity supplies the centripetal force: mv\xB2/(R+x) = GMm/(R+x)\xB2.
So v\xB2 = GM/(R+x). Use GM = gR\xB2: v\xB2 = gR\xB2/(R+x).
v = (gR\xB2/(R+x))^(1/2).
Answer: (d).`},{y:"AIEEE 2003",tp:"pge",st:"Escape speed",q:"The escape velocity for a body projected vertically upwards from the surface of earth is 11 km/s. If the body is projected at an angle of 45\xB0 with the vertical, the escape velocity will be",o:["11\u221A2 km/s","22 km/s","11 km/s","{11;\u221A2} km/s"],c:2,s:`Escape speed comes from energy: \xBDmv\u2091\xB2 = GMm/R. Only the speed matters, not the direction.
The launch direction does not change it (the path must not hit the earth, but the minimum speed to escape stays the same).
So the escape velocity is still 11 km/s.
Answer: (c).`},{y:"AIEEE 2003",tp:"pgs",st:"Geostationary and comparing satellites",q:"The time period of a satellite of earth is 5 hour. If the separation between the earth and the satellite is increased to 4 times the previous value, the new time period will become",o:["10 hour","80 hour","40 hour","20 hour"],c:2,s:`T \u221D r^(3/2).
r becomes 4r, so T becomes T \xD7 4^(3/2) = 8T.
New period = 8 \xD7 5 = 40 hours.
Answer: (c).`},{y:"AIEEE 2002",tp:"pge",st:"Escape speed",q:"The escape velocity of a body depends upon mass as",o:["m\u2070","m\xB9","m\xB2","m\xB3."],c:0,s:`Escape speed: \xBDmv\u2091\xB2 = GMm/R, so v\u2091 = \u221A(2GM/R).
The mass m of the body cancels, so v\u2091 does not depend on m.
v\u2091 \u221D m\u2070.
Answer: (a).`},{y:"AIEEE 2002",tp:"pge",st:"Escape speed",q:"The kinetic energy needed to project a body of mass m from the earth surface (radius R) to infinity is",o:["mgR/2","2mgR","mgR","mgR/4"],c:2,s:`Total energy at infinity is zero, so the body needs KE = \u2212(potential energy at surface).
Potential energy at the surface = \u2212GMm/R.
So KE = GMm/R.
Using GM = gR\xB2, KE = mgR.
Answer: mgR.`},{y:"AIEEE 2002",tp:"pgs",st:"Orbital speed and period",q:"If suddenly the gravitational force of attraction between Earth and a satellite revolving around it becomes zero, then the satellite will",o:["continue to move in its orbit with same velocity","move tangentially to the original orbit in the same velocity","become stationary in its orbit","move towards the earth."],c:1,s:`Gravity provides the centripetal force that bends the path into a circle.
If this force vanishes, no net force acts on the satellite.
By Newton's first law it keeps its velocity at that instant.
So it moves along the tangent to the orbit with the same speed.
Answer: moves tangentially with the same velocity.`},{y:"IIT-JEE 2002",tp:"pgs",st:"Geostationary and comparing satellites",q:"A geo-stationary satellite orbits around the earth in a circular orbit of radius 36,000 km. Then, the time period of a spy satellite orbiting a few hundred km above the earth's surface (R_earth = 6,400 km) will approximately be",o:["(1/2) hr","1 hr","2 hr","4 hr"],c:2,s:`Kepler's third law: T\xB2 \u221D r\xB3.
Geostationary: r\u2081 = 36000 km, T\u2081 = 24 hr.
Spy satellite: r\u2082 \u2248 6400 km (a few hundred km above the surface is only a small change).
T\u2082 = 24 \xD7 (6400/36000)^(3/2) = 24 \xD7 (0.178)^1.5 \u2248 24 \xD7 0.075 \u2248 1.8 hr.
Answer: about 2 hr.`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1997",tp:"pgs",st:"Energy of a satellite",q:"An artificial satellite moving in a circular orbit around the earth has total (kinetic + potential) energy E\u2080. Its potential energy is",o:["\u2212 E\u2080","1.5E\u2080","2E\u2080","E\u2080"],c:2,s:`For a circular orbit, KE = GMm/2r and PE = \u2212GMm/r.
Total energy E\u2080 = \u2212GMm/2r.
PE = \u2212GMm/r = 2 \xD7 (\u2212GMm/2r) = 2E\u2080.
Answer: 2E\u2080.`},{y:"JEE Advanced 2013",tp:"pge",st:"Escape speed",q:"Two bodies, each of mass M, are kept fixed with a separation 2L. A particle of mass m is projected from the midpoint of the line joining their centres, perpendicular to the line. The gravitational constant is G. The correct statement(s) is (are)",fig:{w:340,h:130,alt:"Two fixed masses M separated by 2L with a particle of mass m at the midpoint, projected perpendicular to the line joining them.",d:[["l",60,70,280,70,"d"],["c",60,70,11,"F"],["c",280,70,11,"F"],["c",170,70,6,"F"],["t",60,100,"M","m"],["t",280,100,"M","m"],["t",178,86,"m"],["l",170,64,170,22,"a"],["t",178,30,"v"],["l",60,116,280,116,"b"],["t",170,128,"2L","m"]]},o:["The minimum initial velocity of the mass m to escape the gravitational field of the two bodies is 4\u221A{GM;L}.","The minimum initial velocity of the mass m to escape the gravitational field of the two bodies is 2\u221A{GM;L}.","The minimum initial velocity of the mass m to escape the gravitational field of the two bodies is \u221A{2GM;L}.","The energy of the mass m remains constant."],c:[1,3],s:`Potential at the midpoint: each mass is at distance L, so V = \u2212GM/L \u2212 GM/L = \u22122GM/L.
For escape, \xBDmv\xB2 \u2212 2GMm/L = 0, so v = 2\u221A(GM/L). So (b) is correct, (a) and (c) are not.
The two big masses are fixed and the force is conservative, so the total energy of m stays constant. So (d) is correct.
Answer: (b) and (d).`},{y:"IIT-JEE 2012",tp:"pge",st:"Escape speed",q:"Two spherical planets P and Q have the same uniform density \u03C1, masses M_P and M_Q, and surface areas A and 4A, respectively. A spherical planet R also has uniform density \u03C1 and its mass is (M_P + M_Q). The escape velocities from the planets P, Q and R are V_P, V_Q and V_R, respectively. Then",o:["V_Q > V_R > V_P","V_R > V_Q > V_P","{V_R;V_P} = 3","{V_P;V_Q} = {1;2}"],c:[1,3],s:`Area ratio 1 : 4 gives radius ratio R_P : R_Q = 1 : 2, so M_Q = 8M_P (same density).
Planet R has mass 9M_P, so R_R\xB3 = 9R_P\xB3 and R_R = 9^(1/3) R_P \u2248 2.08R_P.
Escape speed v = \u221A(2GM/R) = R\u221A(8\u03C0G\u03C1/3), so v \u221D R for equal density.
So V_P : V_Q : V_R = 1 : 2 : 2.08, giving V_R > V_Q > V_P and V_P/V_Q = 1/2.
V_R/V_P = 9^(1/3) \u2248 2.08, not 3.
Answer: (b) and (d).`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1998",tp:"pgk",st:"Elliptical orbits and angular momentum",q:"A satellite S is moving in an elliptical orbit around the earth. The mass of the satellite is very small compared to the mass of the earth.",o:["The acceleration of S is always directed towards the centre of the earth.","The angular momentum of S about the centre of the earth changes in direction, but its magnitude remains constant.","The total mechanical energy of S varies periodically with time.","The linear momentum of S remains constant in magnitude."],c:[0],s:`Gravity is a central force, so the acceleration always points to the centre of the earth. (a) is true.
Torque about the centre is zero, so angular momentum is constant in both magnitude and direction (the orbit stays in one plane). (b) is false.
Gravity is conservative and the earth's motion is negligible, so total mechanical energy is constant. (c) is false.
The speed changes along the ellipse (fastest at perigee), so linear momentum changes. (d) is false.
Answer: only (a).`},{y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 2 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pgs",st:"Energy of a satellite",q:`A satellite of mass 1000 kg is launched to revolve around the earth in an orbit at a height of 270 km from the earth's surface. Kinetic energy of the satellite in this orbit is ______ \xD7 10\xB9\u2070 J.
(Mass of earth = 6 \xD7 10\xB2\u2074 kg, Radius of earth = 6.4 \xD7 10\u2076 m, Gravitational constant = 6.67 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\xB9 Nm\xB2kg\u207B\xB2)`,n:3,s:`Orbit radius r = 6.4 \xD7 10\u2076 + 0.27 \xD7 10\u2076 = 6.67 \xD7 10\u2076 m.
KE = GMm/(2r).
GM = 6.67 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\xB9 \xD7 6 \xD7 10\xB2\u2074 = 4.0 \xD7 10\xB9\u2074.
KE = 4.0 \xD7 10\xB9\u2074 \xD7 1000 / (2 \xD7 6.67 \xD7 10\u2076) = 3.0 \xD7 10\xB9\u2070 J.
Answer: 3.`},{y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 23 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pgs",st:"Orbital speed and period",q:"A satellite of mass M/2 is revolving around earth in a circular orbit at a height of R/3 from earth's surface. The angular momentum of the satellite is M\u221A{GMR;x}. The value of x is ______, where M and R are the mass and radius of earth, respectively. (G is the gravitational constant)",n:3,s:`Orbit radius r = R + R/3 = 4R/3.
Orbital speed v = \u221A(GM/r) = \u221A(3GM/4R).
L = mvr = (M/2) \xD7 \u221A(3GM/4R) \xD7 (4R/3).
L = (M/2)(4R/3)(\u221A3/2)\u221A(GM/R) = M\u221A(GMR)/\u221A3 = M\u221A{GMR;3}.
So x = 3.`},{y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 29 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pgs",st:"Geostationary and comparing satellites",q:"Two planets, A and B, are orbiting a common star in circular orbits of radii R_A and R_B, respectively, with R_B = 2R_A. The planet B is 4\u221A2 times more massive than planet A. The ratio ({L_B;L_A}) of angular momentum (L_B) of planet B to that of planet A(L_A) is closet to integer ______.",n:8,s:`For a circular orbit L = mvr with v = \u221A(GM_s/r), so L = m\u221A(GM_s r).
L \u221D m\u221Ar.
L_B/L_A = (m_B/m_A) \xD7 \u221A(R_B/R_A) = 4\u221A2 \xD7 \u221A2 = 8.
Answer: 8.`},{y:"JEE Advanced 2025",tp:"pgs",st:"Geostationary and comparing satellites",q:"A geostationary satellite above the equator is orbiting around the earth at a fixed distance r\u2081 from the center of the earth. A second satellite is orbiting in the equatorial plane in the opposite direction to the earth's rotation, at a distance r\u2082 from the center of the earth, such that r\u2081 = 1.21 r\u2082. The time period of the second satellite as measured from the geostationary satellite is {24;p} hours. The value of p is ______.",n:2.33,s:`Geostationary period T\u2081 = 24 h. By Kepler's law T\u2082 = 24 \xD7 (r\u2082/r\u2081)^(3/2) = 24/1.21^1.5 = 24/1.331 h.
The satellites move in opposite directions, so their angular speeds add as seen from the first one.
Relative angular speed = 2\u03C0(1/T\u2082 + 1/T\u2081) = 2\u03C0(1.331/24 + 1/24) = 2\u03C0 \xD7 2.331/24.
Period seen from satellite 1 = 24/2.331 h, so p = 2.331 \u2248 2.33.`},{y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 25 Jun \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pgs",st:"Geostationary and comparing satellites",q:"Two satellites S\u2081 and S\u2082 are revolving in circular orbits around a planet with radius R\u2081 = 3200 km and R\u2082 = 800 km respectively. The ratio of speed of satellite S\u2081 to the speed of satellite S\u2082 in their respective orbits would be {1;x}, where x = ______.",n:2,s:`Orbital speed v = \u221A(GM/r), so v \u221D 1/\u221Ar.
v\u2081/v\u2082 = \u221A(R\u2082/R\u2081) = \u221A(800/3200) = \u221A(1/4) = 1/2.
So x = 2.`},{y:"JEE Advanced 2022",tp:"pge",st:"Escape speed",q:"Two spherical stars A and B have densities \u03C1_A and \u03C1_B, respectively. A and B have the same radius, and their masses M_A and M_B are related by M_B = 2M_A. Due to an interaction process, star A loses some of its mass, so that its radius is halved, while its spherical shape is retained, and its density remains \u03C1_A. The entire mass lost by A is deposited as a thick spherical shell on B with the density of the shell being \u03C1_A. If v_A and v_B are the escape velocities from A and B after the interaction process, the ratio {v_B;v_A} = \u221A{10n;15^(1/3)}. The value of n is ______.",n:2.3,s:`Let the original radius be R. A keeps density, so its new mass = M_A/8 (radius R/2). Mass lost = 7M_A/8.
Shell volume = (7M_A/8)/\u03C1_A = (7/8)(4/3)\u03C0R\xB3, so the new outer radius r of B satisfies r\xB3 \u2212 R\xB3 = (7/8)R\xB3, giving r = R(15/8)^(1/3).
New mass of B = 2M_A + 7M_A/8 = 23M_A/8.
v_A\xB2 = 2G(M_A/8)/(R/2) = GM_A/(2R). v_B\xB2 = 2G(23M_A/8)/r.
v_B\xB2/v_A\xB2 = [(23/8)/(15/8)^(1/3)] \xD7 4 = 23/15^(1/3) (since v_B\xB2/v_A\xB2 = [23GM_A/(4r)] \xF7 [GM_A/(2R)])..
So 10n = 23 and n = 2.3.`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 17 Mar \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pge",st:"Escape speed",q:"The radius in kilometer to which the present radius of earth (R = 6400 km) to be compressed so that the escape velocity is increased 10 times is ______.",n:64,s:`Escape speed v = \u221A(2GM/R). The mass M stays the same.
So v \u221D 1/\u221AR.
For 10 times the speed, \u221AR must become 10 times smaller, so R becomes 100 times smaller.
New radius = 6400/100 = 64 km.`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 25 Feb \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pge",st:"Height reached and speed at infinity",q:"The initial velocity v_i required to project a body vertically upward from the surface of the earth to reach a height of 10R, where R is the radius of the earth, may be described in terms of escape velocity v_e such that v_i = \u221A{x;y} \xD7 v_e. The value of x will be ______.",n:10,s:`Energy conservation from the surface (r = R) to the top (r = 11R, speed zero):
\xBDv_i\xB2 \u2212 GM/R = \u2212GM/(11R).
\xBDv_i\xB2 = (GM/R)(1 \u2212 1/11) = (10/11)(GM/R).
v_i\xB2 = (10/11)(2GM/R) = (10/11)v_e\xB2.
So v_i = \u221A(10/11) v_e, giving x = 10.`},{y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 8 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pge",st:"Height reached and speed at infinity",q:"An asteroid is moving directly towards the centre of the earth. When at a distance of 10 R (R is the radius of the earth) from the earth's centre, it has a speed of 12 km/s. Neglecting the effect of earth's atmosphere, what will be the speed of the asteroid when it hits the surface of the earth (Escape velocity from the earth is 11.2 km/s)? Give your answer to the nearest integer in kilometer/s ______.",n:16,s:`Energy conservation between 10R and R:
\xBDv\xB2 = \xBDv\u2080\xB2 + GM(1/R \u2212 1/10R) = \xBDv\u2080\xB2 + 0.9 GM/R.
v\xB2 = v\u2080\xB2 + 0.9 \xD7 (2GM/R) = v\u2080\xB2 + 0.9 v_e\xB2.
v\xB2 = 144 + 0.9 \xD7 (11.2)\xB2 = 144 + 112.9 = 256.9.
v \u2248 16.03 km/s, so the answer is 16.`},{y:"JEE Advanced 2021",tp:"pgs",st:"Binary stars and multiple bodies",q:"The distance between two stars of masses 3M_S and 6M_S is 9R. Here R is the mean distance between the centers of the Earth and the Sun, and M_S is the mass of the Sun. The two stars orbit around their common center of mass in circular orbits with period nT, where T is the period of Earth's revolution around the Sun. The value of n is ______.",n:9,s:`For a binary with separation d, \u03C9\xB2 = G(M\u2081+M\u2082)/d\xB3, so T\xB2 = 4\u03C0\xB2d\xB3/[G(M\u2081+M\u2082)].
Stars: T_b\xB2 = 4\u03C0\xB2(9R)\xB3/(G \xD7 9M_S) = 81 \xD7 4\u03C0\xB2R\xB3/(GM_S).
Earth around the Sun: T\xB2 = 4\u03C0\xB2R\xB3/(GM_S).
So T_b\xB2 = 81T\xB2, T_b = 9T.
n = 9.`},{y:"JEE Advanced 2015",tp:"pge",st:"Height reached and speed at infinity",q:"A bullet is fired vertically upwards with velocity v from the surface of a spherical planet. When it reaches its maximum height, its acceleration due to the planet's gravity is 1/4th of its value at the surface of the planet. If the escape velocity from the planet is v_esc = v\u221AN, then the value of N is ______. (ignore energy loss due to atmosphere)",n:2,s:`g \u221D 1/r\xB2. Acceleration 1/4 of surface value means r = 2R at the top.
Energy: \xBDv\xB2 \u2212 GM/R = \u2212GM/(2R), so v\xB2 = GM/R.
Escape speed: v_esc\xB2 = 2GM/R = 2v\xB2, so v_esc = v\u221A2.
N = 2.`},{y:"IIT-JEE 2010",tp:"pgs",st:"Binary stars and multiple bodies",q:"A binary star consists of two stars A (mass 2.2M_s) and B (mass 11M_s), where M_s is the mass of the sun. They are separated by distance d and are rotating about their centre of mass, which is stationary. The ratio of the total angular momentum of the binary star to the angular momentum of star B about the centre of mass is ______.",n:6,s:`Distances from the centre of mass: r_A = d \xD7 11/13.2 and r_B = d \xD7 2.2/13.2, so r_A/r_B = 5.
Both stars have the same \u03C9, and angular momentum L = mr\xB2\u03C9.
L_A/L_B = (2.2 \xD7 r_A\xB2)/(11 \xD7 r_B\xB2) = 0.2 \xD7 25 = 5.
Total/L_B = (L_A + L_B)/L_B = 5 + 1 = 6.`},{y:"IIT-JEE 2010",tp:"pge",st:"Escape speed",q:"Gravitational acceleration on the surface of a planet is {\u221A6;11} g, where g is the gravitational acceleration on the surface of the earth. The average mass density of the planet is {2;3} times that on earth. If the escape speed on the surface of the earth is taken to be 11 km s\u207B\xB9, the escape speed on the surface of the planet in km s\u207B\xB9 will be ______.",n:3,s:`g = (4/3)\u03C0G\u03C1R, so R \u221D g/\u03C1.
R_p/R_e = (\u221A6/11)/(2/3) = 3\u221A6/22.
Escape speed v = \u221A(2gR), so v_p/v_e = \u221A[(g_p/g_e)(R_p/R_e)].
v_p/v_e = \u221A[(\u221A6/11)(3\u221A6/22)] = \u221A(18/242) = 3/11.
v_p = 11 \xD7 3/11 = 3 km/s.`},{y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 5 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pgs",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:`Match List I with List II.
List I
(A) Kinetic energy of planet
(B) Gravitation Potential energy of sun-planet system
(C) Total mechanical energy of planet
(D) Escape energy at the surface of planet for unit mass object
List II
(I) \u2212GMm/a
(II) {GMm;2a}
(III) Gm/r
(IV) \u2212{GMm;2a}
(Where a = radius of planet orbit, r = radius of planet, M = mass of Sun, m = mass of planet).
Choose the correct answer from the options given below.`,o:["(A) - (I), (B) - (II), (C) - (III), (D) - (IV)","(A) - (I), (B) - (IV), (C) - (II), (D) - (III)","(A) - (III), (B) - (IV), (C) - (I), (D) - (II)","(A) - (II), (B) - (I), (C) - (IV), (D) - (III)"],c:3,s:`Circular orbit of radius a around the Sun: KE = GMm/2a, so (A) matches (II).
Potential energy of the sun-planet system = \u2212GMm/a, so (B) matches (I).
Total energy = KE + PE = \u2212GMm/2a, so (C) matches (IV).
Escape energy per unit mass at the planet's surface = Gm/r (m is the planet's mass, r its radius), so (D) matches (III).
Answer: (A)-(II), (B)-(I), (C)-(IV), (D)-(III).`},{y:"JEE Advanced 2018",tp:"pgs",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:`A planet of mass M, has two natural satellites with masses m\u2081 and m\u2082. The radii of their circular orbits are R\u2081 and R\u2082 respectively. Ignore the gravitational force between the satellites. Define v\u2081, L\u2081, K\u2081 and T\u2081 to be, respectively, the orbital speed, angular momentum, kinetic energy and time period of revolution of satellite 1; and v\u2082, L\u2082, K\u2082 and T\u2082 to be the corresponding quantities of satellite 2. Given m\u2081/m\u2082 = 2 and R\u2081/R\u2082 = 1/4, match the ratios in List-I to the numbers in List-II.
List-I
P. v\u2081/v\u2082
Q. L\u2081/L\u2082
R. K\u2081/K\u2082
S. T\u2081/T\u2082
List-II
1. 1/8
2. 1
3. 2
4. 8`,o:["P \u2192 4; Q \u2192 2; R \u2192 1; S \u2192 3","P \u2192 3; Q \u2192 2; R \u2192 4; S \u2192 1","P \u2192 2; Q \u2192 3; R \u2192 1; S \u2192 4","P \u2192 2; Q \u2192 3; R \u2192 4; S \u2192 1"],c:1,s:`v = \u221A(GM/R), so v\u2081/v\u2082 = \u221A(R\u2082/R\u2081) = \u221A4 = 2. P matches 3.
L = m\u221A(GMR), so L\u2081/L\u2082 = 2 \xD7 \u221A(1/4) = 1. Q matches 2.
K = GMm/2R, so K\u2081/K\u2082 = 2 \xD7 4 = 8. R matches 4.
T \u221D R^(3/2), so T\u2081/T\u2082 = (1/4)^(3/2) = 1/8. S matches 1.
Answer: P\u21923; Q\u21922; R\u21924; S\u21921.`},{y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 4 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pge",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:`Given below are two statements : one is labelled as Assertion (A) and the other is labelled as Reason (R).
Assertion (A) : The kinetic energy needed to project a body of mass m from earth surface to infinity is \xBDmgR, where R is the radius of earth.
Reason (R) : The maximum potential energy of a body is zero when it is projected to infinity from earth surface.
In the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below.`,o:["A is false but R is true","Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A.","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A.","A is true but R is false"],c:0,s:`The potential energy at infinity is zero and it is the maximum value (all other values are negative). So R is true.
KE needed = GMm/R = mgR (using GM = gR\xB2), not \xBDmgR.
So A is false.
Answer: A is false but R is true.`},{y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 27 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pgs",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:`Given below are two statements : one is labelled as Assertion (A) and the other is labelled as Reason (R).
Assertion (A) : The angular speed of the moon in its orbit about the earth is more than the angular speed of the earth in its orbit about the sun.
Reason (R) : The moon takes less time to move around the earth than the time taken by the earth to move around the sun.
In the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below.`,o:["A is correct but R is not correct.","Both A and R are correct but R is not the correct explanation of A.","A is not correct but R is correct.","Both A and R are correct and R is the correct explanation of A."],c:3,s:`Moon's period around the earth is about 27 days; earth's period around the sun is 365 days. So R is true.
Angular speed \u03C9 = 2\u03C0/T, so a smaller period means a larger angular speed.
Hence \u03C9 of the moon > \u03C9 of the earth, so A is true, and R is exactly the reason for it.
Answer: both A and R are correct and R is the correct explanation of A.`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 6 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pge",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:`Given below are two statements : One is labelled as Assertion A and the other is labelled as Reason R.
Assertion A : Earth has atmosphere whereas moon doesn't have any atmosphere.
Reason R : The escape velocity on moon is very small as compared to that on earth.
In the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the option given below.`,o:["Both A and R are correct but R is NOT the correct explanation of A.","Both A and R are correct and R is the correct explanation of A.","A is true but R is false.","A is false but R is true."],c:1,s:`Escape speed on the moon is about 2.4 km/s, far less than 11.2 km/s on earth. So R is true.
Gas molecules on the moon can easily reach this small speed and escape, so the moon cannot hold an atmosphere. The earth can.
A is true, and R explains A.
Answer: both correct and R is the correct explanation.`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 8 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pgs",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:`Given below are two statements :
Statement I : If E be the total energy of a satellite moving around the earth, then its potential energy will be {E;2}.
Statement II : The kinetic energy of a satellite revolving in an orbit is equal to the half the magnitude of total energy E.
In the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below.`,o:["Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect.","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect.","Both Statement I and Statement II are correct.","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct."],c:0,s:`For a circular orbit, KE = GMm/2r, PE = \u2212GMm/r and E = \u2212GMm/2r.
So PE = 2E, not E/2. Statement I is incorrect.
KE = \u2212E = |E|, the full magnitude of E, not half of it. Statement II is incorrect.
Answer: both statements are incorrect.`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 13 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pge",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:`Given below are two statements :
Statement I : For a planet, if the ratio of mass of the planet to its radius increases, the escape velocity from the planet also increases.
Statement II : Escape velocity is independent of the radius of the planet.
In the light of above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below.`,o:["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct.","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect.","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct.","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect."],c:1,s:`Escape velocity v = \u221A(2GM/R) = \u221A(2G \xD7 (M/R)).
So v increases when M/R increases. Statement I is correct.
v depends on R (for a fixed M, v \u221D 1/\u221AR). Statement II is incorrect.
Answer: Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect.`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 25 Feb \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pge",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:`Assertion A : The escape velocities of planet A and B are same. But A and B are of unequal mass.
Reason R : The product of their mass and radius must be same. M\u2081R\u2081 = M\u2082R\u2082.
Choose the correct option.`,o:["A is correct but R is not correct.","Both A and R are correct but R is not the correct explanation of A.","A is not correct but R is correct.","Both A and R are correct and R is the correct explanation of A."],c:0,s:`Escape velocity v = \u221A(2GM/R). Equal escape velocities need M/R to be equal.
Planets with unequal masses can have equal v if their radii differ in the same ratio, so A is correct.
The condition is M\u2081/R\u2081 = M\u2082/R\u2082, not M\u2081R\u2081 = M\u2082R\u2082. So R is incorrect.
Answer: A is correct but R is not correct.`},{y:"IIT-JEE 2008",tp:"pgs",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:`Statement-1 : An astronaut in an orbiting space station above the Earth experiences weightlessness.
Statement-2 : An object moving around the Earth under the influence of Earth's gravitational force is in a state of free-fall.`,o:["Statements-1 and 2 are true and statement-2 is a correct explanation for statement-1","Statements-1 and 2 are true and statement-2 is not a correct explanation for statement-1","Statement-1 is true, statement-2 is false","Statement-1 is false, statement-2 is true."],c:0,s:`Orbiting under gravity alone means the station and astronaut are both in free-fall, with the same acceleration. So Statement-2 is true.
Because both fall together, the floor does not push on the astronaut, so the apparent weight is zero. Statement-1 is true.
Free-fall is the reason for weightlessness, so Statement-2 explains Statement-1.
Answer: (a).`},{y:"IIT-JEE 2003",tp:"pge",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:"A body is projected vertically upwards from the bottom of a crater of moon of depth {R;100} where R is the radius of moon with a velocity equal to the escape velocity on the surface of moon. Calculate maximum height attained by the body from the surface of the moon.",a:"h \u2248 99.5R",s:`Take the moon as a uniform sphere. Inside, at distance r from the centre, V = \u2212GM(3R\xB2 \u2212 r\xB2)/2R\xB3.
At the bottom r = 0.99R: V = \u2212(GM/R)(3 \u2212 0.9801)/2 = \u22121.00995 GM/R. At the start KE = \xBDv_e\xB2 = GM/R (per unit mass).
Total energy E = GM/R \u2212 1.00995 GM/R = \u22120.00995 GM/R.
At the highest point (outside the moon) E = \u2212GM/r_max, so r_max = R/0.00995 \u2248 100.5R from the centre.
Height above the surface = 100.5R \u2212 R \u2248 99.5R.`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1990",tp:"pgs",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:`An artificial satellite is moving in a circular orbit around the earth with a speed equal to half the magnitude of escape velocity from the earth.
(i) Determine the height of the satellite above the earth's surface.
(ii) If the satellite is stopped suddenly in its orbit and allowed to fall freely onto the earth, find the speed with which it hits the surface of the earth.`,a:"(i) h = 6400 km   (ii) v = 7920 m/s",s:`(i) Orbital speed v = \u221A(GM/r) and escape speed v_e = \u221A(2GM/R). Given v = v_e/2, so GM/r = (1/4)(2GM/R) = GM/(2R). Hence r = 2R and h = r \u2212 R = R = 6400 km.
(ii) Falling from rest at r = 2R to r = R: \xBDv\xB2 = GM(1/R \u2212 1/2R) = GM/(2R) = gR/2.
v = \u221A(gR) = \u221A(9.8 \xD7 6.4 \xD7 10\u2076) \u2248 7.92 \xD7 10\xB3 m/s = 7920 m/s.`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1986",tp:"pgs",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:`Two satellites S\u2081 and S\u2082 revolve round a planet in coplanar circular orbits in the same sense. Their periods of revolution are 1 hour and 8 hour respectively. The radius of the orbit of S\u2081 is 10\u2074 km. When S\u2082 is closest to S\u2081, find
(a) the speed of S\u2082 relative to S\u2081,
(b) the angular speed of S\u2082 as actually observed by an astronaut in S\u2081.`,a:"(a) \u2212\u03C0 \xD7 10\u2074 km/h   (b) \u03C9 = 3 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 rad/s",s:`Kepler: r \u221D T^(2/3), so r\u2082 = 10\u2074 \xD7 8^(2/3) = 4 \xD7 10\u2074 km.
v\u2081 = 2\u03C0r\u2081/T\u2081 = 2\u03C0 \xD7 10\u2074 km/h. v\u2082 = 2\u03C0 \xD7 4 \xD7 10\u2074/8 = \u03C0 \xD7 10\u2074 km/h.
(a) At closest approach both move in the same direction, so the relative velocity is v\u2082 \u2212 v\u2081 = \u2212\u03C0 \xD7 10\u2074 km/h (S\u2082 appears to move backwards, speed \u03C0 \xD7 10\u2074 km/h).
(b) Distance between them = r\u2082 \u2212 r\u2081 = 3 \xD7 10\u2074 km. Angular speed = relative speed/distance = \u03C0 \xD7 10\u2074/(3 \xD7 10\u2074) = \u03C0/3 rad/h.
\u03C9 = (\u03C0/3)/3600 \u2248 2.9 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 \u2248 3 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 rad/s.`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1984",tp:"pgs",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:"It is possible to put an artificial satellite into orbit in such a way that it will always remain directly over New Delhi.",o:["True","False"],c:1,s:`The orbit of a satellite must be a circle or ellipse whose plane passes through the centre of the earth.
A satellite that stays over one point must be geostationary, and that orbit lies in the equatorial plane.
New Delhi is far from the equator (about 28.6\xB0 N), so no orbit can keep a satellite always above it.
Answer: False.`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1988",tp:"pge",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:"The masses and radii of the earth and the moon are M\u2081, R\u2081 and M\u2082, R\u2082 respectively. Their centres are a distance d apart. The minimum speed with which a particle of mass m should be projected from a point midway between the two centres so as to escape to infinity is ______.",a:"v = \u221A{4G(M\u2081 + M\u2082);d}",s:`The midpoint is at distance d/2 from each centre.
Potential energy there = \u2212GM\u2081m/(d/2) \u2212 GM\u2082m/(d/2) = \u22122Gm(M\u2081 + M\u2082)/d.
For escape, \xBDmv\xB2 = 2Gm(M\u2081 + M\u2082)/d.
v\xB2 = 4G(M\u2081 + M\u2082)/d.
v = \u221A(4G(M\u2081 + M\u2082)/d).`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1987",tp:"pgs",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:"A geo-stationary satellite is orbiting the earth at a height of 6 R above the surface of the earth, where R is the radius of the earth. The time period of another satellite at a height of 2.5 R from the surface of the earth is ______ hour.",a:"T\u2082 = 8.48 hour",s:`Geostationary satellite: orbit radius r\u2081 = R + 6R = 7R, period T\u2081 = 24 hour.
Other satellite: r\u2082 = R + 2.5R = 3.5R.
Kepler's third law: T \u221D r^(3/2).
T\u2082 = 24 \xD7 (3.5R/7R)^(3/2) = 24 \xD7 (1/2)^(3/2) = 24/2.828.
T\u2082 \u2248 8.48 hour.`}]})();
